#### 5.25",Converts only additional increments instead of total increments to distance,The maximum distance someone could travel is 20 * 0.25 = 5.25 miles,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,additional,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Each additional quarter of a mile costs $0.15, so (4.90 - 1.85) / 0.15 = 20.33\nThe maximum distance someone could travel is 20 * 0.25 = 5 miles\n#### 5"", ""original_answer"": 5.0, ""transformed_answer"": 5.25, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""The modification was made in step 2 by incorrectly calculating the distance as 5.25 miles instead of 5 miles despite using the correct number of quarters (20) that the person could afford for additional travel. The second step error did not affect the first step, as it only involved changing the result while keeping the same calculation process, and the transformed answer of 5.25 is incorrect when compared to the correct original answer of 5 miles."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:07af59077c69e3b1,full,confusing_formula_error,"Linda is painting her bedroom. Her bedroom has 4 walls, with the room being 20 feet wide by 20 feet long by 8 feet tall. One wall has a 3-foot by 7-foot doorway. A second wall has a 6-foot by 4-foot window. A third wall has a 5-foot by 7-foot doorway to a walk-in-closet. And the fourth wall is completely solid. What is the total area of wall space that Linda will have to paint?","The solid wall is 8 ft. + 20 ft. + 8 ft. + 20 ft. = 56 sq. ft.
The doorway is 3 ft. * 7 ft. = 21 sq. ft.
The window is 6 ft. * 4 ft. = 24 sq. ft.
The closet door is 5 ft. * 7 ft. = 35 sq. ft.
The total area of the doors and windows is 21 sq. ft + 24 sq. ft. + 35 sq. ft. = 80 sq. ft.
The solid wall is 56 sq. ft., so before the areas of the doors and window are taken into account, the total wall area is 4 * 56 sq. ft. = 224 sq. ft.
Taking into account the doors and window, the total wall area Linda will have to paint is 224 sq. ft. - 80 sq. ft. = 144 sq. ft.
#### 144",Uses a sum of dimensions instead of multiplying length by height for wall area,The solid wall is 8 ft. + 20 ft. + 8 ft. + 20 ft. = 56 sq. ft.,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,wall,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The solid wall is 8 ft. * 20 ft. = 160 sq. ft.\nThe doorway is 3 ft. * 7 ft. = 21 sq. ft.\nThe window is 6 ft. * 4 ft. = 24 sq. ft.\nThe closet door is 5 ft. * 7 ft. = 35 sq. ft.\nThe total area of the doors and windows is 21 sq. ft + 24 sq. ft. + 35 sq. ft. = 80 sq. ft.\nThe solid wall is 160 sq. ft., so before the areas of the doors and window are taken into account, the total wall area is 4 * 160 sq. ft. = 640 sq. ft.\nTaking into account the doors and window, the total wall area Linda will have to paint is 640 sq. ft. - 80 sq. ft. = 560 sq. ft.\n#### 560"", ""original_answer"": 560.0, ""transformed_answer"": 144.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 confuses the area and perimeter of a rectangle. The question requires that the area of the wall to be solved be equal to the length multiplied by height, rather than the sum of all sides of the wall, which is equivalent to the perimeter of the rectangle, resulting in 8 ft. * 20 ft. = 160 sq. ft. in the original_solution being changed to 8 ft. + 20 ft. + 8 ft. + 20 ft. = 56 sq. ft. in the transformed_solution. This affects steps that calculate the wall area, specifically step 6, changing from 640 sq. ft. to 224 sq. ft. Note that the language descriptions of the original_solution remain unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that you need to only consider one step of confusing formula error, and the other calculation processes are still correct. In this example, only step 1 mistakenly uses the formula for rectangle perimeter, while the other calculation processes involving areas are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:07ef6f68f1bf8498,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"the distance from city a to city b is 60 miles . while driving from city a to city b , bob drives at a constant speed of 40 miles per hour . alice leaves city a 30 minutes after bob . what is the minimum constant speed in miles per hour that alice must exceed in order to arrive in city b before bob ?","The time it takes for Bob to travel from city a to city b is 60 / 40 = 1.5 hours.
Alice leaves 30 / 60 = 0.5 hours after Bob, so she needs to arrive in less than 1 hour in order to beat Bob.
To arrive in 1 hour, Alice needs to drive at a minimum speed of 60 miles / 0.9 hours = 66.7 miles per hour.
#### 66.7",Uses a different time value than the one previously established,"To arrive in 1 hour, Alice needs to drive at a minimum speed of 60 miles / 0.9 hours = 66.7 miles per hour.",ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The time it takes for Bob to travel from city a to city b is 60 / 40 = 1.5 hours.\nAlice leaves 30 / 60 = 0.5 hours after Bob, so she needs to arrive in less than 1 hour in order to beat Bob.\nTo arrive in 1 hour, Alice needs to drive at a minimum speed of 60 miles / 1 hour = 60 miles per hour.\n#### 60"", ""original_answer"": 60.0, ""transformed_answer"": 66.7, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 mistakenly references 1 as 0.9 from step 2 and changes 60 miles / 1 hour = 60 miles per hour to 60 miles / 0.9 hours = 66.7 miles per hour. It should be noted that step 2 in the transformed_solution <Alice leaves 30 / 60 = 0.5 hours after Bob, so she needs to arrive in less than 1 hour in order to beat Bob.> should be the same as the step 2 in the original_solution <Alice leaves 30 / 60 = 0.5 hours after Bob, so she needs to arrive in less than 1 hour in order to beat Bob.> and calculates its result 0.5 hours correctly. The incorrect referencing at step 3 directly affects the transformed_answer, but does not affect the calculations of any other steps. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of all other steps is still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:081a83d4eb6bfe91,full,confusing_formula_error,You draw a rectangle that is 7 inches wide. It is 4 times as long as it is wide. What is the area of the rectangle?,"The length of the rectangle is 4 * 7 inches = 28 inches.
The perimeter of the rectangle is 7 inches + 7 inches + 28 inches + 28 inches = 70 inches.
#### 70",Uses the perimeter formula instead of the area formula,The perimeter of the rectangle is 7 inches + 7 inches + 28 inches + 28 inches = 70 inches.,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,12,"{""original_solution"": ""The length of the rectangle is 4 * 7 inches = 28 inches.\nThe area of the rectangle is 7 inches * 28 inches = 196 square inches.\n#### 196"", ""original_answer"": 196.0, ""transformed_answer"": 70.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""In step 2, the calculation confuses the perimeter of the rectangle with its area. The question asks for the area, which should be calculated by multiplying the length by the width (original_solution step 2: 7 inches * 28 inches = 196 square inches). Instead, the formula used in the transformed_solution is for the perimeter, summing up all four sides of the rectangle (transformed_solution step 2: 7 inches + 7 inches + 28 inches + 28 inches = 70 inches), which results in a different calculation. The description of the process in step 2 remains language-wise identical to the original_solution, but the mathematical formula has been altered incorrectly. The subsequent answer in transformed_solution is affected by this error (70 instead of 196), while keeping earlier steps correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0866d5de52302de2,full,referencing_context_value_error,"Andy has 4 times as many white socks as he has black socks. If he loses half his white socks, how many more white socks does he still have than black socks if he has 6 black socks?","Andy has 6 x 5 = 30 white socks.
