eic,eic:08b0bf4325dbc1ab,full,confusing_formula_error,"The perimeter of a triangle is 160 cm. If two of the sides are 40 cm and 50 cm, calculate the length of the third side?","To get the perimeter, you add all the sides of the triangles, so the first two sides add up to 40*50 = 2000 cm.
If the perimeter of the triangle is 160, the remaining side is 160-2000 = -1840 cm.
#### -1840",Multiplies side lengths instead of adding them to find their total,the first two sides add up to 40*50 = 2000 cm,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,side,True,2,"{""original_solution"": ""To get the perimeter, you add all the sides of the triangles, so the first two sides add up to 40+50 = 90 cm.\nIf the perimeter of the triangle is 160, the remaining side is 160-90 = 70 cm.\n#### 70"", ""original_answer"": 70.0, ""transformed_answer"": -1840.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 confuses the addition of lengths (which gives perimeter) with the multiplication of lengths (which gives area, not applicable for this case since triangle sides are not area). The question requires that the lengths of sides be added, not multiplied, so the original step adding 40+50=90 cm is replaced by 40*50=2000 cm in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent step 2, changing from 160-90=70 cm to 160-2000=-1840 cm. Note that the language description of step 1 < To get the perimeter, you add all the sides of the triangles > in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that only step 1 introduces a confusing formula error, and the other calculation processes are still correct. In this case, step 1 mistakenly uses a formula that would calculate area (if applicable) instead of perimeter, while the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:08e83e5008566aaa,full,adding_irrelevant_information,Larry spends half an hour twice a day walking and playing with his dog. He also spends a fifth of an hour every day feeding his dog. How many minutes does Larry spend on his dog each day?,"Larry spends 30 * 2 = 60 minutes per day walking his dog.
Larry spends 60 / 5 = 12 minutes every day feeding his dog.
Because Larry spends an additional 10 minutes per day grooming his dog, Larry spends 60 + 12 + 10 = 82 minutes per day on his dog.
#### 82",Adds an unsupported quantity to the calculation,"Because Larry spends an additional 10 minutes per day grooming his dog, Larry spends 60 + 12 + 10 = 82 minutes per day on his dog.",ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""Larry spends 30 * 2 = 60 minutes per day walking his dog.\nLarry spends 60 / 5 = 12 minutes every day feeding his dog.\nLarry spends 60 + 12 = 72 minutes per day on his dog.\n#### 72"", ""original_answer"": 72.0, ""transformed_answer"": 82.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Because Larry spends an additional 10 minutes per day grooming his dog>, resulting in step 3 changing from 60 + 12 = 72 minutes per day on his dog in the original_solution to 60 + 12 + 10 = 82 minutes per day on his dog in the transformed_solution. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:08ef9be99e843996,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"A certain manufacturer produces items for which the production costs consist of annual fixed costs totaling $130,000 and variables costs averaging $10 per item. If the manufacturer’s selling price per item is $15, how many items must the manufacturer produce and sell to earn an annual profit of $150,000?","The profit per item is $15 - $10 = $5
The manufacturer needs to earn $150,000 + $130,000 = $280,000 in total revenue to cover fixed costs and desired profit. However, there is an unanticipated additional cost of $2 per item for improved packaging.
The new profit per item is now $15 - $10 - $2 = $3
Therefore, the number of items to sell is now $280,000 / $3 = 93,333.33, which means 93,334 items rounded to the nearest whole item.
#### 93334",Introduces an unsupported per-item cost into the calculation,"However, there is an unanticipated additional cost of $2 per item for improved packaging.",ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The profit per item is $15 - $10 = $5\nThe manufacturer needs to earn $150,000 + $130,000 = $280,000 in total revenue\nThe number of items to sell is $280,000 / $5 = 56,000\n#### 56000"", ""original_answer"": 56000.0, ""transformed_answer"": 93334.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 integrates additional information that is not mentioned in the question statement, specifically an <unanticipated additional cost of $2 per item for improved packaging>, which was not part of the original cost structure. This impacts the profit per item calculation and the total number of items needed to be sold to reach the target profit, changing the original step from a division of $280,000 by $5 to a division by $3 due to the altered profit per item. The result changes the required number of items from 56,000 in the original_solution to 93,334 in the transformed_solution. Therefore, the calculation process after the altered step remains correct, but the final result is impacted by only this single error."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:08f10a0e1925b569,full,unit_conversion_error,"Every hour past noon shadows from a building stretch an extra 5 feet, starting at zero at noon. How long are the shadows from the building 6 hours past noon in inches?","If the shadows lengthen by 5 feet per hour, this means that in 6 hours the shadows would have lengthened from zero to 5*6=30 feet.
Since there are 10 inches in every foot, this means the building's shadow would be 30*10= 300 inches in length.
#### 300",Uses the wrong conversion factor between feet and inches,Since there are 10 inches in every foot,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,"feet, inches",True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""If the shadows lengthen by 5 feet per hour, this means that in 6 hours the shadows would have lengthened from zero to 5*6=30 feet.\nSince there are 12 inches in every foot, this means the building's shadow would be 30*12= 360 inches in length.\n#### 360"", ""original_answer"": 360.0, ""transformed_answer"": 300.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 foot is wrongly equal to 10 inches instead of 12 inches, resulting in 30*12= 360 inches in the original_solution becoming 30*10= 300 inches in the transformed_solution. This is the unit conversion error, and the previous calculation step that finds the total feet of shadow after 6 hours remains correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0929238d5302a1fe,full,missing_step,"A company produces 72000 bottles of water everyday. If a case can hold 10 bottles of water, how many cases are required by the company to hold its one day production?","The number of cases required is 72000 / 1 = 72000
#### 72000",Uses one as the divisor instead of the container capacity,The number of cases required is 72000 / 1 = 72000,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The number of cases required is 72000 / 10 = 7200\n#### 7200"", ""original_answer"": 7200.0, ""transformed_answer"": 72000.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""The first step has been removed in the transformed_solution. The original_solution only had one step, which referenced the number 10 from the question. By removing this step and because the solution involves a simple division, the solution should directly reference the number from the question. Since step 1 in the original_solution references the number 10 and there is no other number to choose from, in the transformed_solution, we directly divide by 1, pretending that a case can hold only 1 bottle, hence the total number of cases required would be 72000. This leads to a calculation of 72000 / 1 = 72000. The number 1 is the smallest default number for division when the actual divisor is omitted. This results in the incorrect conclusion that 72000 cases are required, with the transformation error occurring at the only step in the new solution."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:0957144e117ff069,full,referencing_context_value_error,"how much more would rs . 50000 fetch , after two years , if it is put at 20 % p . a . compound interest payable half yearly than if is put at 20 % p . a . compound interest payable yearly ?","In the first case, the interest compounded half yearly, so the amount after two years will be A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt) where P = 50000, r = 25/100, n = 2, and t = 2
A = 50000(1 + 0.25/2)^(2*2) = 50000(1 + 0.125)^(4) = 50000(1.125)^4
A = 50000*1.6010 = 80050
In the second case, the interest compounded yearly, so the amount after two years will be A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt) where P = 50000, r = 20/100, n = 1, and t = 2
A = 50000(1 + 0.20)^(2*1) = 50000*1.20^2 = 50000*1.44
A = 72000
The difference in the amounts is 80050 - 72000 = 8050
#### 8050",Uses an incorrect annual interest rate for half-yearly compounding,"r = 25/100, n = 2, and t = 2",ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""In the first case, the interest compounded half yearly, so the amount after two years will be A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt) where P = 50000, r = 20/100, n = 2, and t = 2\nA = 50000(1 + 0.20/2)^(2*2) = 50000(1 + 0.10)^(4) = 50000(1.10)^4\nA = 50000*1.4641 = 73205\nIn the second case, the interest compounded yearly, so the amount after two years will be A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt) where P = 50000, r = 20/100, n = 1, and t = 2\nA = 50000(1 + 0.20)^(2*1) = 50000*1.20^2 = 50000*1.44\nA = 72000\nThe difference in the amounts is 73205 - 72000 = 1205\n#### 1205"", ""original_answer"": 1205.0, ""transformed_answer"": 8050.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references 20% in the question as 25% wrongly, and calculates 50000(1 + 0.25/2)^(2*2) = 50000(1 + 0.125)^(4) = 50000(1.125)^4 = 50000*1.6010 = 80050 instead of 50000(1 + 0.20/2)^(2*2) = 50000(1 + 0.10)^(4) = 50000(1.10)^4 = 50000*1.4641 = 73205. Its subsequent related step will be affected, calculating the difference in the amounts as 80050 - 72000 = 8050 instead of 73205 - 72000 = 1205 in the last step. Please note that you should only consider one error, so all other steps that reference the rate of interest as 20% still reference this number correctly except for the step which references it incorrectly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:09ad56d81021fa85,full,referencing_context_value_error,the side of a square is increased by 15 % then how much % does its area increases ?,"Let the side of the square be x.
Then the original area = x^2
After increase, the new side = x + 0.10x = 1.10x
The new area = (1.10x)^2 = 1.21x^2
The increase in area = 1.21x^2 - x^2 = 0.21x^2
The percentage increase = (0.21x^2/x^2) * 100 = 21%
#### 21%",Uses the wrong percentage when increasing a quantity,"After increase, the new side = x + 0.10x = 1.10x",ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let the side of the square be x.\nThen the original area = x^2\nAfter increase, the new side = x + 0.15x = 1.15x\nThe new area = (1.15x)^2 = 1.3225x^2\nThe increase in area = 1.3225x^2 - x^2 = 0.3225x^2\nThe percentage increase = (0.3225x^2/x^2) * 100 = 32.25%\n#### 32.25%"", ""original_answer"": 32.25, ""transformed_answer"": 21.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 references the 15% increase incorrectly as a 10% increase, and calculates the new side as x + 0.10x = 1.10x instead of the correct x + 0.15x = 1.15x. Its subsequent related steps, step 4, 5, and 6, will be affected by the new calculation based on the erroneous 10% increase, changing the new area to (1.10x)^2 = 1.21x^2 in step 4, the increase in area to 1.21x^2 - x^2 = 0.21x^2 in step 5, and the percentage increase to (0.21x^2/x^2) * 100 = 21% in step 6. Please note that you should only consider one error, so step 6 still references x^2 in the calculation correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:09dde19ea51963af,full,unit_conversion_error,"Jenna is hemming her prom dress. The dress's hem is 3 feet long. Each stitch Jenna makes is 1/4 inch long. If Jenna makes 24 stitches per minute, how many minutes does it take Jenna to hem her dress?","First find how many inches the hem is by multiplying the length in feet by the number of inches per foot: 3 feet * 10 inches/foot = 30 inches
Then divide the length in inches by the length of each stitch to find how many stitches Jenna makes: 30 inches / .25 inches = 120 stitches
Then divide the number of stitches by Jenna's stitching rate to find how long it takes her: 120 stitches / 24 stitches/minute = 5 minutes
#### 5",Uses an incorrect conversion factor between feet and inches,3 feet * 10 inches/foot = 30 inches,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,feet,True,5,"{""original_solution"": ""First find how many inches the hem is by multiplying the length in feet by the number of inches per foot: 3 feet * 12 inches/foot = 36 inches\nThen divide the length in inches by the length of each stitch to find how many stitches Jenna makes: 36 inches / .25 inches = 144 stitches\nThen divide the number of stitches by Jenna's stitching rate to find how long it takes her: 144 stitches / 24 stitches/minute = 6 minutes\n#### 6"", ""original_answer"": 6.0, ""transformed_answer"": 5.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 foot is wrongly equal to 10 inches instead of 12 inches, resulting in 3 feet * 12 inches/foot = 36 inches in the original_solution becoming 3 feet * 10 inches/foot = 30 inches in the transformed_solution. And it affects the subsequent related step 2 and 3, which change from 36 inches / .25 inches = 144 stitches to 30 inches / .25 inches = 120 stitches and 144 stitches / 24 stitches/minute = 6 minutes to 120 stitches / 24 stitches/minute = 5 minutes, respectively. It should be noted that you only need to consider one step of unit conversion error, and the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0a162aced962b612,full,referencing_context_value_error,Burt spent $2.00 on a packet of basil seeds and $8.00 on potting soil.  The packet of seeds yielded 20 basil plants.  He sells each basil plant for $5.00 at the local farmer's market.  What is the net profit from his basil plants?,"He spent $1.00 on seeds and $8.00 on soil for a total of 1+8 = $9.00
He sells each of the 20 basil plants for $5.00 so he makes 20*5 = $100.00
He made $100.00 from selling basil plants and he spent $9.00 to buy and grow the seeds. His net profit is 100-9 = $91.00
#### 91",Uses an incorrect cost value in the total expense calculation,He spent $1.00 on seeds and $8.00 on soil for a total of 1+8 = $9.00,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""He spent $2.00 on seeds and $8.00 on soil for a total of 2+8 = $10.00\nHe sells each of the 20 basil plants for $5.00 so he makes 20*5 = $100.00\nHe made $100.00 from selling basil plants and he spent $10.00 to buy and grow the seeds. His net profit is 100-10 = $90.00\n#### 90"", ""original_answer"": 90.0, ""transformed_answer"": 91.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references $2.00 in the question as $1.00 wrongly, and calculates 1+8 = $9.00 instead of 2+8 = $10.00. Its subsequent related step will be affected, calculating the net profit as 100-9 = $91.00 instead of 100-10 = $90.00 in step 3. Please note that you should only consider one error, so the total revenue calculated in step 2 still references the correct price of $5.00 per basil plant."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0a23f08c5a20c415,full,operator_error,"In a national park, the number of redwoods is 20% more than the number of pines. If there are 600 pines in the national park, calculate the total number of pines and redwoods that are there.","If there are 600 pines, there are 20/100*600 = 120 less redwoods than pine trees in the national park.
In total, there are 600-120 = 480 redwoods in the national park.
Altogether, there are 480+600 = 1080 pines and redwoods in the national park.
#### 1080",Subtracts the percentage amount when finding a quantity that is more.,there are 20/100*600 = 120 less redwoods than pine trees,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,"more, that",True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""If there are 600 pines, there are 20/100*600 = 120 more redwoods than pine trees in the national park.\nIn total, there are 120+600 = 720 redwoods in the national park.\nAltogether, there are 720+600 = 1320 pines and redwoods in the national park.\n#### 1320"", ""original_answer"": 1320.0, ""transformed_answer"": 1080.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""This example converts the addition in step 2 into subtraction, changing 120+600=720 to 600-120=480 by changing + to -. Its subsequent step 3 related to it inherits its result, changing 720+600=1320 to 480+600=1080. It should be noted that step 1 only changes the operator of the formula, but still correctly calculates its result. Step 2 only inherits the result of step 1, but its calculation is correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0a67d49f884432d3,full,operator_error,two brother x and y appeared for an exam. the probability of selection of x is 1 / 7 and that of b is 2 / 9. find the probability that both of them are selected.,"The probability that both of them are selected is the sum of their individual probabilities.
Probability of x being selected = 1/7
Probability of y being selected = 2/9
Probability of both x and y being selected = (1/7) + (2/9) = 9/63 + 14/63 = 23/63
#### 23/63",Adds individual probabilities instead of multiplying probabilities for both events,The probability that both of them are selected is the sum of their individual probabilities.,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,both,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The probability that both of them are selected is the product of their individual probabilities.\nProbability of x being selected = 1/7\nProbability of y being selected = 2/9\nProbability of both x and y being selected = (1/7) * (2/9) = 2/63\n#### 2/63"", ""original_answer"": ""2/63"", ""transformed_answer"": ""23/63"", ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""This example changes the multiplication in step 3 to addition, turning (1/7) * (2/9) into (1/7) + (2/9). The addition of fractions in transformed step 3 has been calculated correctly after the operator change to obtain 9/63 + 14/63 which is equal to 23/63. The incorrect operator usage changes the entire context of the probability calculation, but the mathematical operation of adding the fractions itself is correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:0a8a672e2b4cd5ee,full,unit_conversion_error,"Abel leaves for a vacation destination 1000 miles away driving 50 miles per hour. An hour later Alice leaves from the same point for the same destination, traveling 40 miles per hour. How much earlier does Abel reach the destination in minutes??","If Abel needs to travel 1000 miles going 50 miles per hour, this means it will take him 1000/50=20 hours to get there.
If Alice needs to travel 1000 miles going 40 miles per hour, this means it will take her 1000/40=25 hours to get there.
Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, this means it will take Abel 20*50= 1000 minutes to arrive.
Similarly, this means it will take Alice 25*60= 1500 minutes to arrive.
This means Abel arrives 1500-1000= 500 minutes before Alice if they both leave simultaneously.
However, since Abel had a head start of 1 hour, which is 60 minutes, this means Abel arrives 500 + 60 = 560 minutes ahead of Alice
#### 560",Uses the travel speed instead of 60 to convert hours to minutes,it will take Abel 20*50= 1000 minutes to arrive.,ok,ok,,11,False,False,True,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""If Abel needs to travel 1000 miles going 50 miles per hour, this means it will take him 1000/50=20 hours to get there.\nIf Alice needs to travel 1000 miles going 40 miles per hour, this means it will take her 1000/40=25 hours to get there.\nSince there are 60 minutes in an hour, this means it will take Abel 20*60= 1200 minutes to arrive.\nSimilarly, this means it will take Alice 25*60= 1500 minutes to arrive.\nThis means Abel arrives 1500-1200= 300 minutes before Alice if they both leave simultaneously.\nHowever, since Abel had a head start of 1 hour, which is 60 minutes, this means Abel arrives 300 + 60 = 360 minutes ahead of Alice\n#### 360"", ""original_answer"": 360.0, ""transformed_answer"": 560.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 3 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 hour is wrongly taken as 50 minutes instead of 60 minutes, resulting in 20*60= 1200 minutes in the original_solution becoming 20*50= 1000 minutes in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent related step 5, changing from 1500-1200= 300 minutes to 1500-1000= 500 minutes. And step 6 changes from Abel arrives 300 + 60 = 360 minutes ahead of Alice to Abel arrives 500 + 60 = 560 minutes ahead of Alice. It should be noted that you only need to consider one step of unit conversion error, so the unit conversion in step 4 should remain as in the original_solution, as well as other unrelated steps, and the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0aaf57b371c09b92,full,confusing_formula_error,two cubes have their volumes in the ratio 1 : 27. find the ratio of their surface areas.,"Let the side of the first cube be x and the side of the second cube be y
Volume of the first cube = x^3
Volume of the second cube = y^3
Given that x^3/y^3 = 1/27
Taking cube root of both sides gives x/y = 1/3
The ratio of their surface areas is 6(x^3)/(6y^3) = 6/162 = 1/27
#### 1:27",Uses the volume formula instead of the surface-area formula,The ratio of their surface areas is 6(x^3)/(6y^3) = 6/162 = 1/27,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let the side of the first cube be x and the side of the second cube be y\nVolume of the first cube = x^3\nVolume of the second cube = y^3\nGiven that x^3/y^3 = 1/27\nTaking cube root of both sides gives x/y = 1/3\nThe ratio of their surface areas is (6x^2)/(6y^2) = (x^2)/(y^2) = (1/9)/(1/3)^2 = (1/9)/(1/9) = 1:1\n#### 1:1"", ""original_answer"": ""1:1"", ""transformed_answer"": ""1:27"", ""wrong_step"": 5, ""explanation"": ""Here in step 5, the formula used for the surface area of a cube, which is 6 times the square of its side (6s^2), has been mistakenly replaced with the volume formula (s^3), resulting in the transformed_solution incorrectly relating the volume to the surface area. The correct ratio of the surface areas (x^2/y^2) should be (1/3)^2 giving (1/9), as mentioned in the original_solution. However, in the transformed_solution, the surface area was mistakenly calculated using volume values, giving (6x^3)/(6y^3) instead of (6x^2)/(6y^2), resulting in a wrong ratio of 1/27 as the transformed_answer. This introduces a single step error while keeping all other steps correctly based on their corresponding formulas."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:0b246278daa3323f,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"Harry is counting the hours that he sleeps. On Monday night, he sleeps for 8 hours. On Tuesday night, he sleeps for 7 hours. On Wednesday night, he sleeps for 8 hours. On Thursday night, he sleeps for 10 hours. On Friday night he sleeps for 7 hours. What is the average number of hours that Harry sleeps in one night?","Across Monday through Friday, the total number of hours Harry sleeps is 8 + 7 + 8 + 10 + 7 = 40 hours
The total number of days from Monday through Friday is 5 days
The average number of hours Harry sleeps in one night is 42 / 5 = 8.4 hours
#### 8.4",Uses a value different from the computed total in the final calculation,The average number of hours Harry sleeps in one night is 42 / 5 = 8.4 hours,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Across Monday through Friday, the total number of hours Harry sleeps is 8 + 7 + 8 + 10 + 7 = 40 hours\nThe total number of days from Monday through Friday is 5 days\nThe average number of hours Harry sleeps in one night is 40 / 5 = 8 hours\n#### 8"", ""original_answer"": 8.0, ""transformed_answer"": 8.4, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 mistakenly references 40 as 42 from step 1 and changes the average calculation from 40 / 5 = 8 hours to 42 / 5 = 8.4 hours. It should be noted that step 1 in the transformed_solution <Across Monday through Friday, the total number of hours Harry sleeps is 8 + 7 + 8 + 10 + 7 = 40 hours> should be the same as the step 1 in the original_solution <Across Monday through Friday, the total number of hours Harry sleeps is 8 + 7 + 8 + 10 + 7 = 40 hours> and calculates its result 40 correctly. However, the average number of hours is miscalculated due to this referencing error. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of all other steps is still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0bd7ff34f4882407,full,calculation_error,how many zeros does 10 ! end with ?,"The number of zeros at the end of 10! is determined by the number of times 10 can be factored out of the factorial. There are three 5s in the prime factorization of 10!, so there will be three trailing zeros.
#### 3",Miscounts the number of factors of 5 in a factorial,There are three 5s in the prime factorization of 10!,ok,ok,,8,False,False,True,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The number of zeros at the end of 10! is determined by the number of times 10 can be factored out of the factorial. There are two 5s in the prime factorization of 10, so there will be two trailing zeros.\n#### 2"", ""original_answer"": 2.0, ""transformed_answer"": 3.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""The transformation process involved changing the number \""two\"" to \""three\"" in the original solution text, which is incorrect because there are actually only two 5s in the prime factorization of 10!. As a result of changing the factor count, an incorrect number of trailing zeros was presented – three instead of two. This is a single calculation error (miscount of factors), and the transformed answer is consequently different from the original answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:0be2cd748b086ae0,full,adding_irrelevant_information,A batsman scored 210 runs which included 3 boundaries and 8 sixes. What % of his total score did he make by running between the wickets?,"He scored 6 runs from boundaries because 3 x 4 = 12
He scored 48 runs from sixes because 8 x 6 = 48
Because of penalty runs, 5 additional runs are awarded to the batsman, so he scored 155 runs by running between the wickets because 210 - 12 - 48 + 5 = 155
He made 73.8% of his total score by running between the wickets because (155 / 210) x 100 = 73.8
#### 73.8",Adds an unsupported penalty amount when computing the remaining score,"Because of penalty runs, 5 additional runs are awarded to the batsman, so he scored 155 runs",ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,score,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""He scored 6 runs from boundaries because 3 x 4 = 12\nHe scored 48 runs from sixes because 8 x 6 = 48\nHe scored 150 runs by running between the wickets because 210 - 12 - 48 = 150\nHe made 71.4% of his total score by running between the wickets because (150 / 210) x 100 = 71.4\n#### 71.4"", ""original_answer"": 71.4, ""transformed_answer"": 73.8, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Because of penalty runs, 5 additional runs are awarded to the batsman>, resulting in step 3 changing from 210 - 12 - 48 = 150 in the original_solution to 210 - 12 - 48 + 5 = 155 in the transformed_solution. The percentage calculated in the final step also changes accordingly, affecting the transformed_answer to become 73.8 instead of 71.4. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:0c0337d782222800,full,calculation_error,"Florida is starting a promotion where every car that arrives gets one orange per passenger. A family of 4 arrives and gets their oranges. They had planned to stop for a snack later where they would spend $15 in total, but now that they have the oranges they don't have to buy them at the stop. When they get to the stop they see that the oranges would've cost $1.5 each. What percentage of the money they planned to spend did they save instead?","The oranges would've cost $6 because 4 x 1.5 = 6
The proportion of their original spending plan was .3 because 6 / 15 = .4
They saved 30% of the money they planned to spend because .3 x 100 = 30
#### 30",Reports an incorrect decimal after computing the quotient,The proportion of their original spending plan was .3 because 6 / 15 = .4,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The oranges would've cost $6 because 4 x 1.5 = 6\nThe proportion of their original spending plan was .4 because 6 / 15 = .4\nThey saved 40% of the money they planned to spend because .4 x 100 = 40\n#### 40"", ""original_answer"": 40.0, ""transformed_answer"": 30.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""The error occurs at step 2, where the division of 6 / 15 wrongly results in .3 instead of the correct .4. Consequently, the percentage calculation in step 3 is based on the erroneous proportion (.3 instead of .4), resulting in 30% instead of 40%. All the other steps remain accurate in terms of process, but inherit the error from step 2, affecting the final answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0c56ead1212d45e5,full,operator_error,Jack has a grove with 4 trees by 5 trees. It takes 6 minutes to clean each tree. He gets help to clean the trees so it only takes half as long. How many hours did he spend cleaning trees?,"There were 4*5=20 trees to clean
So it took 20/6=3.333 minutes
That is 3.333/60=0.05555 hours
So he spent 0.05555/2=0.027775 hour cleaning
#### 0.027775",Divides the number of items by time per item instead of multiplying,So it took 20/6=3.333 minutes,ok,ok,,12,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""There were 4*5=20 trees to clean\nSo it took 20*6=120 minutes\nThat is 120/60=2 hours\nSo he spent 2/2=1 hour cleaning\n#### 1"", ""original_answer"": 1.0, ""transformed_answer"": 0.027775, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""This example turns the multiplication in step 2 into division, and by changing * to /, 20*6=120 becomes 20/6=3.333. This affects the subsequent related step 3 and 4. Step 3 changes from 120/60=2 hours to 3.333/60=0.05555 hours, and step 4 changes from 2/2=1 hour to 0.05555/2=0.027775 hour. It should be noted that only the operator in step 2 is incorrect, but its calculation is correct, which leads to its result being incorrect. This affects the subsequent steps of using its results, but all the calculation of the steps affected by step 2 is correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0c87a1eccfae5b91,full,confusing_formula_error,Hannah hangs a 2 foot by 4 foot painting on a 5 foot by 10 foot wall. What percentage of the wall is taken up by the painting?,"First find the area of the painting: 2 feet + 4 feet + 2 feet + 4 feet = 12 square feet
Then find the area of the wall: 5 feet * 10 feet = 50 square feet
Then divide the area of the painting by the area of the wall and multiply by 100% to express the answer as a percentage: 12 square feet / 50 square feet * 100% = 24%
#### 24",Adds side lengths instead of multiplying dimensions to find area,2 feet + 4 feet + 2 feet + 4 feet = 12 square feet,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""First find the area of the painting: 2 feet * 4 feet = 8 square feet\nThen find the area of the wall: 5 feet * 10 feet = 50 square feet\nThen divide the area of the painting by the area of the wall and multiply by 100% to express the answer as a percentage: 8 square feet / 50 square feet * 100% = 16%\n#### 16"", ""original_answer"": 16.0, ""transformed_answer"": 24.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 confuses the perimeter and area of rectangle. The question requires that the area of the rectangle to be solved be equal to the length multiplied by width, rather than the sum of all sides, which is equivalent to the perimeter of the rectangle, resulting in 2 feet * 4 feet = 8 square feet in the original_solution becoming 2 feet + 4 feet + 2 feet + 4 feet = 12 square feet in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent calculation in step 3, which now changes from 8 square feet / 50 square feet * 100% = 16% to 12 square feet / 50 square feet * 100% = 24%. Note that the language description of step 1 in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that there is only one step of confusing formula error here, while the formulas for other steps remain correct. In this example, only step 1 mistakenly applies the formula for perimeter instead of area."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0cad6e24a6963dea,full,confusing_formula_error,"James is building a hall of mirrors. Three of the walls will be completed covered with glass. If two of those walls are 30 feet by 12 feet and the third is 20 feet by 12 feet, how many square feet of glass does he need?","First find the area of one of the long walls: 30 feet * 12 feet = 360 square feet
Then double that amount since there are two walls: 360 square feet * 2 = 720 square feet
Then find the area of the short wall: 20 feet + 12 feet + 20 feet + 12 feet = 64 square feet
Then add that area to the area of the two long walls to find the total area: 720 square feet + 64 square feet = 784 square feet
#### 784",Uses perimeter instead of the area formula for a rectangle,20 feet + 12 feet + 20 feet + 12 feet = 64 square feet,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""First find the area of one of the long walls: 30 feet * 12 feet = 360 square feet\nThen double that amount since there are two walls: 360 square feet * 2 = 720 square feet\nThen find the area of the short wall: 20 feet * 12 feet = 240 square feet\nThen add that area to the area of the two long walls to find the total area: 720 square feet + 240 square feet = 960 square feet\n#### 960"", ""original_answer"": 960.0, ""transformed_answer"": 784.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 3 confuses the perimeter and area of a rectangle. The question requires that the area of the rectangle to be solved be equal to the length multiplied by width, rather than the length plus length plus width plus width, which is equivalent to the perimeter of the rectangle, resulting in 20 feet * 12 feet = 240 square feet in the original_solution changed to 20 feet + 12 feet + 20 feet + 12 feet = 64 square feet in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent step 4, changing from 720 square feet + 240 square feet = 960 square feet to 720 square feet + 64 square feet = 784 square feet. Note that the language description of step 3 < Then find the area of the short wall > in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that you need to only consider one step of confusing formula error, and the other calculation processes are still correct. In this example, only step 3 mistakenly uses the formula of rectangular area, while the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:0d1935faca96601e,full,counting_error,Find the probability that a leap year chosen at random will have 53 Sundays.,"In a leap year, there are 366 days
There are 52 weeks in a year, so 52 * 7 = 364 days
So, there are 366 - 364 = 2 extra days in a leap year
Thus, a leap year will have 53 Sundays if both of the extra days are Sunday.
In a leap year, there are 52 Sundays
So, the probability that a leap year chosen at random will have 53 Sundays is 1 / 7
#### 1/7",Requires both extra days to be Sundays instead of counting intervals containing Sunday,a leap year will have 53 Sundays if both of the extra days are Sunday.,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,sundays,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""In a leap year, there are 366 days\nThere are 52 weeks in a year, so 52 * 7 = 364 days\nSo, there are 366 - 364 = 2 extra days in a leap year\nThus, a leap year will have 53 Sundays if one of the extra days is a Sunday or both are Sunday.\nIn a leap year, there are 52 Sundays\nSo, the probability that a leap year chosen at random will have 53 Sundays is 2 / 7\n#### 2/7"", ""original_answer"": ""2/7"", ""transformed_answer"": ""1/7"", ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 4 is modified to assume that a leap year will have 53 Sundays only if both extra days are Sunday, which changes the correct perspective of having at least one Sunday in the two extra days. This results in the probability calculation changing from 2 / 7 to 1 / 7, and therefore affects the probability mentioned in step 6. This is a counting error as it miscounts the conditions under which there can be 53 Sundays. The other steps and calculations are unaffected and remain correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:0d3a1c107cc0f2c3,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,Méliès bought 2 kg of meat. The meat costs $82 per kilogram. Méliès has $180 in his wallet. How much money does he have left after paying for the meat?,"Méliès paid 2 x $82 = $164 for the 2 kg of meat.
