9b = 63
b = 9
Putting the value of b into the equation f(1) = 0, 1 + 9 + c = 0
c = -10
Putting b and c into the equation f(x) = x^2 + bx + c, we get f(x) = x^2 + 9x - 10
The y-coordinate when f(x) crosses the y-axis = -10
#### -10",Makes an arithmetic error when dividing to solve for a coefficient,"9b = 63
b = 9",ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""When f(1) = 0, 1 + b + c = 0\nWhen f(-8) = 0, 64 - 8b + c = 0\nSubtracting the two equations, 63 - 9b = 0\n9b = 63\nb = 7\nPutting the value of b into the equation f(1) = 0, 1 + 7 + c = 0\nc = -8\nPutting b and c into the equation f(x) = x^2 + bx + c, we get f(x) = x^2 + 7x - 8\nThe y-coordinate when f(x) crosses the y-axis = -8\n#### -8"", ""original_answer"": -8, ""transformed_answer"": -10, ""wrong_step"": 5, ""explanation"": ""Step 5 incorrectly references b = 9 instead of b = 7. It incorrectly substitutes this value into the equation, affecting the calculation of c = -10 instead of c = -8. The subsequent related steps of this erroneous step will only inherit its erroneous result and be calculated correctly. The transformed equation f(x) = x^2 + 9x - 10 has the y-coordinate crossing at -10 instead of -8."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:118200964eed23f3,full,missing_step,"Lou Senior took 3 cookies out of the cookie jar and ate them. Since he didn't get caught by his wife, he went back the next day and took another 3 cookies out of the jar. But after eating just one of the cookies, he felt guilty about it and put the other two cookies back. His son, Louie Junior saw that his Dad was eating cookies. So, Louie Junior took seven cookies out of the jar and hid them in his bedroom for later. The next morning, Debra, Lou's wife looked into the cookie jar and reacted by accusing her husband of eating half of the cookies out of the cookie jar. How many cookies remained in the jar?","Lou Sr took 3 cookies from the jar, then put two back, for a total of 3-2=1 cookie removed.
Louie Jr took seven more out of the jar, for a total of 1+7=8 cookies removed.
If Debra thought half were gone, then the number of missing cookies would equal the number of cookies that remain, or 8 missing=8 remaining cookies
#### 8",Omits an earlier quantity when calculating the total removed,"Lou Sr took 3 cookies from the jar, then put two back, for a total of 3-2=1 cookie removed.",ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Lou Sr took 3 cookies from the jar, then another three, but then put two back, for a total of 3+3-2=4 cookies removed.\nLouie Jr took seven more out of the jar, for a total of 4+7=11 cookies removed.\nIf Debra thought half were gone, then the number of missing cookies would equal the number of cookies that remain, or 11 missing=11 remaining cookies\n#### 11"", ""original_answer"": 11.0, ""transformed_answer"": 8.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""In the original_solution, step 1 calculates the total number of cookies removed by Lou Sr by combining actions from two days, but step 1 can be seen as two distinct actions. To transform the solution, step 1 is modified by considering only the second action, removing the first day's 3 cookies resulting in 3-2=1 cookie removed. This initial action is directly taken from the question. The second step then changes accordingly, adding only the remaining cookies from day two plus the 7 from Louie Jr, resulting in 1+7=8 cookies removed. The last step remains valid as the logic still applies that the missing number of cookies equals those remaining. Thus, the first incorrect reference becomes the new step 1, replacing \""3+3-2=4\"" with \""3-2=1\"", reflecting only the second day's action. The removal of the first day's action causes the transformed_answer to be different from the original_answer which is necessary for the case generation process."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:11b12fe7658e03bc,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"Jack has $43 in his piggy bank. He also gets an allowance of $10 a week. If Jack puts half of his allowance into his piggy bank every week, after 8 weeks how much will Jack have in his piggy bank?","Jack saves $10 / 2 = $5.00 a week.
Jack also finds an extra $1.00 a week under the sofa.
Over 8 weeks, Jack saves $5.00/week from allowance and finds $1.00/week, giving $5.00 + $1.00 = $6.00/week.
Over 8 weeks Jack saves $6.00/week x 8 weeks = $48.00
In total Jack has $43.00 + $48.00 = $91.00 dollars in his piggy bank
#### 91",Introduces an unsupported extra quantity into the calculation,Jack also finds an extra $1.00 a week under the sofa.,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,into,True,5,"{""original_solution"": ""Jack saves $10 / 2 = $5.00 a week.\nOver 8 weeks Jack saves $5.00/week x 8 weeks = $40.00\nIn total Jack has $43.00 + $40.00 = $83.00 dollars in his piggy bank\n#### 83"", ""original_answer"": 83.0, ""transformed_answer"": 91.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 of the transformed solution adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Jack also finds an extra $1.00 a week under the sofa>. This new information is integrated into the existing step of calculating the total savings per week, changing the original $5.00/week to $6.00/week resulting from $5.00 from allowance plus $1.00 found. It affects the final calculation, changing the total amount in the piggy bank from $83 to $91. This is the only error introduced, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:11e5872b03c348a5,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"If 0.25 of a number is equal to 0.05 of another number, the ratio of the numbers is:","Let x be the first number and y be the second number.
