The combined area is 150000 - 157500 = -7500 square meters.
#### -7500",Subtracts quantities that should be added,The combined area is 150000 - 157500 = -7500 square meters.,ok,ok,,6,False,False,False,,True,2,"{""original_solution"": ""The area of the first tract is 300 x 500 = 150000 square meters.\nThe area of the second tract is 250 x 630 = 157500 square meters.\nThe combined area is 150000 + 157500 = 307500 square meters.\n#### 307500"", ""original_answer"": 307500.0, ""transformed_answer"": -7500.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""This example turns the addition in step 3 into subtraction, and by changing + to -, 150000 + 157500 = 307500 becomes 150000 - 157500 = -7500. It should be noted that the subtraction operation is the only incorrect operation that changes the operator, and its result becomes negative, which is not possible for the area of land. The calculation of the rest of the steps is correct, but the transformed answer is invalid because areas cannot be negative."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:17b85e7aa85ac008,full,unit_conversion_error,"Boston had .5 feet of snow on the first day of winter.  The next day they got an additional 8 inches.  Over the next 2 days, 2 inches of the snow melted.  On the fifth day, they received another 2 times the amount of snow they received on the first day.  How many feet of snow do they now have?","10 inches is 1 foot and they received a .5 foot of snow on the first day so they received 10*.5 = 5 inches of snow
The first day they had 5 inches and then received another 8 inches for a total of 5+8 = 13 inches
2 inches melted from their 13 inches so that leaves 13-2 = 11 inches of snow
They received 2 times the amount as the first day's 5 inches of snow so that's 2*5 = 10 inches of snow
So they had 11 inches of snow and received another 10 inches of snow for a total of 11+10 = 21 inches
10 inches is 1 foot and they have 21 inches of snow so they have 21/10 = 2.1 feet of snow
#### 2.1",Uses an incorrect inches-to-feet conversion factor,10 inches is 1 foot,ok,ok,,6,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""12 inches is 1 foot and they received a .5 foot of snow on the first day so they received 12*.5 = 6 inches of snow\nThe first day they had 6 inches and then received another 8 inches for a total of 6+8 = 14 inches\n2 inches melted from their 14 inches so that leaves 14-2 = 12 inches of snow\nThey received 2 times the amount as the first day's 6 inches of snow so that's 2*6 = 12 inches of snow\nSo they had 12 inches of snow and received another 12 inches of snow for a total of 12+12 = 24 inches\n12 inches is 1 foot and they have 24 inches of snow so they have 24/12 = 2 feet of snow\n#### 2"", ""original_answer"": 2.0, ""transformed_answer"": 2.1, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 makes an incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 foot is wrongly equal to 10 inches instead of 12 inches, resulting in 12*.5 = 6 inches of snow in the original_solution becoming 10*.5 = 5 inches of snow in the transformed_solution. This incorrect conversion affects all the subsequent related steps, changing the amounts of snow measured in inches. The final step that converts inches to feet also uses the incorrect conversion rate, resulting in 24/12 = 2 feet of snow changing to 21/10 = 2.1 feet of snow. It should be noted that you only need to consider one step of unit conversion error, while the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:18083bfa9b38ec07,full,counting_error,"On Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, college student Kimo has three 1-hour classes each day. On Tuesdays and Thursdays, he has two 2-hour classes each day. In one semester, there are 16 weeks of school. In one semester, how many hours does Kimo spend attending classes?","Kimo spends 3 x 1 = 3 hours in class on any given Monday, Wednesday or Friday.
Each week, he spends 3 x 3 = 9 hours in class on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday.
In one semester, he spends 9 x 16 = 144 hours in class for Monday, Wednesday, and Friday.
He spends 2 x 2 = 4 hours in class on any given Tuesday or Thursday.
Each week, he spends 4 x 1 = 4 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.
In one semester, he spends 4 x 16 = 64 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.
In total for one semester, Kimo spends 144 + 64 = 208 hours attending classes.
#### 208",Multiplies a per-occurrence amount by one instead of the number of occurrences,"Each week, he spends 4 x 1 = 4 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.",ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Kimo spends 3 x 1 = 3 hours in class on any given Monday, Wednesday or Friday.\nEach week, he spends 3 x 3 = 9 hours in class on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday.\nIn one semester, he spends 9 x 16 = 144 hours in class for Monday, Wednesday, and Friday.\nHe spends 2 x 2 = 4 hours in class on any given Tuesday or Thursday.\nEach week, he spends 4 x 2 = 8 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.\nIn one semester, he spends 8 x 16 = 128 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.\nIn total for one semester, Kimo spends 144 + 128 = 272 hours attending classes.\n#### 272"", ""original_answer"": 272.0, ""transformed_answer"": 208.0, ""wrong_step"": 5, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 5 erroneously counts Tuesday and Thursday as 1 day each instead of 2 days each, transforming the original 'Each week, he spends 4 x 2 = 8 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.' into 'Each week, he spends 4 x 1 = 4 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.' And it affects the subsequent related step, with the original 'In one semester, he spends 8 x 16 = 128 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.' changing into 'In one semester, he spends 4 x 16 = 64 hours in class on Tuesday and Thursday.' The final sum in step 7 changes accordingly from 144 + 128 = 272 hours to 144 + 64 = 208 hours. The transformed answer should be different from the original answer, and no previous or unrelated steps are affected."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:181351d2407cb199,full,confusing_formula_error,"In a rectangular axis system, what is the area of a parallelogram with the coordinates: (3,5), (9,5), (2,7), (8,7)?","The width of the parallelogram is the difference in the x-coordinates of the points on the same horizontal level, so 9-3=6.
The height of the parallelogram is the difference in the y-coordinates of the points on the same vertical level, so 7-5=2.
The area of the parallelogram is width+height=6+2=8 square units.
#### 8",Adds dimensions instead of multiplying them to calculate area,The area of the parallelogram is width+height=6+2=8 square units.,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""The width of the parallelogram is the difference in the x-coordinates of the points on the same horizontal level, so 9-3=6.\nThe height of the parallelogram is the difference in the y-coordinates of the points on the same vertical level, so 7-5=2.\nThe area of the parallelogram is width*height=6*2=12 square units.\n#### 12"", ""original_answer"": 12.0, ""transformed_answer"": 8.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 3 confuses the formula for finding the area of a parallelogram (width multiplied by height) with the formula for finding the perimeter of a rectangle (width plus height). The question requires that the area of the parallelogram to be solved be equal to the width multiplied by height. Therefore, the step width*height=6*2=12 in the original_solution is replaced with an incorrect formula width+height=6+2=8 in the transformed_solution. This affects the answer to the question, leading to an incorrect transformed_answer of 8 square units instead of the correct original_answer of 12 square units. Note that the language description in steps 1 and 2 in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that only step 3 is modified with an incorrect formulation, while the other steps correctly describe the measurement process for the width and height of the parallelogram."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:1822ba27069405ba,full,operator_error,"A factory produced televisions at a constant rate of 10 per day in a certain year. If they reduced the total production by 10 percent in the second year, calculate the total production of television by the factory in the second year.","Since a year has 365 days, the total television production in the first year is 10*365 = 3650
In the second year, they reduced the total television production by 10*100*3650 = 3650000
The total television production in the second year was 3650-3650000 = -3646350.
#### -3646350",Multiplies by the percentage value instead of converting it to a decimal,10*100*3650 = 3650000,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Since a year has 365 days, the total television production in the first year is 10*365 = 3650\nIn the second year, they reduced the total television production by 10/100*3650 = 365\nThe total television production in the second year was 3650-365 = 3285.\n#### 3285"", ""original_answer"": 3285.0, ""transformed_answer"": -3646350.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""In this example, the division in step 2 is changed to multiplication, turning 10/100*3650=365 into 10*100*3650=3650000 by changing / to *. The consequent step 3 that utilizes this result is affected, changing 3650-365=3285 into 3650-3650000=-3646350. It should be noted that the change takes place only in the operator of step 2, but the calculation thereafter is correct, leading to a different result that impacts the related subsequent steps, all of which, however, are correctly computed based on the altered result of the erroneous step 2."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:18360432ee64044c,full,counting_error,"A baker bakes 5 loaves of bread an hour in one oven. He has 4 ovens. From Monday to Friday, he bakes for 5 hours, but on Saturday and Sunday, he only bakes for 2 hours. How many loaves of bread does the baker bake in 3 weeks?","In an hour, the baker bakes 5 x 4 = 20 loaves of bread.
From Monday to Friday, he bakes 5 x 20 = 100 loaves of bread per day.
From Monday to Friday, he bakes a total of 100 x 5 = 500 loaves of bread.
On Saturday and Sunday, he bakes 2 x 20 = 40 loaves of bread per day.
On Saturday and Sunday, he bakes a total of 40 x 1 = 40 loaves of bread.
In a week, he bakes 500 + 40 = 540 loaves of bread.
In 3 weeks, he bakes 540 x 3 = 1620 loaves of bread.
#### 1620",Counts only one day when aggregating a multi-day period,"On Saturday and Sunday, he bakes a total of 40 x 1 = 40 loaves of bread.",ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,only,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""In an hour, the baker bakes 5 x 4 = 20 loaves of bread.\nFrom Monday to Friday, he bakes 5 x 20 = 100 loaves of bread per day.\nFrom Monday to Friday, he bakes a total of 100 x 5 = 500 loaves of bread.\nOn Saturday and Sunday, he bakes 2 x 20 = 40 loaves of bread per day.\nOn Saturday and Sunday, he bakes a total of 40 x 2 = 80 loaves of bread.\nIn a week, he bakes 500 + 80 = 580 loaves of bread.\nIn 3 weeks, he bakes 580 x 3 = 1740 loaves of bread.\n#### 1740"", ""original_answer"": 1740, ""transformed_answer"": 1620, ""wrong_step"": 5, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 5 erroneously counts Saturday and Sunday as 1 day instead of 2 days, changing the original '40 x 2 = 80 loaves of bread' to '40 x 1 = 40 loaves of bread'. This in turn affects step 6, changing the weekly total from '500 + 80 = 580 loaves of bread' to '500 + 40 = 540 loaves of bread', and step 7, changing the 3-week total from '580 x 3 = 1740 loaves of bread' to '540 x 3 = 1620 loaves of bread'. It should be noted that only one counting error is considered, and the previous steps where the counting was correct remain unchanged."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:18c67e7ca7b60b9f,full,calculation_error,"Out of the 80 students who took the biology exam, only 2/5 of them managed to score 100%. If a score below 80% qualified as failing, and 50 percent of the remaining students who didn't score 100% managed to score over 80%, calculate the number of students who failed the exam.","The number of students who scored 100% is 2/5*80 = 32
Out of the 80 students, 80-32 = 48 did not score 100%.
If 50% of the students who did not score 100% managed to score over 80%, then 50/100*48 = 23 students scored over 80%
The number of students who failed is 48-23 = 25
#### 25",Calculates half of a quantity incorrectly,50/100*48 = 23 students scored over 80%,ok,ok,,5,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""The number of students who scored 100% is 2/5*80 = 32\nOut of the 80 students, 80-32 = 48 did not score 100%.\nIf 50% of the students who did not score 100% managed to score over 80%, then 50/100*48 =24 students scored over 80%\nThe number of students who failed is 48-24 = 24\n#### 24"", ""original_answer"": 24.0, ""transformed_answer"": 25.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""In this example, the calculation of 50/100*48 has been changed to result in 23 instead of 24. This is a calculation error introduced in step 3. As a result, the number of students who failed (step 4) will be based on this incorrect number. Therefore, step 4 has been modified to adjust for the change in step 3, updating the calculation to 48-23 = 25, which becomes the transformed answer. Only one calculation error has been introduced and it has correctly affected the subsequent step without altering the previous steps or unrelated parts of the solution."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:18e10ef613c059ba,full,counting_error,"Danivan Drugstore has 4500 bottles of hand sanitizer gel in inventory at the beginning of the week. On Monday 2445 bottles were sold, on Tuesday 900 bottles were sold, and 50 bottles each day for the rest of the week were sold (from Wednesday until Sunday). On Saturday, the supplier delivers an order for 650 bottles. How many bottles of sanitizer gel does the Drugstore have at the end of the week?","The number of bottles sold on Monday and Tuesday is 2445 + 900 = 3345 bottles
Each day of the rest of the week, 50 bottles were sold, that is 6 * 50 = 300 bottles sold.
Now the total of bottles sold in a week is 3345 + 300 = 3645 bottles
The drugstore has in stock 4500 – 3645 = 855 bottles
Finally the drugstore has 855 + 650 the supplier brought = 1505 bottles at the end of the week.
#### 1505",Counts the days in a stated interval incorrectly,that is 6 * 50 = 300 bottles sold.,ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The number of bottles sold on Monday and Tuesday is 2445 + 900 = 3345 bottles\nEach day of the rest of the week, 50 bottles were sold, that is 5 * 50 = 250 bottles sold.\nNow the total of bottles sold in a week is 3345 + 250= 3595 bottles\nThe drugstore has in stock 4500 – 3595= 905 bottles\nFinally the drugstore has 905 + 650 the supplier brought = 1555 bottles at the end of the week.\n#### 1555"", ""original_answer"": 1555.0, ""transformed_answer"": 1505.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 mistakenly counts the rest of the days of the week (Wednesday to Sunday) as 6 days instead of 5 days, resulting in 5 * 50 = 250 becoming 6 * 50 = 300. This affects the subsequent related steps; step 3 changes from 3345 + 250 = 3595 to 3345 + 300 = 3645, and step 4 changes from 4500 – 3595 = 905 to 4500 – 3645 = 855, along with step 5 changing from 905 + 650 = 1555 to 855 + 650 = 1505. It should be noted that only one step of counting error is introduced, and the other calculations are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:192393f5aa18566a,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"At what price must an article costing Rs. 100 be marked in order that after deducting 5% from the list price, it may be sold at a profit of 25% on the cost price?","List price = Cost price + Profit + Packing charges
List price = 100 + (25/100)*100 + 10 = 135
Marked price = List price / (1 - 5/100) = 135 / 0.95 = 142.11
#### 142.11",Adds an unsupported extra charge to the price calculation,List price = 100 + (25/100)*100 + 10 = 135,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""List price = Cost price + Profit\nList price = 100 + (25/100)*100 = 125\nMarked price = List price / (1 - 5/100) = 125 / 0.95 = 131.58\n#### 131.58"", ""original_answer"": 131.58, ""transformed_answer"": 142.11, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <+ Packing charges>, resulting in step 2 changing from 100 + (25/100)*100 = 125 in the original_solution to 100 + (25/100)*100 + 10 = 135 in the transformed_solution. This added packing charge of Rs. 10 affects the calculation, resulting in a new marked price of 135 / 0.95 = 142.11 instead of the original 125 / 0.95 = 131.58. The rest of the calculation process remains correct, only considering this single error of adding irrelevant information."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:195870f4e71f02ab,full,missing_step,"Last month, Tasha made $80 from selling lemonade and mowing lawns. The first week, she mowed Kamala's lawn thrice as many times as she did Joe's. The following week, she mowed Alba's lawn five times as she did Joe's. If Joe paid Tasha $6 for her work, how much did she make from lemonade sales?","Since the lawn mowing sessions at Alba's were 5 times those at Joe's Tasha also made 5 * 6 = 30 dollars.
Adding up all the money earned from lawn mowing gives 6 + 6 + 30 = 42 dollars
Subtracting the lawn mowing money from the total amount gives us 80 - 42 = 38 dollars
Tasha therefore made 38 dollars selling lemonade.
#### 38",Omits multiplying one amount by its stated ratio,Adding up all the money earned from lawn mowing gives 6 + 6 + 30 = 42 dollars,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""At Kamala's Tasha made three times what she made mowing Joe's lawn, which gives 3 * 6 = 18 dollars\nSince the lawn mowing sessions at Alba's were 5 times those at Joe's Tasha also made 5 * 6 = 30 dollars.\nAdding up all the money earned from lawn mowing gives 6 + 18 + 30 = 54 dollars\nSubtracting the lawn mowing money from the total amount gives us 80 - 54 = 26 dollars\nTasha therefore made 26 dollars selling lemonade.\n#### 26"", ""original_answer"": 26.0, ""transformed_answer"": 38.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""In this transformed_solution, the missing intermediate step is the calculation of the money Tasha made at Kamala's, which is 3 * 6 = 18 dollars. As a consequence, step 2 in the transformed_solution is the first instance that does not follow from the preceding steps, instead combining Tasha's earnings from Joe's lawn (6 dollars) with Alba's (30 dollars). This step also adds another lawn mowing session at Joe's (6 dollars), which corresponds to the largest number referenced by the deleted step 1 related to lawn mowing. The subsequent steps are all affected by this change and are modified to calculate and subtract from the total based on the incorrect total lawn mowing earnings. It is step 2 in the transformed_solution, that serves as the first step referencing the incorrect total after deleting the first step, so the wrong_step is 2."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:198827e0604c8edf,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,Each bank teller has 10 rolls of coins. Each roll has 25 coins. How many coins do four bank tellers have in all?,"Each bank teller has 10 x 25 = 250 coins.
Thus, four bank tellers have 250 x 3 = 750 coins.
#### 750",Uses the wrong number of groups in the final multiplication,"Thus, four bank tellers have 250 x 3 = 750 coins.",ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Each bank teller has 10 x 25 = 250 coins.\nThus, four bank tellers have 250 x 4 = 1000 coins.\n#### 1000"", ""original_answer"": 1000.0, ""transformed_answer"": 750.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 mistakenly references 4 as 3 from step 1 and changes 250 x 4 = 1000 coins to 250 x 3 = 750 coins. It should be noted that step 1 in the transformed_solution <Each bank teller has 10 x 25 = 250 coins.> should be the same as the step 1 in the original_solution <Each bank teller has 10 x 25 = 250 coins.> and calculates its result 250 correctly. The error of referencing has caused the original answer to change from 1000 coins to 750 coins. Despite the referencing error in step 2, the calculation of this step is still internally consistent. The transformed_answer is different from the original_answer, and there is only a single error considered within the entire transformation process."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:1990943543a2ee9f,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"A man has some hens and cows. If the number of heads be 48 and the number of feet equals 140, then the number of hens will be:","Assuming there were also 2 duck family pets, adding an extra 2 heads and 8 feet, the equations will be adjusted accordingly. Let h = number of hens, c = number of cows, and d = number of ducks, where d = 2.
The number of heads equation with ducks is h + c + d = 48 + 2
The number of feet equation with ducks is 2h + 4c + 2d = 140 + 8
Solve the first equation for c: c + d = 50 - h
Substitute c + d = 50 - h into the second equation: 2h + 4(50 - h -2) = 148
Simplify the equation: 2h + 200 - 4h - 8 = 148
Combine like terms: -2h + 192 = 148
Subtract 192 from both sides: -2h = -44
Divide by -2: h = 22
#### 22",Introduces irrelevant entities and modifies the given totals,"Assuming there were also 2 duck family pets, adding an extra 2 heads and 8 feet",ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let h = number of hens and c = number of cows\nThe number of heads equation is h + c = 48\nThe number of feet equation is 2h + 4c = 140\nSolve the first equation for c: c = 48 - h\nSubstitute c = 48 - h into the second equation: 2h + 4(48 - h) = 140\nSimplify the equation: 2h + 192 - 4h = 140\nCombine like terms: -2h + 192 = 140\nSubtract 192 from both sides: -2h = -52\nDivide by -2: h = 26\n#### 26"", ""original_answer"": 26, ""transformed_answer"": 22, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""The assumption of additional ducks incorrectly adds information not mentioned in the question statement. This error is introduced at step 1, where the equation for the number of heads is adjusted to account for the additional ducks, resulting in a changed outcome where the number of hens calculated is 22 rather than the original 26 because of the fabricated extra 2 heads and 8 feet attributed to ducks."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:199ee343951536cb,full,missing_step,a batsman scored 210 runs which included 3 boundaries and 8 sixes. what % of his total score did he make by running between the wickets,"He scored 48 runs from sixes because 8 x 6 = 48
He scored 210 - 48 = 162 runs by running between the wickets and boundaries
He made 77.14% of his total score by running between the wickets and boundaries because (162 / 210) x 100 = 77.14
#### 77.14",Includes boundary runs when calculating runs scored by running between wickets,162 runs by running between the wickets and boundaries,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,"between, running, runs, scored, wickets",True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""He scored 6 runs from boundaries because 3 x 4 = 12\nHe scored 48 runs from sixes because 8 x 6 = 48\nHe scored 150 runs by running between the wickets because 210 - 12 - 48 = 150\nHe made 71.4% of his total score by running between the wickets because (150 / 210) x 100 = 71.4\n#### 71.4"", ""original_answer"": 71.4, ""transformed_answer"": 77.14, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""In this example, the first intermediate step has been removed. In the original_solution, step 1 references the number 3 from the question and the result of step 1 is 12, which is used in step 3. The removal of step 1 involves changing step 3 so that instead of subtracting the results of step 1 and step 2 from 210, only the result of step 2 (sixes) is subtracted. This changes the wording to 'He scored 210 - 48 = 162 runs by running between the wickets and boundaries' instead of '210 - 12 - 48'. The calculation in step 4 is then altered to use 162 instead of 150, changing '(150 / 210) x 100' to '(162 / 210) x 100', which results in a percentage of 77.14 instead of 71.4. The wrong_step is 2 because this is the step in the transformed_solution that first references the result of the deleted step."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:19aa21cdc7ecd717,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"If two dice are thrown together, the probability of getting an even number on one die and an odd number on the other is -","Total outcomes when two dice are thrown = 6 * 6 = 36
Favorable outcomes when one die is even and the other is odd = 3 * 3 = 9
Probability = Favorable outcomes / Total outcomes = 9 / 40 = 9/40
#### 9/40",Uses an incorrect total when dividing favorable outcomes by total outcomes,Probability = Favorable outcomes / Total outcomes = 9 / 40 = 9/40,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Total outcomes when two dice are thrown = 6 * 6 = 36\nFavorable outcomes when one die is even and the other is odd = 3 * 3 = 9\nProbability = Favorable outcomes / Total outcomes = 9 / 36 = 1/4\n#### 1/4"", ""original_answer"": 0.25, ""transformed_answer"": 0.225, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 mistakenly references 36 as 40 from step 1 and changes 9 / 36 to 9 / 40. It should be noted that step 1 in the transformed_solution <Total outcomes when two dice are thrown = 6 * 6 = 36> should be the same as the step 1 in the original_solution <Total outcomes when two dice are thrown = 6 * 6 = 36> and calculates its result 36 correctly. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of all steps is still correct, but the transformed solution reflects this single mistake by changing the denominator to 40, which is incorrect, leading to a different and incorrect answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:19bf0a3ce2dcb9e0,full,unit_conversion_error,"The school nurse must conduct insect checks at the elementary school. She must check 26 Kindergarteners, 19 first graders, 20 second graders, and 25 third graders. If each check takes 2 minutes, how many hours will it take the nurse to complete all the checks?","The school nurse must check a total of 26 Kindergarteners + 19 first graders + 20 second graders + 25 third graders = 90 students in all.
If every check takes 2 minutes, it will take 90 students x 2 minutes = 180 minutes.
There are 50 minutes in one hour, so it will take 180 minutes / 50 = 3.6 hours to complete the checks.
#### 3.6",Uses an incorrect number of minutes per hour for unit conversion,There are 50 minutes in one hour,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""The school nurse must check a total of 26 Kindergarteners + 19 first graders + 20 second graders + 25 third graders = 90 students in all.\nIf every check takes 2 minutes, it will take 90 students x 2 minutes = 180 minutes.\nThere are 60 minutes in one hour, so it will take 180 minutes / 60 = 3 hours to complete the checks.\n#### 3"", ""original_answer"": 3.0, ""transformed_answer"": 3.6, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 3 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 hour wrongly has 50 minutes instead of 60 minutes, resulting in 180 minutes / 60 = 3 hours in the original_solution becoming 180 minutes / 50 = 3.6 hours in the transformed_solution. It should be noted that you only need to consider one step of unit conversion error, so the calculation of step 1 and 2 and the other processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:19deb121eaee7df9,full,missing_step,"Sam and Jeff had a skipping competition at recess. The competition was split into four rounds. Sam completed 1 more skip than Jeff in the first round. Jeff skipped 3 fewer times than Sam in the second round. Jeff skipped 4 more times than Sam in the third round. Jeff got tired and only completed half the number of skips as Sam in the last round. If Sam skipped 16 times in each round, what is the average number of skips per round completed by Jeff?","In round two, Jeff completed 16 - 3 = 13.
In round three, Jeff completed 16 + 4 = 20.
In round four, Jeff completed 16 / 2 = 8.
Jeff completed 16 + 13 + 20 + 8 = 57 skips in total.
Jeff skipped an average of 57 / 4 = 14.25 skips per round.
#### 14.25",Uses the given count instead of calculating the corresponding count for one round,Jeff completed 16 + 13 + 20 + 8 = 57 skips in total.,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,round,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""In round one, Jeff completed 16 - 1 = 15.\nIn round two, Jeff completed 16 - 3 = 13.\nIn round three, Jeff completed 16 + 4 = 20.\nIn round four, Jeff completed 16 / 2 = 8.\nJeff completed 15 + 13 + 20 + 8 = 56 skips in total.\nJeff skipped an average of 56 / 4 = 14 skips per round.\n#### 14"", ""original_answer"": 14.0, ""transformed_answer"": 14.25, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""In this example, the first step is removed, which detailed how many skips Jeff completed in round one. Thus, step 4 should transition from referencing Jeff's skips in round one to directly referencing Sam's skips from the question. In round one, Jeff did 16 - 1 skips (15 skips), so the 15 is no longer calculated and 16 is used directly in the total computation instead. The updated formula is 'Jeff completed 16 + 13 + 20 + 8 = 57 skips in total.' This mistake modifies the rest of the calculations, leading to an altered total and average. In step 5, which previously added up Jeff's total skips, the number 15 from step 1 is replaced by 16, the number in the question, adjusting the total. This step becomes the new step 4 in the transformed_solution and is therefore the wrong_step where the first incorrect reference occurs."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:19dfd01d94bb999c,full,confusing_formula_error,Hannah hangs a 2 foot by 4 foot painting on a 5 foot by 10 foot wall. What percentage of the wall is taken up by the painting?,"First find the area of the painting: 2 feet * 4 feet = 8 square feet
Then find the perimeter of the wall: 5 feet + 5 feet + 10 feet + 10 feet = 30 feet
Then divide the area of the painting by the perimeter of the wall and multiply by 100% to express the answer as a percentage: 8 square feet / 30 feet * 100% = 26.67%
#### 26.67",Uses the perimeter instead of the area when calculating percentage,Then find the perimeter of the wall: 5 feet + 5 feet + 10 feet + 10 feet = 30 feet,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""First find the area of the painting: 2 feet * 4 feet = 8 square feet\nThen find the area of the wall: 5 feet * 10 feet = 50 square feet\nThen divide the area of the painting by the area of the wall and multiply by 100% to express the answer as a percentage: 8 square feet / 50 square feet * 100% = 16%\n#### 16"", ""original_answer"": 16.0, ""transformed_answer"": 26.67, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 confuses the perimeter and area of the wall. The question requires that the area of the wall to be solved be equal to the length multiplied by width, rather than the length plus length plus width plus width, which is equivalent to the perimeter of the rectangle, resulting in 5 feet * 10 feet = 50 square feet in the original_solution being changed to 5 feet + 5 feet + 10 feet + 10 feet = 30 feet in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent step 3, changing from dividing the area of the painting by the area of the wall to dividing the area of the painting by the perimeter of the wall. The rest of the steps, such as finding the area of the painting in step 1 and calculating the percentage in step 3, remain correct according to the problem's requirements. Note that the language description of step 2 < Then find the area of the wall: > in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:19e7ffa98bc30c68,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"A metal company's old machine makes bolts at a constant rate of 100 bolts per hour. The company's new machine makes bolts at a constant rate of 150 bolts per hour. If both machines start at the same time and continue making bolts simultaneously, how many minutes will it take the two machines to make a total of 400 bolts?","Combined rate of both machines is 100 + 150 = 250 bolts per hour.
It will take 400 / 250 = 1.6 hours to make 400 bolts.
Assuming there is a maintenance break of 15 minutes during the operation, there are 60 minutes in an hour, so it will take 1.6 x 60 + 15 = 111 minutes to make 400 bolts.
#### 111",Adds an unsupported extra time or condition to the calculation,Assuming there is a maintenance break of 15 minutes during the operation,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Combined rate of both machines is 100 + 150 = 250 bolts per hour.\nIt will take 400 / 250 = 1.6 hours to make 400 bolts.\nThere are 60 minutes in an hour so it will take 1.6 x 60 = 96 minutes to make 400 bolts.\n#### 96"", ""original_answer"": 96.0, ""transformed_answer"": 111.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Assuming there is a maintenance break of 15 minutes during the operation>, resulting in step 3 changing from 1.6 x 60 = 96 minutes in the original_solution to 1.6 x 60 + 15 = 111 minutes in the transformed_solution. The addition of a maintenance break affects the final calculation and thus changes the original answer. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:1a21bd74d1f5102d,full,referencing_context_value_error,"A baseball cap factory made 320 caps the first week, 400 the second week, and 300 the third week. If the company makes their average number of caps from the first 3 weeks during the fourth week, how many total caps will they make?","During the first 3 weeks the factory made 320 caps + 400 caps + 250 caps = 970 caps total.
The average for the first 3 weeks is 970 caps / 3 weeks = 323.33.
If the factor makes the average during the fourth week they will make 970 caps + 323.33 = 1293.33 caps.
#### 1293.33",Uses an incorrect given value when computing the total,320 caps + 400 caps + 250 caps = 970 caps total,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""During the first 3 weeks the factory made 320 caps + 400 caps + 300 caps = 1020 caps total.\nThe average for the first 3 weeks is 1020 caps / 3 weeks = 340.\nIf the factor makes the average during the fourth week they will make 1020 caps + 340 = 1360 caps.\n#### 1360"", ""original_answer"": 1360.0, ""transformed_answer"": 1293.33, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references 300 in the question as 250 wrongly, and calculates 320+400+250=970 instead of 320+400+300=1020. Its subsequent related steps, step 2 and 3, will be affected. Step 2 changes from 1020/3=340 to 970/3=323.33 and step 3 changes from 1020+340=1360 to 970+323.33=1293.33. Please note that you should only consider one error, so all other steps that reference this number (300 caps for the third week) should still reference it correctly except for the step referenced incorrectly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:1a2752ac113db125,full,unit_conversion_error,"Kramer can pack 10 boxes of cigarettes per minute. If there are 5 boxes of cigarettes in one case, how many cases can Kramer pack in 2 hours?","In 1 minute, Kramer can pack 10 boxes, so in 1 minute, he can pack 10/5 = 2 cases.
In 2 hours, there are 2 hours * 50 minutes per hour = 100 minutes.
So, in 2 hours, Kramer can pack 2 cases per minute * 100 minutes = 200 cases.
#### 200",Uses an incorrect minutes-per-hour conversion,"In 2 hours, there are 2 hours * 50 minutes per hour = 100 minutes.",ok,ok,,5,False,False,False,,True,11,"{""original_solution"": ""In 1 minute, Kramer can pack 10 boxes, so in 1 minute, he can pack 10/5 = 2 cases.\nIn 2 hours, there are 2 hours * 60 minutes per hour = 120 minutes.\nSo, in 2 hours, Kramer can pack 2 cases per minute * 120 minutes = 240 cases.\n#### 240"", ""original_answer"": 240.0, ""transformed_answer"": 200.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means the duration of 1 hour is wrongly taken as 50 minutes instead of 60 minutes, resulting in 2 hours * 60 minutes per hour = 120 minutes in the original_solution becoming 2 hours * 50 minutes per hour = 100 minutes in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent related step 3, changing from 2 cases per minute * 120 minutes = 240 cases to 2 cases per minute * 100 minutes = 200 cases. The other calculation steps are correctly maintained from the original_solution."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:1a32a623818964af,full,confusing_formula_error,"A cheese factory sells its cheese in rectangular blocks. A normal block has a volume of 5 cubic feet. If a large block has twice the width, twice the depth, and the same length of a normal block, what is the volume of cheese in a large block in cubic feet?","The volume of the large block is calculated as follows: 
Let V = volume, L = length, W = width, D = depth
For a normal block, V = L*W*D = 5 cubic feet
For a large block, L remains the same, but W = 2*W and D = 2*D
So the surface area of the large block would be 2*(L*W + W*D + D*L) = 5*2*2*3 = 60 cubic feet
#### 60",Uses a surface-area formula instead of the volume formula,So the surface area of the large block would be 2*(L*W + W*D + D*L) = 5*2*2*3 = 60 cubic feet,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The volume of the large block is calculated as follows: \nLet V = volume, L = length, W = width, D = depth\nFor a normal block, V = L*W*D = 5 cubic feet\nFor a large block, L remains the same, but W = 2*W and D = 2*D\nSo the volume of the large block is V = L*(2W)*(2D) = 5*2*2 = 20 cubic feet\n#### 20"", ""original_answer"": 20.0, ""transformed_answer"": 60.0, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""In this case, step 4 incorrectly applies a formula for calculating surface area in place of volume. The question calculates volume using the formula L*W*D for a normal block and then applies the same approach for a large block with modified dimensions (2W and 2D). However, in the transformed_solution, instead of using the volume formula L*(2W)*(2D), it mistakenly calculates what appears to be the surface area using an incorrect formula 2*(L*W + W*D + D*L). The multiplier \""3\"" should not be there, as the correct surface area formula does not require it, and the context of the problem is volume calculation, not surface area. This modifies the correct value of 20 cubic feet to an incorrect transformed_answer of 60 cubic feet. The explanation indicates the mistake of replacing the volume calculation with an incorrect surface area calculation in the context of the problem."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:1ae177be368333d0,full,unit_conversion_error,"Jane, Kyla, and Anthony have summer jobs in a resort. Their task is to fold guests' towels. Jane can fold 3 towels in 5 minutes. Kyla can fold 5 towels in 10 minutes, and Anthony can fold 7 towels in 20 minutes. If they all fold towels together, how many towels can they fold in one hour?","There are 1 x 50 minutes = 50 minutes in 1 hour.
There are 50/5 = 10 sets of 5 minutes in 1 hour.
So, Jane can fold 3 x 10 = 30 towels in an hour.
There are 50/10 = 5 sets of 10 minutes in 1 hour.
So, Kyla can fold 5 x 5 = 25 towels in an hour.
There are 50/20 = 2.5 sets of 20 minutes in 1 hour.
So, Anthony can fold 7 x 2.5 = 17.5 towels in an hour.
Therefore, the 3 of them can fold a total of 30 + 25 + 17.5 = 72.5 towels in 1 hour.
#### 72.5",Converts one hour to 50 minutes instead of 60,There are 1 x 50 minutes = 50 minutes in 1 hour.,ok,ok,,7,False,False,True,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""There are 1 x 60 minutes = 60 minutes in 1 hour.\nThere are 60/5 = 12 sets of 5 minutes in 1 hour.\nSo, Jane can fold 3 x 12 = 36 towels in an hour.\nThere are 60/10 = 6 sets of 10 minutes in 1 hour.\nSo, Kyla can fold 5 x 6 = 30 towels in an hour.\nThere are 60/20 = 3 sets of 20 minutes in 1 hour.\nSo, Anthony can fold 7 x 3 = 21 towels in an hour.\nTherefore, the 3 of them can fold a total of 36 + 30 + 21 = 87 towels in 1 hour.\n#### 87"", ""original_answer"": 87.0, ""transformed_answer"": 72.5, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 hour wrongly has 50 minutes instead of the usual 60 minutes, resulting in all follow-up calculations based on the time being computed in a 50-minute hour. Consequently, the sets of time divisions for Jane, Kyla, and Anthony are recalculated based on a 50-minute hour (steps 2, 4, and 6) instead of 60 minutes, which affects their individual totals (steps 3, 5, and 7) and the final cumulative total (step 8). This error in step 1 is also classified as a single error despite impacting subsequent related steps."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:1af22c41f08eadd6,full,unit_conversion_error,"Excluding stoppages, the speed of a bus is 54 kmph and including stoppages, it is 36 kmph. For how many minutes does the bus stop per hour?","Let x be the time the bus stops per hour.
According to the given information, the distance covered excluding stoppages is the same as that including stoppages. Therefore, the time taken excluding stoppages is less than the time taken including stoppages.
Distance = speed x time
The speed of the bus without stoppages is 54 kmph, and with stoppages is 36 kmph, which means the bus covers 18 km less in an hour when it stops.
To find out for how long the bus stops, we calculate the difference in distance traveled: 54 km/h - 36 km/h = 18 km/h difference.
This difference of 18 km occurs because the bus is stopping for some time during the hour. To convert this lost distance back into time, we find out the fraction of the hour the bus stoppages take, which is the difference in speed (18 km/h) divided by the original speed (54 km/h).
This gives us 18/54 of an hour the bus is stopped. To convert this fraction to minutes incorrectly, we multiply by 50 minutes/hour instead of the correct 60 minutes/hour.
Thus, the bus stops for (18/54) * 50 minutes per hour = 16.67 minutes per hour.
#### 16.67",Uses an incorrect hours-to-minutes conversion factor,we multiply by 50 minutes/hour instead of the correct 60 minutes/hour.,ok,ok,,6,False,False,False,,True,7,"{""original_solution"": ""Let x be the time the bus stops per hour.\nAccording to the given information, the distance covered excluding stoppages is the same as that including stoppages. Therefore, the time taken excluding stoppages is less than the time taken including stoppages.\nDistance = speed x time\nThe speed of the bus without stoppages is 54 kmph, and with stoppages is 36 kmph, which means the bus covers 18 km less in an hour when it stops.\nTo find out for how long the bus stops, we calculate the difference in distance traveled: 54 km/h - 36 km/h = 18 km/h difference.\nThis difference of 18 km occurs because the bus is stopping for some time during the hour. To convert this lost distance back into time, we find out the fraction of the hour the bus stoppages take, which is the difference in speed (18 km/h) divided by the original speed (54 km/h).\nThis gives us 18/54 of an hour the bus is stopped. To convert this fraction to minutes, we multiply by 60 minutes/hour.\nThus, the bus stops for (18/54) * 60 minutes per hour = 20 minutes per hour.\n#### 20"", ""original_answer"": 20, ""transformed_answer"": 16.67, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 3 makes an incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 hour is wrongly multiplied by 50 minutes instead of the correct 60 minutes, resulting in an error in calculating the time the bus stops per hour. The original solution used the correct figure of 60 minutes per hour, but the transformed solution incorrectly uses 50 minutes per hour, leading to a calculated time of (18/54) * 50 = 16.67 minutes per hour that the bus stops, rather than the correct answer of 20."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:1b0425f69ca5be84,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,Jordan read 120 French novels last holiday. His brother Alexandre read 1/10 of what Jordan read. How many more novels did Jordan read than Alexandre?,"Alexandre read 120 * 1/10 = 12 novels.
