#### 8",Substitutes the wrong given value into the equation,x + 2(2) = 12,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Substitute the value of y into the first equation: x + 2(3) = 12\nSimplify the equation: x + 6 = 12\nSubtract 6 from both sides: x = 12 - 6\nx = 6\n#### 6"", ""original_answer"": 6.0, ""transformed_answer"": 8.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references the value of y as 2 instead of 3, mistakenly using 2(2) = 4 when performing the substitution. The correct substitution should have been 2(3) = 6. This error affects all subsequent calculations, leading to an erroneous final answer of x = 8 instead of the correct x = 6. Each step following the error performs the arithmetical operations correctly, albeit based on the initial incorrect substitution."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:2d2c7b8c93364a53,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"On Friday, Hank opened his used-bicycle store with a stock of bicycles, all fixed-up and ready to ride. Over the course of the day, he sold 10 bicycles and bought an additional 15 to fix up. On Saturday, he sold 12 bicycles and bought 8 more. And on Sunday, he sold 9 bicycles and bought 11 more. What was the net increase in the number of bicycles in stock in Hank's store over the three days?","He sold 10+12+9=31 bicycles.
