#### 42",Includes a number outside the specified range,including an extra number 63 mistakenly included within the range.,ok,ok,,6,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The numbers divisible by 7 between 18 and 57 are 21, 28, 35, 42, 49, 56.\nThe sum of these numbers is 21+28+35+42+49+56 = 231.\nThe average is 231/6 = 38.5.\n#### 38.5"", ""original_answer"": 38.5, ""transformed_answer"": 42.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <including an extra number 63 mistakenly included within the range>, resulting in step 2 changing from 21+28+35+42+49+56 = 231 in the original_solution to 21+28+35+42+49+56+63 = 294 in the transformed_solution. The related subsequent step also changes to reflect this new total number, with the average calculated as 294/7 = 42 instead of 231/6. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:4eabab8c32f16c52,full,counting_error,"One week, a certain truck rental lot had a total of 18 trucks, all of which were on the lot Monday morning. If 50% of the trucks that were rented out during the week were returned to the lot on or before Saturday morning of that week, and if there were at least 12 trucks on the lot that Saturday morning, what is the greatest number of different trucks that could have been rented out during the week?","18 trucks were on the lot on Monday morning
If at least 12 trucks were on the lot on Saturday morning, then at most 18 - 6 = 12 trucks were rented out
If 50% of the rented out trucks were returned by Saturday morning, then at most 12 / 0.5 = 24 trucks were rented out
The greatest number of different trucks that could have been rented out during the week is 24 trucks
#### 24",Confuses trucks currently absent with the total number rented over time,"If at least 12 trucks were on the lot Saturday morning, then at most 18 - 6 = 12 trucks were rented out",ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,"rented, trucks",False,1,"{""original_solution"": ""18 trucks were on the lot on Monday morning\nIf at least 12 trucks were on the lot on Saturday morning, then at most 18 - 12 = 6 trucks were rented out\nIf 50% of the rented out trucks were returned by Saturday morning, then at most 6 / 0.5 = 12 trucks were rented out\nThe greatest number of different trucks that could have been rented out during the week is 12 trucks\n#### 12"", ""original_answer"": 12, ""transformed_answer"": 24, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 erroneously subtracts 6 instead of 12 from 18, suggesting that 6 trucks were on the lot instead of 12, which does not adhere to the condition of having 'at least 12 trucks on the lot'. This error transforms the calculation from 18-12 to 18-6, increasing the number of rented out trucks that could have been returned. This counting error affects step 3 by making the number appear as though 24 trucks could be rented out, which is not possible since there are only 18 trucks total. Therefore, the transformation introduces a counting mistake in step 2, and as a result, the wrong conclusion is drawn in the final step."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:4eb05604ca958817,full,calculation_error,"a and b finish the job in 15 days. while a, b and c can finish it in 3 days. c alone will finish the job in","a, b, and c together finish 1/3 of the job in 1 day
