thus in total, for each 6 minutes of performance time, she spends 18 minutes + 30 minutes + 6 minutes + 9 minutes (for warming up) = 63 minutes on all four activities.
#### 63",Adds an unmentioned activity to the requested total,she also spends 9 minutes warming up her voice before the performance,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""For each 6 minutes she performs, Carlotta spends 6 * 3 minutes = 18 minutes practicing.\nFor each 6 minutes she performs, Carlotta throws tantrums for 6 * 5 minutes = 30 minutes.\nThus, in total, for each 6 minutes of performance time, she spends 18 minutes + 30 minutes + 6 minutes = 54 minutes on all three activities.\n#### 54"", ""original_answer"": 54.0, ""transformed_answer"": 63.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information not mentioned in the question statement <Additionally, she also spends 9 minutes warming up her voice before the performance>, resulting in step 3 changing from 18 minutes + 30 minutes + 6 minutes = 54 minutes in the original_solution to 18 minutes + 30 minutes + 6 minutes + 9 minutes = 63 minutes in the transformed_solution. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:5ee9c4fe7bd41203,full,referencing_context_value_error,"The rim of a standard basketball hoop is 10 feet above the ground.  Additionally, most experts will say that a player must be able to reach at least 6 inches above the rim to dunk a basketball.  Suppose a basketball player is 6 feet tall and can reach 22 inches above their head using the wingspan of their arms.  How high must this player be able to jump to reach 6 inches above the rim to dunk a basketball?","In inches, the height of the rim is 11*12 = 132 inches.
Thus, the height that the player needs to be able to reach is 132 + 6 = 138 inches.
Without jumping, the player can reach their height plus an additional 22 inches, or a total of 6*12 + 22 = 94 inches.
Therefore, the player needs to jump 138 - 94 = 44 inches to reach the height required to dunk a basketball.
#### 44",,"Therefore, the player needs to jump 138 - 94 = 44 inches",label_conflict,ok,,0,True,True,False,,True,0,"{""original_solution"": ""In inches, the height of the rim is 10*12 = 120 inches.\nThus, the height that the player needs to be able to reach is 120 + 6 = 126 inches.\nWithout jumping, the player can reach their height plus an additional 22 inches, or a total of 6*12 + 22 = 94 inches.\nTherefore, the player needs to jump 126 - 94 = 32 inches to reach the height required to dunk a basketball.\n#### 32"", ""original_answer"": 32.0, ""transformed_answer"": 44.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references 10 in the question as 11 wrongly and calculates 11*12=132 instead of 10*12=120. Its subsequent related steps, step 2 and 4, will be affected. Step 2 changes from 120+6=126 to 132+6=138, and step 4 changes from 126-94=32 to 138-94=44. Please note that you should only consider one error, so step 3 still references 6 in the question correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:5f13c7f24396ff3d,full,confusing_formula_error,"Bob is building a vegetable garden. It is 220 feet long by 120 feet wide. Half of the garden is going to be tilled land. A third of the rest of it will have trellises for vining plants, and the rest will be devoted to raised beds. How big will the raised bed section of the garden be?","The garden is 220 + 120 + 220 + 120 = 680 square feet.
The tilled land will take up half the area, and the trellises and raised beds will be the other half. Each half is 680 / 2 = 340 square feet.
The trellises will take up 1/3 of the remaining land, so the raised beds will take 1 - 1/3 = 2/3 of the remaining land.
So the total area of the raised bed area will be 340 square feet * (2 / 3) = 226.67 square feet.
#### 226.67",Uses a perimeter formula instead of an area formula,The garden is 220 + 120 + 220 + 120 = 680 square feet.,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""The garden is 220 * 120 = 26,400 square feet.\nThe tilled land will take up half the area, and the trellises and raised beds will be the other half. Each half is 26,400 / 2 = 13,200 square feet.\nThe trellises will take up 1/3 of the remaining land, so the raised beds will take 1 - 1/3 = 2/3 of the remaining land.\nSo the total area of the raised bed area will be 13,200 square feet * (2 / 3) = 8,800 square feet.\n#### 8800"", ""original_answer"": 8800.0, ""transformed_answer"": 226.67, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 mistakenly uses the formula for calculating the perimeter of a rectangle instead of the area. The original solution calculates the area correctly with the formula 220 * 120, but the transformed solution incorrectly calculates the perimeter with the formula 220 + 120 + 220 + 120. This confusion of formula leads to the incorrect result of 680 square feet for the perimeter instead of 26,400 square feet for the area. As a result, all subsequent calculations based on the perimeter value are affected, yet the calculations using fractions of the original area remain correctly applied to the incorrect value. The language descriptions remain unchanged while the formulas applied within have been altered to reflect the confusion between area and perimeter in step 1."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:5f143c805bf10c90,full,confusing_formula_error,"Jerry needs to shingle 3 roofs. Each roof is made of two slanted rectangular sides measuring 20 feet by 40 feet. If he needs 8 shingles to cover one square foot of roof, how many shingles does he need total?","First find the area of one side of a roof: 20 feet + 40 feet + 20 feet + 40 feet = 120 square feet
Then multiply that number by 2 to find the total area of both sides of the roof: 120 square feet/side * 2 sides = 240 square feet
Then multiply that number by the number of roofs to find the total area of all the roofs: 240 square feet/roof * 3 roofs = 720 square feet
Then multiply the total area of the roofs by the number of shingles needed per square foot to find the total number of shingles needed: 720 square feet * 8 singles/square foot = 5760 shingles
#### 5760",Uses addition of side lengths instead of multiplying dimensions to find area,20 feet + 40 feet + 20 feet + 40 feet = 120 square feet,ok,ok,,12,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""First find the area of one side of a roof: 20 feet * 40 feet = 800 square feet\nThen multiply that number by 2 to find the total area of both sides of the roof: 800 square feet/side * 2 sides = 1600 square feet\nThen multiply that number by the number of roofs to find the total area of all the roofs: 1600 square feet/roof * 3 roofs = 4800 square feet\nThen multiply the total area of the roofs by the number of shingles needed per square foot to find the total number of shingles needed: 4800 square feet * 8 singles/square foot = 38400 shingles\n#### 38400"", ""original_answer"": 38400.0, ""transformed_answer"": 5760.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 confuses the perimeter and area of rectangle. The question requires that the area of the rectangle to be solved be equal to the length multiplied by width, rather than the sum of all its sides, which is equivalent to the perimeter of the rectangle, resulting in 20 feet * 40 feet = 800 square feet in the original_solution becoming 20 feet + 40 feet + 20 feet + 40 feet = 120 square feet in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent related steps due to the propagation of the error in the calculations, changing the original answers from 1600 square feet, 4800 square feet, and 38400 shingles to the transformed answers of 240 square feet, 720 square feet, and 5760 shingles respectively. Note that the language description of step 1 <First find the area of one side of a roof> in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that only step 1 has the confusing formula error, and the other calculation processes follow this mistaken step correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:5f91a4b975d68238,full,referencing_context_value_error,"Seth is twice as old as Brooke. In 2 years, the sum of their ages will be 28. How old is Seth?","To solve this problem, we can set up an equation where x represents Brooke’s age and 2x represents Seth’s age and noting that in three years, the sum of their ages is (2x + 3) + (x + 3) = 28.
Then we add all the values together, 3x + 6 = 28.
And we subtract 6 from each side, 3x + 6 - 6 = 28 – 6.
Then we divide each side by 3, x = 22/3.
We find that x = 22/3, or Brooke’s age is approximately 7.33.
Seth is twice as old as Brooke, so Seth is approximately 7.33 x 2 = 14.67.
#### 14.67",Uses the wrong time interval when forming future-age expressions,"noting that in three years, the sum of their ages is (2x + 3) + (x + 3) = 28.",ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""To solve this problem, we can set up an equation where x represents Brooke’s age and 2x represents Seth’s age and noting that in two years, the sum of their ages is (2x + 2) + (x + 2) = 28.\nThen we add all the values together, 3x + 4 = 28.\nAnd we subtract 4 from each side, 3x + 4 - 4 = 28 – 4.\nThen we divide each side by 3, x = 24/3.\nWe find that x = 8, or Brooke’s age is 8.\nSeth is twice as old as Brooke, so Seth is 8 x 2 = 16.\n#### 16"", ""original_answer"": 16.0, ""transformed_answer"": 14.67, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references \""in two years\"" in the question as \""in three years\"" wrongly, and calculates (2x + 3) + (x + 3) = 28 instead of (2x + 2) + (x + 2) = 28. Its subsequent related steps will be affected by the erroneous result of this step but still be calculated correctly. Therefore, step 2 changes to 3x + 6 = 28, step 3 changes to 3x + 6 - 6 = 28 – 6, step 4 changes to x = 22/3, step 5 changes to x = 22/3, and step 6 changes to Seth’s age being approximately 7.33 x 2 = 14.67. Please note that you should only consider one error, so the subsequent related steps of this erroneous step will only inherit its erroneous result and be calculated correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:5f9720ad4090acd3,full,calculation_error,"a salesmanâ€™s terms were changed from a flat commission of 5% on all his sales to a fixed salary of Rs. 1000 plus 2.5% commission on all sales exceeding Rs. 4,000. If his remuneration as per the new scheme was Rs. 600 more than that by the previous scheme, his sales were worth","Let x be the total sales worth
Previous remuneration = 0.05x
New remuneration = 1000 + 0.025(x-4000)
New remuneration - Previous remuneration = 600
1000 + 0.025(x-4000) - 0.05x = 600
1000 - 100 + 0.025x - 0.05x = 600
0.025x - 0.05x = 600 - 800
-0.025x = -200
x = 8000
#### 8000",Makes an arithmetic error when rearranging constant terms.,0.025x - 0.05x = 600 - 800,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,terms,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let x be the total sales worth\nPrevious remuneration = 0.05x\nNew remuneration = 1000 + 0.025(x-4000)\nNew remuneration - Previous remuneration = 600\n1000 + 0.025(x-4000) - 0.05x = 600\n1000 - 100 + 0.025x - 0.05x = 600\n0.025x - 0.05x = 600 - 900\n-0.025x = -300\nx = 12000\n#### 12000"", ""original_answer"": 12000, ""transformed_answer"": 8000, ""wrong_step"": 6, ""explanation"": ""In the transformed_solution, a miscalculation is introduced in step 6 where the correct operation 1000-100 is mistakenly changed to yield 600 - 800 instead of 600 - 900. This intentional error propagates down to step 7, changing the equation to -0.025x = -200 from the correct -0.025x = -300, which in turn affects the final step where the sales worth is erroneously found to be Rs. 8000 instead of Rs. 12000. Steps prior to the wrong step and unrelated to the wrong calculation remain unchanged."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:5fae6461e9b80143,full,operator_error,"The number of Christmas presents David found was twice as many as the number of birthday presents. If David found a total of 60 Christmas presents, how many presents did he get all together?","The number of birthday presents that David found is 60*2 = 120
For his birthday and Christmas, David found a total of 120+60 = 180 presents.
#### 180",Uses multiplication instead of division to find a quantity that is twice another,The number of birthday presents that David found is 60*2 = 120,ok,ok,,12,False,False,False,twice,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The number of birthday presents that David found is 60/2 = 30\nFor his birthday and Christmas, David found a total of 30+60 = 90 presents.\n#### 90"", ""original_answer"": 90.0, ""transformed_answer"": 180.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""This example replaces the division operator in step 1 with a multiplication operator, changing the original operation from 60/2 = 30 to an incorrect operation 60*2 = 120. The result of this incorrect step affects the subsequent step 2, so the calculation changes from 30+60 = 90 to 120+60 = 180. Even though step 2 correctly adds up the numbers, the premise is flawed due to the earlier operator error. Therefore, the transformed solution yields an incorrect total number of presents, 180, which is different from the correct answer of 90."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:5fc52c0c71230394,full,adding_irrelevant_information,Jessa needs to make cupcakes for 3 fourth-grade classes that each have 30 students and a P.E. class with 50 students. How many cupcakes does she need to make?,"For the fourth-grade classes, Jessa needs 3 classes * 30 cupcakes/class = 90 cupcakes
Adding the cupcakes for the P.E. class 50 plus 5 extra cupcakes for teachers, she needs to make a total of 90 cupcakes + 50 cupcakes + 5 cupcakes = 145 cupcakes
#### 145",Adds an irrelevant extra quantity to the required total,Adding the cupcakes for the P.E. class 50 plus 5 extra cupcakes for teachers,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""For the fourth-grade classes, Jessa needs 3 classes * 30 cupcakes/class = 90 cupcakes\nAdding the cupcakes for the P.E. class 50, she needs to make a total of 90 cupcakes + 50 cupcakes = 140 cupcakes\n#### 140"", ""original_answer"": 140.0, ""transformed_answer"": 145.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <plus 5 extra cupcakes for teachers>, resulting in step 2 changing from 90 cupcakes + 50 cupcakes = 140 cupcakes in the original_solution to 90 cupcakes + 50 cupcakes + 5 cupcakes = 145 cupcakes in the transformed_solution. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:5fe9b5ea1a68e78d,full,missing_step,Two trains are moving in the same direction at 162 kmph and 18 kmph. The faster train crosses a man in the slower train in 33 seconds. Find the length of the faster train?,"The distance covered in 33 seconds at the speed of 162 kmph is 162 x 33 x (5/18) = 1485 meters
The length of the faster train is 1485 meters
#### 1485",Uses absolute speed instead of relative speed between the trains,The distance covered in 33 seconds at the speed of 162 kmph is 162 x 33 x (5/18) = 1485 meters,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,trains,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The relative speed of the two trains is 162 - 18 = 144 kmph\nThe distance covered in 33 seconds at this speed is 144 x 33 x (5/18) = 1320 meters\nThe length of the faster train is 1320 meters\n#### 1320"", ""original_answer"": 1320.0, ""transformed_answer"": 1485.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, the first step has been removed, which calculates the relative speed of the two trains. Since step 1 references the two speeds from the question and calculates their difference, the largest number should be used for the subsequent related steps to reference. After deletion, step 1 of the transformed solution directly calculates the distance using the speed of the faster train, changing the formula from 144 x 33 x (5/18) to 162 x 33 x (5/18) and getting a different result 1485 meters. The language in step 1 also changes from <The relative speed of the two trains is 162 - 18 = 144 kmph> to <The distance covered in 33 seconds at the speed of 162 kmph is>. Step 2 stays the same but now references the incorrect distance, hence the wrong_step is 1."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:6028dc4ca40b0e10,full,counting_error,"From Monday to Friday, Elle practices piano for 30 minutes. On Saturday, she practices piano three times as much as on a weekday. There is no practice on Sunday.  How many hours does Elle spend practicing piano each week?","From Monday to Friday, Elle practices 0.50 x 4 = 2 hours.
On Saturday, she practices 0.50 x 3 = 1.5 hours.
Each week, Elle practices piano for 2 + 1.5 = 3.5 hours.
#### 3.5",Counts only four weekdays instead of five,"From Monday to Friday, Elle practices 0.50 x 4 = 2 hours.",ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,,True,2,"{""original_solution"": ""From Monday to Friday, Elle practices 0.50 x 5 = 2.5 hours.\nOn Saturday, she practices 0.50 x 3 = 1.5 hours.\nEach week, Elle practices piano for 2.5 + 1.5 = 4 hours.\n#### 4"", ""original_answer"": 4.0, ""transformed_answer"": 3.5, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 erroneously counts Monday to Friday as 4 days instead of 5 days, resulting in the calculation of 0.50 x 5=2.5 hours becoming 0.50 x 4=2 hours. And it affects step 3, changing it from 2.5 + 1.5 = 4 hours to 2 + 1.5 = 3.5 hours. It should be noted that you only need to consider one step of counting error; the other calculation processes related to this error are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:604a0299c3ed204c,full,operator_error,"Josh wants to build a square sandbox that is 3 ft long, 3 ft wide for his son. He can buy sand in 3 sq ft bags for $4.00 a bag. How much will it cost him to fill up the sandbox?","The sandbox is 3' long, 3' wide so the sq footage of the sandbox is 3*3 = 9 sq ft
He needs 9 sq ft of sand to fill the sandbox and he can buy it in 3 sq ft bags so he needs 9*3 = 27 bags of sand
The sand costs $4.00 a bag and he needs 27 bags so it will cost him 4*27 = $108.00 in sand
#### 108",Multiplies instead of dividing to find the number of bags,he needs 9*3 = 27 bags of sand,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,bags,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The sandbox is 3' long, 3' wide so the sq footage of the sandbox is 3*3 = 9 sq ft\nHe needs 9 sq ft of sand to fill the sandbox and he can buy it in 3 sq ft bags so he needs 9/3 = 3 bags of sand\nThe sand costs $4.00 a bag and he needs 3 bags so it will cost him 4*3 = $12.00 in sand\n#### 12"", ""original_answer"": 12.0, ""transformed_answer"": 108.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""This example turns the division in step 2 into multiplication, and by changing / to *, 9/3 = 3 becomes 9*3 = 27. This affects the subsequent related step 3, where the calculation changes from 4*3 = $12.00 to 4*27 = $108.00. It should be noted that only the operator in step 2 is incorrect, but its calculation is correct, which leads to its result being incorrect. This affects the subsequent step of using its result, but the calculation in step 3 is correct based on the incorrect step 2 result."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:60a822942ac81022,full,counting_error,average expenditure of a person for the first 3 days of a week is rs . 350 and for the next 4 days is rs . 420 . average expenditure of the man for the whole week is :,"For the first 2 days, the total expenditure is 2 * 350 = 700 rs
For the next 4 days, the total expenditure is 4 * 420 = 1680 rs
The total expenditure for the whole week is 700 + 1680 = 2380 rs
The average expenditure for the whole week is 2380 / 7 = 340 rs
#### 340",Uses the wrong count for one interval when calculating its total,"For the first 2 days, the total expenditure is 2 * 350 = 700 rs",ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""For the first 3 days, the total expenditure is 3 * 350 = 1050 rs\nFor the next 4 days, the total expenditure is 4 * 420 = 1680 rs\nThe total expenditure for the whole week is 1050 + 1680 = 2730 rs\nThe average expenditure for the whole week is 2730 / 7 = 390 rs\n#### 390"", ""original_answer"": 390, ""transformed_answer"": 340, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 mistakenly counts the first 3 days as 2 days when calculating the total for the first part of the week, resulting in 3 * 350=1050 becoming 2 * 350=700. Subsequent steps related to this counting error also change accordingly, while steps not affected by this counting error remain correct. Step 3 changes from 1050 + 1680=2730 to 700 + 1680=2380, and step 4 changes from 2730 / 7=390 to 2380 / 7=340."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:60f8c4e11d29daf4,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"Radhika got a new gaming system and asked everyone to get her games as gifts to add to the games she already has that she bought with her gaming system. On Christmas, she is given 12 new video games. On her birthday in February, she is given 8 more video games. Radhika already owned 1/2 the number of games as what she was given on Christmas and her birthday. How many video games does she own now?","Radhika was given 12 video games on Christmas and 8 video games on her birthday, so she was given 12 + 8 = 20 video games.
She had 1/2 this many before she was given gifts, 20 / 2 = 10 video games she already owned.
She was given 20 video games and had 10 already, 20 + 15 = 35 video games.
Now Radhika has 35 video games.
#### 35",Uses an incorrect value from a previous step in the final calculation,"She was given 20 video games and had 10 already, 20 + 15 = 35 video games.",ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""Radhika was given 12 video games on Christmas and 8 video games on her birthday, so she was given 12 + 8 = 20 video games.\nShe had 1/2 this many before she was given gifts, 20 / 2 = 10 video games she already owned.\nShe was given 20 video games and had 10 already, 20 + 10 = 30 video games.\nNow Radhika has 30 video games.\n#### 30"", ""original_answer"": 30.0, ""transformed_answer"": 35.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 mistakenly references the correct calculation of games already owned as 10 from step 2, but then incorrectly adds 15 instead of 10 to the number of games given, changing the correct addition of 20 + 10 to 20 + 15. The correct number of games Radhika already owned, which is 10, remains unchanged in the transformed_solution, as required. However, the referencing error only occurs in the third step when adding to the total number of video games owned. Subsequent calculations are based on the erroneous result of this step yet are still correctly computed in line with their respective new formulas. Therefore, step 3 introduces the single error in the transformed_solution with the transformed_answer differing from the original_answer as a result."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:610797f8950ddcf5,full,missing_step,There is a playground that is surrounded by a square fence that has a side length of 27 yards. There is a 12 yard by 9 yard garden that has fencing around it. How many yards of fencing do the playground and garden have together?,"The square fence around the playground is 27 * 4 = 108 yards
The fencing for the garden is 2 * (27) = 54 yards instead of 2 * (12 + 9)
The playground and garden have a combined fence length of 108 + 54 = 162 yards.
#### 162",Uses the square's side length when calculating the rectangle's perimeter,The fencing for the garden is 2 * (27) = 54 yards instead of 2 * (12 + 9),ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,side,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""27 * 4 = 108 yards\n2 * (12 + 9) = 42 yards\n108 + 42 = 150 yards\nThe playground and garden have a combined fence length of 150 yards.\n#### 150"", ""original_answer"": 150.0, ""transformed_answer"": 162.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""This example removes intermediate step 2, which calculates the fence length of the garden correctly as 2 * (12 + 9) = 42 yards. After removing this step, the next step, which should calculate the combined fence length, should reference the result from the previous step. However, the transformed solution incorrectly references the largest side length from the question, which is 27, assuming that one side of the garden shares the same length as the playground's side length. It makes an error by incorrectly calculating the garden's fence length as 2 * (27) = 54 yards. Subsequently, the calculation for the combined length becomes 108 + 54 = 162 yards. The wrong_step is 2, which serves as the first step that references the incorrect assumption about the garden fence length in the transformed solution."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:6149f2a18a874ea8,full,missing_step,"Johnny is an engineer designing a bridge. The roadway deck for the top of the bridge needs 1600 tons of concrete to construct. The two bridge anchors need equal amounts of concrete, but only one has been built so far, using 700 tons of concrete. Johnny had to calculate how many tons of concrete the supporting pillars underneath the bridge would need, and he found that the total amount of concrete in the entire bridge will be 4800 tons. How much concrete did Johnny find he needs for the supporting pillars?","The roadway deck and bridge anchors need 1600 + 700 = 2300 tons of concrete.
Thus, Johnny found the supporting pillars need 4800 - 2300 = 2500 tons of concrete.
#### 2,500",Fails to double a quantity when two equal parts are required,The roadway deck and bridge anchors need 1600 + 700 = 2300 tons of concrete.,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,equal,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The bridge anchors will take 700 * 2 = 1400 tons of concrete.\nThe roadway deck and bridge anchors need 1600 + 1400 = 3000 tons of concrete.\nThus, Johnny found the supporting pillars need 4800 - 3000 = 1800 tons of concrete.\n#### 1,800"", ""original_answer"": 1800.0, ""transformed_answer"": 2500.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""In the transformation, step 1 from the original_solution has been removed, which originally calculated the total concrete needed for both bridge anchors. The subsequent steps that relied on the result of this deleted step have been modified to reference the concrete of only one anchor (700 tons) from the question. Step 2 now adds together 1600 tons (roadway deck) and 700 tons (one bridge anchor already built) to get 2300 tons, as the deleted step 1 had originally referenced 1400 tons for both bridge anchors. In the transformed_solution, step 2, which referenced 1400 from the deleted step 1, has changed to reference the 700 tons of one anchor, causing the calculation to change from 1600 + 1400 = 3000 to 1600 + 700 = 2300. This causes an error in calculating the total amount of concrete for roadway deck and bridge anchors, and affects step 3, where the total tonnage for the supporting pillars is now calculated as 4800 - 2300 = 2500 tons. The wrong_step in the transformed_solution is 1, as it is the first step that references the incorrect tonnage due to the deletion of the initial step."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:616706e3c5d13a6e,full,missing_step,"Frank went to a shop to buy some food for his breakfast. He bought 10 buns for $0.1 each, two bottles of milk, for $2 each, and a carton of eggs, which was three times more expensive than one bottle of milk. How much did Frank pay for his breakfast shopping?","He bought two bottles of milk, for $2 each, so he paid for them 2 * 2 = $4.
One carton of eggs was three times more expensive than one bottle of milk, so it cost 3 * 2 = $6.
So for all his shopping Frank had to pay 10 * 0.1 + 4 + 6 = $15.
#### 15",Makes an arithmetic error when adding the component costs,10 * 0.1 + 4 + 6 = $15.,label_conflict,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""For the buns, Frank paid 10 * 0.1 = $1.\nHe bought two bottles of milk, for $2 each, so he paid for them 2 * 2 = $4.\nOne carton of eggs was three times more expensive than one bottle of milk, so it cost 3 * 2 = $6.\nSo for all his shopping Frank had to pay 1 + 4 + 6 = $11.\n#### 11"", ""original_answer"": 11.0, ""transformed_answer"": 15.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""In this example, the first step has been removed. In the original_solution, step 1 calculates the cost for buns and steps 3 and 4 reference the result of $1 from step 1. After removing step 1, the cost of buns is no longer calculated separately, so the final calculation step which was step 4 in the original solution, now becomes step 3 in the transformed solution and now directly references the cost for 10 buns with the formula 10 * 0.1 instead of the previously calculated $1, which results in 10 * 0.1 + 4 + 6 = $15. Since the transformed_solution's step 3 is the first instance where the formula was adjusted to include the omitted step's calculation, the wrong_step is designated as 3."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:6199846e9bff793e,full,calculation_error,"Daniela goes shopping during a sale. She finds out that the store has 40 percent off on shoes and 20 percent off on dresses. If Daniela buys 2 pairs of shoes originally priced at $50 a pair and a dress originally priced at $100, how much money does she spend?","Two pairs of shoes at $50 a pair costs $50 x 2 = $100.
The shoes are discounted by 40 percent so she only pays 100 - 40 = 60 percent of the original price.
Therefore the two pairs of shoes cost $100 x 0.60 = $70.
The dress is discounted by 20 percent so she only pays 100 - 20 = 80 percent of the original price.
Therefore the dress costs $100 x 0.80 = $80.
In total Daniela spends $70 + $80 = $150 for two pairs of shoes and a dress.
#### 150",Miscomputes a decimal multiplication,Therefore the two pairs of shoes cost $100 x 0.60 = $70.,ok,ok,,3,True,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Two pairs of shoes at $50 a pair costs $50 x 2 = $100.\nThe shoes are discounted by 40 percent so she only pays 100 - 40 = 60 percent of the original price.\nTherefore the two pairs of shoes cost $100 x 0.60 = $60.\nThe dress is discounted by 20 percent so she only pays 100 - 20 = 80 percent of the original price.\nTherefore the dress costs $100 x 0.80 = $80.\nIn total Daniela spends $60 + $80 = $140 for two pairs of shoes and a dress.\n#### 140"", ""original_answer"": 140.0, ""transformed_answer"": 150.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""The example mistakenly calculates the 40 percent discounted price of shoes in step 3 as $100 x 0.60 = $70 instead of the correct calculation of $100 x 0.60 = $60. This changes the answer in step 6 to $60 (incorrectly written as $70) + $80 = $150 instead of the correct $60 + $80 = $140. The steps before the wrong step (step 3) are unaffected, as are the constants and the calculation in step 4 for the dress's price. The altered calculation in step 3 affects the total in the final step (step 6)."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:61d19801c90a9b91,full,counting_error,"A factory produces 4560 toys per week. If the workers at this factory work 4 days a week and if these workers make the same number of toys everyday, how many toys are produced each day?","The total number of toys produced per week is 4560
