#### 6",Uses the number of packages instead of calculating the total number of cars,Tom gave 10 x 1/5 = 2 miniature racing cars,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,"cars, packages",True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Tom had 10 x 5 = 50 miniature racing cars.\nTom gave 50 x 1/5 = 10 miniature racing cars to each of his two nephews.\nSo, he gave a total of 10 x 2 = 20 cars to his two nephews.\nTherefore, Tom is left with 50 - 20 = 30 miniature racing cars.\n#### 30"", ""original_answer"": 30.0, ""transformed_answer"": 6.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""The intermediate step 1 of the original solution was removed. That step calculates the total number of miniature racing cars. After deleting it, step 2 should now reference the number of packages directly, but instead of using the correct total calculated earlier (50), it incorrectly assumes that each package has a quantity of 1 car (which is the largest number in the question referenced in step 1). As a result, this changes the operation from 50 x 1/5 to 10 x 1/5, which results in an incorrect calculation of the cars given to a nephew (2 instead of 10). Then, the next steps that depended on step 2 are also changed to maintain a logical (but incorrect) flow, adjusting the number of cars given away to 4 and finally concluding that Tom has 6 cars left. It is step 1 in the transformed_solution, which is also step 2 in the original_solution, that serves as the first step referencing an incorrect number in the transformed_solution, thus the wrong_step is 1."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:6ec945556f83b353,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,Tom decides to make lasagna with all his beef. It takes twice as many noodles as beef. He has 10 pounds of beef. He already has 4 pounds of lasagna noodles and the noodles come in 2-pound packages. How many packages does he need to buy?,"He needs 10*2=20 pounds of noodles
