#### 56",Uses the wrong base amount when calculating an increased quantity,"For the second month, Sam earned $210 + $150 = $360.",ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""For the second month, Sam earned $200 + $150 = $350.\nSo, he earned a total of $200 +$350 = $550 for the first two months.\nTherefore, he spent $550/$10 = 55 hours on math tutoring for the first two months\n#### 55"", ""original_answer"": 55, ""transformed_answer"": 56, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references 200 in the question as 210 wrongly, and calculates $210 + $150 = $360 instead of $200 + $150 = $350. Its subsequent related steps, step 2 and 3, will be affected. Step 2 changes $200 +$360 = $560 to $200 +$360 = $560, and step 3 changes $550/$10 = <<550/10=55>>55 to $560/$10 = <<560/10=56>>56. Please note that you should only consider one error, so step 3 and step 4 still reference 550 in the question correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:790bf802fc385daa,full,referencing_context_value_error,"Dallas and Austin went to the pick-your-own orchard. Dallas picked 14 bags of apples and 9 bags of pears. Austin picked 6 bags of apples more than Dallas, and 5 fewer bags of pears than Dallas. How many bags of fruit did Austin pick, in total?","He picked 10+6=16 bags of apples.
