eic,eic:7910ef3419cd84dd,full,confusing_formula_error,A window is made up of 8 glass panes. Each pane has a length of 12 inches and a width of 8 inches. What is the area of the window?,"The area of a glass pane is 8 + 12 + 8 + 12 = 40 sq in
The area of the window is 40 x 8 = 320 sq in
#### 320",Uses perimeter instead of multiplying length by width to find area,The area of a glass pane is 8 + 12 + 8 + 12 = 40 sq in,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,width,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The area of a glass pane is 8 x 12 = 96 sq in\nThe area of the window is 96 x 8 = 768 sq in\n#### 768"", ""original_answer"": 768.0, ""transformed_answer"": 320.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 confuses the perimeter and area of a rectangle. The question requires that the area of the rectangle to be solved be equal to the length multiplied by width, rather than the sum of all sides, which is equivalent to the perimeter of the rectangle, resulting in 8 x 12 = 96 in the original_solution being changed to 8 + 12 + 8 + 12 = 40 in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent step 2, changing from 96 x 8 = 768 to 40 x 8 = 320. Note that the language description of step 1 < The area of a glass pane is > in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that you need to only consider one step of confusing formula error, and the other calculation processes are still correct. In this example, only step 1 mistakenly uses the formula for the rectangular perimeter, while the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:7935d1efb9de9d45,full,operator_error,"In a certain accounting class of 90 students, 70% of the students took the final exam on the assigned day while the rest of the students took the exam on a make-up date. If the students on the assigned day had an average score of 60%, and the students on the make-up date had an average score of 70%, what was the average score for the entire class?","Students who took the exam on the assigned day: 90 x 70% = 63
Students who took the exam on the make-up date: 90 + 63 = 153
Total score for students who took the exam on the assigned day: 63 x 60% = 37.8
Total score for students who took the exam on the make-up date: 153 x 70% = 107.1
Total score for the entire class: 37.8 + 107.1 = 144.9
Average score for the entire class: 144.9 / 90 = 1.61
Average score for the entire class as a percentage: 161%
#### 161%",Adds a part to the total instead of finding the remaining part by subtraction,Students who took the exam on the make-up date: 90 + 63 = 153,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Students who took the exam on the assigned day: 90 x 70% = 63\nStudents who took the exam on the make-up date: 90 - 63 = 27\nTotal score for students who took the exam on the assigned day: 63 x 60% = 37.8\nTotal score for students who took the exam on the make-up date: 27 x 70% = 18.9\nTotal score for the entire class: 37.8 + 18.9 = 56.7\nAverage score for the entire class: 56.7 / 90 = 0.63\nAverage score for the entire class as a percentage: 63%\n#### 63%"", ""original_answer"": ""63%"", ""transformed_answer"": ""161%"", ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""This example changes the subtraction in step 2 to addition. Changing from '90 - 63 = 27' to '90 + 63 = 153' creates an incorrect number of students who took the make-up exam, leading to a cascade of incorrect computations in the further steps. The resulting numbers in steps 4, 5, 6, and 7 are based on the wrong number of make-up exam students but are computed correctly given that incorrect assumption. Ultimately, the transformed answer is nonsensical as it suggests an average score over 100%."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:793c21037f97faae,full,calculation_error,28 buckets of water fill a tank when the capacity of each bucket is 13.5 litres. How many buckets will be required to fill the same tank if the capacity of each bucket is 9 litres?,"The total capacity filled by 28 buckets = 28 x 13.5 = 385 litres
The capacity of each bucket to fill the same tank = 9 litres
Number of buckets required = 385 / 9 = 42.77
#### 42.77",Makes an arithmetic error when multiplying the given quantities,28 x 13.5 = 385 litres,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,when,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The total capacity filled by 28 buckets = 28 x 13.5 = 378 litres\nThe capacity of each bucket to fill the same tank = 9 litres\nNumber of buckets required = 378 / 9 = 42\n#### 42"", ""original_answer"": 42.0, ""transformed_answer"": 42.77, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""In this example, the calculation in step 1 (28 x 13.5) has been incorrectly modified to equal 385 instead of the correct 378. This introduces a calculation error that carries through to step 3, where the number of buckets required to fill the same tank is now based on the incorrect total capacity of 385 litres. Note that we divide 385 by 9 to get an incorrect transformed_answer of 42.77 buckets, which maintains the correct mathematical operation but with an incorrect value inherited from step 1. Steps that do not depend on the miscalculated total capacity remain unaffected."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:7945d7b372c87fbc,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"James runs a TV show and there are 5 main characters and 4 minor characters. He pays the minor characters $15,000 each episode. He paid the major characters three times as much. How much does he pay per episode?","He pays the minor characters 15000*4=$60,000
The major characters each get 15000*3=$45,000
So he pays the main characters 45,000*5=$225,000
So in total he pays 225,000+50,000=$275,000
#### 275,000",Uses an incorrect previous total in the final addition,"So in total he pays 225,000+50,000=$275,000",ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""He pays the minor characters 15000*4=$60,000\nThe major characters each get 15000*3=$45,000\nSo he pays the main characters 45,000*5=$225,000\nSo in total he pays 225,000+60,000=$285,000\n#### 285,000"", ""original_answer"": 285000.0, ""transformed_answer"": 275000.0, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""Step 4 mistakenly references 60,000 as 50,000 from step 1 and changes 225,000+60,000=$285,000 to 225,000+50,000=$275,000. It should be noted that step 1 in the transformed_solution <He pays the minor characters 15000*4=$60,000> should be the same as the step 1 in the original_solution <He pays the minor characters 15000*4=$60,000> and calculates its result 60,000 correctly. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of all steps is still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:794d279e650de336,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"John jogs at a speed of 4 miles per hour when he runs alone, but runs at 6 miles per hour when he is being dragged by his 100-pound German Shepherd dog. If John and his dog go on a run together for 30 minutes, and then John runs for an additional 30 minutes by himself, how far will John have traveled?","John runs at 6 miles per hour for 0.5 hours, for a distance of 6*0.5=3 miles.
Then John runs at 4 miles per hour for 0.5 hours, for a distance of 4*0.5=2 miles.
In total, John runs 3+3=6 miles.
#### 6",Uses a previous result instead of the newly calculated value when adding totals,"In total, John runs 3+3=6 miles.",ok,ok,,12,False,False,False,when,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""John runs at 6 miles per hour for 0.5 hours, for a distance of 6*0.5=3 miles.\nThen John runs at 4 miles per hour for 0.5 hours, for a distance of 4*0.5=2 miles.\nIn total, John runs 3+2=5 miles.\n#### 5"", ""original_answer"": 5.0, ""transformed_answer"": 6.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 mistakenly references 2 as 3 from step 2 and changes 3+2=5 to 3+3=6. It should be noted that step 2 in the transformed_solution <Then John runs at 4 miles per hour for 0.5 hours, for a distance of 4*0.5=2 miles.> should be the same as the step 2 in the original_solution <Then John runs at 4 miles per hour for 0.5 hours, for a distance of 4*0.5=2 miles.> and calculates its result 2 correctly. Furthermore, step 3 is the only step with an error, and the values calculated in step 1 and step 2 should not be altered. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of step 1 and step 2 is still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:7952f5de1aa36daa,full,calculation_error,"A cheetah can run at a top speed of 60 mph. The gazelle can run for speeds of up to 40 miles per hour. If one mile per hour is about 1.5 feet per second, then how many seconds would it take for a cheetah traveling at top speed to catch up to a fleeing gazelle also running at top speed if the two animals were initially 210 feet apart and they both traveled in the same direction?","60 mph is 60*1.5=90 feet per second.
40 mph is 40*1.5=60 feet per second.
Thus, for every second, the cheetah advances on the gazelle by 90-60=30 feet per second.
To close on a distance of 210 feet, it would take the cheetah 210/20=10.5 seconds to catch the gazelle.
#### 10.5",Divides the initial distance by the wrong relative speed,it would take the cheetah 210/20=10.5 seconds,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""60 mph is 60*1.5=90 feet per second.\n40 mph is 40*1.5=60 feet per second.\nThus, for every second, the cheetah advances on the gazelle by 90-60=30 feet per second.\nTo close on a distance of 200 feet, it would take the cheetah 210/30=7 seconds to catch the gazelle.\n#### 7"", ""original_answer"": 7.0, ""transformed_answer"": 10.5, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""In this example, the calculation of 210/30 in step 4 is erroneously modified to 210/20, resulting in an incorrect answer of 10.5 seconds instead of the correct 7 seconds. No other steps are affected by this error, because it's the final step in the process and doesn't influence any calculations before it."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:7986fa9646081810,full,referencing_context_value_error,"in a mixture 60 litres, the ratio of milk and water 2 : 1 . if the this ratio is to be 1 : 2, then the quantity of water to be further added is","The current quantity of milk in the mixture is 2/(2+1) * 60 = 40 liters
The current quantity of water in the mixture is 1/(2+1) * 50 = 16.67 liters
Let the quantity of water to be further added be x
The new total quantity of water in the mixture will be 16.67 + x
The new total quantity of milk in the mixture will be 40 liters
The new ratio of milk and water is 40 : (16.67 + x), and as per the given ratio, we have 40/(16.67 + x) = 1/2
40 = (16.67 + x)/2
80 = 16.67 + x
63.33 = x
#### 63.33",Uses the wrong total quantity when calculating a ratio component,The current quantity of water in the mixture is 1/(2+1) * 50 = 16.67 liters,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The current quantity of milk in the mixture is 2/(2+1) * 60 = 40 liters\nThe current quantity of water in the mixture is 1/(2+1) * 60 = 20 liters\nLet the quantity of water to be further added be x\nThe new total quantity of water in the mixture will be 20 + x\nThe new total quantity of milk in the mixture will be 40 liters\nThe new ratio of milk and water is 40 : (20 + x), and as per the given ratio, we have 40/(20 + x) = 1/2\n40 = (20 + x)/2\n80 = 20 + x\n60 = x\n#### 60"", ""original_answer"": 60.0, ""transformed_answer"": 63.33, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 references 60 in the question as 50 wrongly, and calculates 1/(2+1) * 50 = 16.67 liters instead of 1/(2+1) * 60 = 20 liters. Its subsequent related steps, step 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, and 9, will be affected. Step 4 changes the new total quantity of water in the mixture from 20 + x to 16.67 + x. Step 6 calculates the new ratio of milk and water as 40 : (16.67 + x). Step 7 establishes the incorrect equality 40/(16.67 + x) = 1/2. Step 8 then multiplies both sides by 2 and gets 80 = 16.67 + x. Step 9 subtracts 16.67 from 80 and obtains x = 63.33. Please note that you should only consider one error, so the subsequent related steps of this erroneous step will only inherit its erroneous result and be calculated correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:79a8c4e37a01f7fa,full,counting_error,Suzie and 5 of her friends decide to rent an Airbnb at Lake Tahoe for 4 days from Thursday to Sunday. The rental rate for weekdays is $420 per day. The weekend rental rate is $540 per day. They all decide to split the rental evenly. How much does each person have to pay?,"The cost of the rental for Thursday and Friday comes to $420 * 2 = $840.
The cost of the rental for Saturday and Sunday comes to $540 * 1 = $540.
The total cost of the rental comes to $840 + 540 = $1380.
The payment required for each person comes to $1380 / 6 = $230.
#### 230",Counts only one day at the weekend rate instead of both weekend days,The cost of the rental for Saturday and Sunday comes to $540 * 1 = $540.,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,weekend,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The cost of the rental for Thursday and Friday comes to $420 * 2 = $840.\nThe cost of the rental for Saturday and Sunday comes to $540 * 2 = $1080.\nThe total cost of the rental comes to $840 + 1080 = $1920.\nThe payment required for each person comes to $1920 / 6 = $320.\n#### 320"", ""original_answer"": 320.0, ""transformed_answer"": 230.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 erroneously counts the weekend, Saturday and Sunday, as 1 day instead of 2 days, resulting in the original calculation of $540 * 2 = $1080 being changed to $540 * 1 = $540 in the transformed_solution. The subsequent related step is affected, with step 3 changing from $840 + 1080 = $1920 to $840 + 540 = $1380, and step 4 changing from $1920 / 6 = $320 to $1380 / 6 = $230. It should be noted that only one step of counting error is considered, and the rest of the calculation is still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:79b803506d9bd66d,full,operator_error,"A house’s living room, dining room and kitchen take up 1,000 sq ft. The house also has a guest bedroom and a master bedroom suite. If the guest bedroom is a quarter of the size of the master bedroom suite, and the house is 2,300 sq ft total, how large is the master bedroom suite?","The house is 2,300 sq ft and we know the non-bedroom sections take up 1,000 sq ft, so that leaves 2300*1000 = 2,300,000 sq ft for the bedrooms/bathrooms
Let x be the size of the master bedroom suite.
Then the guest bedroom is 0.25x.
So together the bedrooms are x + 0.25x = 1.25x in size.
This means 1.25x = 2300000
So x = 2300000 / 1.25 = 1840000 sq ft
#### 1840000",Uses multiplication instead of subtraction to find the remaining area,"that leaves 2300*1000 = 2,300,000 sq ft",ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The house is 2,300 sq ft and we know the non-bedroom sections take up 1,000 sq ft, so that leaves 2300-1000 = 1,300 sq ft for the bedrooms/bathrooms\nLet x be the size of the master bedroom suite.\nThen the guest bedroom is 0.25x.\nSo together the bedrooms are x + 0.25x = 1.25x in size.\nThis means 1.25x = 1300\nSo x = 1300 / 1.25 = 1040 sq ft\n#### 1040"", ""original_answer"": 1040.0, ""transformed_answer"": 1840000.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""This example changes the subtraction in step 1 to multiplication, incorrectly computing the remaining square footage for bedrooms/bathrooms as 2300*1000 instead of 2300-1000. The original step is meant to find the remaining area after non-bedroom space is accounted for, but instead, it incorrectly calculates a product. The result of this error is propagated through the rest of the solution, affecting the calculation of the master bedroom suite's size, but leaving the structure and other arithmetic in later steps correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:79c3b758c873ae8f,full,confusing_formula_error,If the perimeter of square region q and the perimeter of rectangular region r are equal and the sides of r are in the ratio 2 : 3 then the ratio of the area of r to the area of q,"Let the side of the square be x.
The perimeter of q is 4x.
Let the sides of rectangular r be 2y and 3y.
The perimeter of r is 2(2y) + 2(3y) = 10y.
Equating the perimeters: 4x = 10y, so x = 5/2y.
The area of q is x + x + x + x = 4x.
The area of r is 2y * 3y = 6y^2.
The ratio of the area of r to q is 6y^2 / 4x = 6y^2 / 4(5/2y) = 6y^2 / (20/2y) = 6y^2 / 10y = 6/10y.
#### 6/10y",Uses the perimeter formula instead of the square’s area formula,The area of q is x + x + x + x = 4x.,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let the side of the square be x.\nThe perimeter of q is 4x.\nLet the sides of rectangular r be 2y and 3y.\nThe perimeter of r is 2(2y) + 2(3y) = 10y.\nEquating the perimeters: 4x = 10y, so x = 5/2y.\nThe area of q is x^2 = (5/2y)^2 = 25/4y^2.\nThe area of r is 2y * 3y = 6y^2.\nThe ratio of the area of r to q is 6y^2 / (25/4y^2) = 24y^4 / 25y^2 = 24/25y^2.\n#### 24/25y^2"", ""original_answer"": ""24/25y^2"", ""transformed_answer"": ""6/10y"", ""wrong_step"": 5, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 5 confuses the area of a square with its perimeter. The question requires that the area of the square be equal to the square of the side length, x^2, rather than the sum of all sides 4x, which is the perimeter of the square, resulting in x^2 in the original_solution becoming 4x in the transformed_solution. This affects the subsequent related step 7, changing the ratio calculation from the original 6y^2 / (25/4y^2) to 6y^2 / 4x. The language description of step 5 <The area of q is> in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. In this example, only step 5 mistakenly uses the formula of square area while the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:79c3e33eeb3b5575,full,referencing_context_value_error,"If it is assumed that 50 percent of those who receive a questionnaire by mail will respond and 300 responses are needed, what is the minimum number of questionnaires that should be mailed?","Let x be the number of questionnaires mailed.
0.60x = 300
