#### 30.67",Introduces an unjustified adjustment to the computed total,Assumed that the batsman had a penalty of 10 runs in one of the next 10 matches,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The total runs scored in 20 matches is 20 x 40 = 800\nThe total runs scored in the next 10 matches is 10 x 13 = 130\nThe total runs scored in all 30 matches is 800 + 130 = 930\nThe average runs in all 30 matches is 930 / 30 = 31\n#### 31"", ""original_answer"": 31.0, ""transformed_answer"": 30.67, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Assumed that the batsman had a penalty of 10 runs in one of the next 10 matches>, resulting in the change of the total runs scored in the next 10 matches from 130 to 120. This affects the subsequent calculation of total runs in all 30 matches and the final average. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:7c1f81d7ad351060,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"Farmer Steven needs to load his truck up with compost. His tractor can scoop up compost at a rate of 75 pounds per minute. Steven's son, Darrel, wants to help. Using a shovel, Darrel can scoop up 10 pounds per minute. How much time, in minutes, would it take for the two working together at the same time to load up 2550 pounds of compost into the truck?","Working together at the same time, the rate at which the two would be able to load compost would be 75+10=85 pounds/minute.
At 80 pounds/minute, the 2550 pounds could be loaded in 2550/80=31.875 minutes.
#### 31.875",Uses an incorrect rate in the final division after computing the combined rate,"At 80 pounds/minute, the 2550 pounds could be loaded in 2550/80=31.875 minutes.",ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Working together at the same time, the rate at which the two would be able to load compost would be 75+10=85 pounds/minute.\nAt 85 pounds/minute, the 2550 pounds could be loaded in 2550/85=30 minutes.\n#### 30"", ""original_answer"": 30.0, ""transformed_answer"": 31.875, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 mistakenly references 85 as 80 from step 1 and changes 2550/85=30 minutes to 2550/80=31.875 minutes. It should be noted that step 1 in the transformed_solution <Working together at the same time, the rate at which the two would be able to load compost would be 75+10=85 pounds/minute.> should be the same as the step 1 in the original_solution <Working together at the same time, the rate at which the two would be able to load compost would be 75+10=85 pounds/minute.> and calculates its result 85 correctly. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of all steps is still correct. The subsequent steps related to this certain step will be affected by the erroneous result of this certain step but still be calculated correctly. Since we are only considering one step error, all other steps that reference this result number still reference this number correctly, and the transformed answer is different from the original answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:7c629398c0ea8bfa,full,unit_conversion_error,a train is moving at 4/5 of its usual speed. the train is 30 minutes too late. what is the usual time (in hours) for the train to complete the journey?,"Let the usual speed be S and the usual time be T.
Then the distance traveled is the same, so D = ST.
When the train is moving at 4/5 of its usual speed, the time it takes is 5/4T.
So the equation becomes D = (4/5S)(5/4T) = ST.
The train is 30 minutes late, so the difference between the usual time and the time it took is 30 minutes.
So T - 5/4T = 30 minutes = 1/3 hour.
Therefore, 1/4T = 1/3, so T = 4/3 hours.
#### 1.33",Reverses the order when subtracting the longer time from the shorter time,So T - 5/4T = 30 minutes = 1/3 hour.,label_conflict,ok,,12,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let the usual speed be S and the usual time be T.\nThen the distance traveled is the same, so D = ST.\nWhen the train is moving at 4/5 of its usual speed, the time it takes is 5/4T.\nSo the equation becomes D = (4/5S)(5/4T) = ST.\nThe train is 30 minutes late, so the difference between the usual time and the time it took is 30 minutes.\nSo T - 5/4T = 30 minutes = 1/2 hour.\nTherefore, 1/4T = 1/2, so T = 2 hours.\n#### 2"", ""original_answer"": 2.0, ""transformed_answer"": 1.33, ""wrong_step"": 6, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 6 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means 30 minutes is wrongly equal to 1/3 hour instead of 1/2 hour, resulting in T - 5/4T = 30 minutes = 1/2 hour in the original_solution becoming T - 5/4T = 30 minutes = 1/3 hour in the transformed_solution. And it affects the subsequent related step 7, which changes from 1/4T = 1/2, so T = 2 hours to 1/4T = 1/3, so T = 4/3 hours (1.33 hours when converted to decimal). It should be noted that you only need to consider one step of unit conversion error, and the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:7c6df344acca5f6a,full,counting_error,"Gumball was counting his water intake for the previous week. He checked his list and saw that he had drank 60 liters of water for the week. He drank nine liters of water on Monday, Thursday and Saturday and 8 liters of water on Tuesday, Friday and Sunday. Unfortunately, no data was input on Wednesday. How many liters of water did he drink on Wednesday?","Gumball's total water intake on Monday, Thursday, and Saturday is 9 x 2 = 18 liters.
And his total water intake on Tuesday, Friday, and Sunday is 8 x 3 = 24 liters.
So his total water intake for 5 days is 24 + 18 = 42 liters.
Therefore Gumball's water intake on Wednesday is 60 - 42 = 18 liters.
#### 18",Miscounts repeated instances when multiplying a daily amount,"Gumball's total water intake on Monday, Thursday, and Saturday is 9 x 2 = 18 liters.",ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Gumball's total water intake on Monday, Thursday, and Saturday is 9 x 3 = 27 liters.\nAnd his total water intake on Tuesday, Friday, and Sunday is 8 x 3 = 24 liters.\nSo his total water intake for 6 days is 24 + 27 = 51 liters.\nTherefore Gumball's water intake on Wednesday is 60 - 51 = 9 liters.\n#### 9"", ""original_answer"": 9.0, ""transformed_answer"": 18.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 erroneously counts Monday, Thursday, and Saturday as 2 days instead of 3 days, resulting in 9 x 3 = 27 liters becoming 9 x 2 = 18 liters. This change affected the subsequent related calculations, with the total water intake for only 5 days calculated as 42 liters instead of 6 days for 51 liters, and the intake for Wednesday was incorrectly deduced to be 18 liters instead of 9 liters. The error was confined to the first step, and the subsequent subtraction step was correctly performed based on the erroneous total."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:7c707e51b349e67a,full,calculation_error,a sum fetched a total simple interest of rs . 2700 at the rate of 6 p . c . p . a . in 3 years . what is the sum ?,"Simple interest = (P * R * T) / 100
