#### 73",Adds an irrelevant quantity to the computed total,and the extra one makes a total of 22+13+11+26+1 = 73 pieces of candy,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Julie caught half the amount of Tabitha's 22 pieces so 22/2 = 11 pieces of candy caught by Julie\nCarlos caught twice the amount of candy as Stan's 13 pieces so 2 * 13 = 26 pieces of candy caught by Carlos\nAll together, Tabitha caught 22 pieces, Stan had 13, Julie had 11 and Carlos had 26, so there were a total of 22+13+11+26 = 72 pieces of candy\n#### 72"", ""original_answer"": 72.0, ""transformed_answer"": 73.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Because there was an extra candy found on the ground>, and this affects the final calculation, resulting in step 3 changing from a total of 22+13+11+26 = 72 pieces of candy in the original_solution to 22+13+11+26+1 = 73 pieces of candy in the transformed_solution. This is done by integrating the additional information into the existing step instead of adding it as a separate calculation step, and thus the transformed_answer differs from the original_answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:99e611036a83e97e,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"For a school fundraiser, Tory needs to sell 50 packs of cookies. So far, he has sold 12 packs to his grandmother, 7 packs to his uncle, and 5 packs to a neighbor. How many more packs of cookies does Tory need to sell?","Tory sold 12 packs + 7 packs = 19 packs to his family members.
