eic,eic:9ed1197ec627d216,full,operator_error,3 consecutive even numbers are such that thrice the first number exceeds double the third number by 7. What is the third number?,"Let the three consecutive even numbers be x, x+2, and x+4
3x = 2*(x+4) - 7
3x = 2x + 8 - 7
3x = 2x + 1
x = 1
The third number is x + 4 = 1 + 4 = 5
#### 5",Reverses the operation when translating “exceeds by” into an equation,3x = 2*(x+4) - 7,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,exceeds,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let the three consecutive even numbers be x, x+2, and x+4\n3x = 2(x+4) + 7\n3x = 2x + 8 + 7\n3x = 2x + 15\nx = 15\nThe third number is x + 4 = 15 + 4 = 19\n#### 19"", ""original_answer"": 19.0, ""transformed_answer"": 5.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""The example changes the operator from \""+\"" to \""-\"" in step 1 of the original solution, turning \""3x = 2(x+4) + 7\"" into \""3x = 2*(x+4) - 7\"". The subsequent steps are revised based on the result of this modification: step 2 changes from \""3x = 2x + 8 + 7\"" to \""3x = 2x + 8 - 7\"", and step 3 changes from \""3x = 2x + 15\"" to \""3x = 2x + 1\"". Thus, the solution steps onward reflect the incorrect alteration in step 1 and are performed with correct calculations, leading to an incorrect transformed_answer that is different from the original_answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""operator_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:9edd5edb52477954,full,calculation_error,"Iris’ family is planning a surprise birthday party for her. The party will include her 3 uncles and 4 aunts who have a son and daughter each as well as her brother and mother. In total, how many people are coming to Iris’ birthday party?","Each of her aunts and uncles have a family unit of 1 son + 1 daughter + 1 aunt/uncle = 4 people.
Iris has a total of 3 uncles + 4 aunts = 7 aunts or uncles in these family units.
So among her aunts, uncles, and cousins, there will be 7 family units * 4 people in each family unit = 28 people.
Including her mother and brother, there will be a total of 28 people + 1 mother + 1 brother = 30 people coming to her party.
#### 30",Incorrectly adds the components of each group,1 son + 1 daughter + 1 aunt/uncle = 4 people,ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,each,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Each of her aunts and uncles have a family unit of 1 son + 1 daughter + 1 aunt/uncle = 3 people.\nIris has a total of 3 uncles + 4 aunts = 7 aunts or uncles in these family units.\nSo among her aunts, uncles, and cousins, there will be 7 family units * 3 people in each family unit = 21 people.\nIncluding her mother and brother, there will be a total of 21 people + 1 mother + 1 brother = 23 people coming to her party.\n#### 23"", ""original_answer"": 23.0, ""transformed_answer"": 30.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""The transformed_solution introduces a calculation error at step 1 by mistakenly counting each family unit as having 4 people instead of 3. This error then changes the expected number of people from each family unit and alters all the subsequent steps that rely on this number. The original and unrelated steps (total number of aunts and uncles) remain correct while steps that involve calculations with the family unit size inherit this error."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:9eea8cbd9dc772d5,full,missing_step,"John used to be able to squat 135 pounds. After training, he increased that by 265 pounds. He then gets a magical strength-increasing bracer that increases his strength by an additional 600%. How much can he now lift?","The bracer adds 135*6=810 pounds
