eic,eic:b5ba6e4c097c893d,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"Hadley wore his cowboy boots everywhere. He walked 2 miles to the grocery store in his boots. Then he walked 1 less than two miles to the pet store in his boots. Then, he walked one less than four miles back home in his boots. How far, in miles, did Hadley walk in his boots?","After he walked 2 miles to the grocery store, he took a detour to see a friend which added another mile, so he walked one less than two miles to the pet store, adding 2-1+1=2 miles to his journey.
To complete his journey, he walked one less than four miles to return home, adding 4-1=3 miles to his journey.
In total, with the detour, Hadley walked 2+2+3=7 miles.
#### 7",Adds an unsupported extra quantity to the calculation,he took a detour to see a friend which added another mile,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,8,"{""original_solution"": ""After he walked 2 miles to the grocery store, he walked one less than two miles to the pet store, adding 2-1=1 mile to his journey.\nTo complete his journey, he walked one less than four miles to return home, adding 4-1=3 miles. to his journey.\nIn total, Hadley walked 2+1+3=6 miles.\n#### 6"", ""original_answer"": 6.0, ""transformed_answer"": 7.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <he took a detour to see a friend which added another mile>, resulting in step 1 changing from 2-1=1 mile in the original_solution to 2-1+1=2 miles in the transformed_solution. The added detour information has affected the total calculation, changing the final sum from 2+1+3=6 miles to 2+2+3=7 miles. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
