#### 35",Uses an unjustified value for the current speed in the equations.,"Then, speed downstream = (x + 30) kmph
Speed upstream = (x - 30) kmph",ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let the speed of the man in still water be x kmph.\nThen, speed downstream = (x + 20) kmph\nSpeed upstream = (x - 20) kmph\nAccording to the given data, x + 20 = 60 and x - 20 = 20\nSolving the equations we get, x = 40 kmph\n#### 40"", ""original_answer"": 40.0, ""transformed_answer"": 35.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 references the speeds incorrectly by adding and subtracting 30 instead of 20 to the speed of the man in still water, resulting in speed downstream = (x + 30) kmph and speed upstream = (x - 30) kmph. It should be x + 20 and x - 20 respectively to match the question’s given downstream and upstream speeds. The subsequent related equations and calculations will be affected by this erroneous step (steps 3 and 4), but these steps will still utilize the wrong values from step 2 to produce subsequent correct calculations based on the erroneous step, leading to x = 35 kmph instead of the correct x = 40 kmph. All other steps that reference the correct speed in the question still do so correctly except for the erroneous Step 2. The transformed answer is different from the original answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
