eic,eic:cec9e222956656d8,full,missing_step,"the length of the bridge , which a train 150 m long and traveling at 45 km / hr can cross in 30 sec is ?","Distance = speed x time = 45 km/hr x (1 hr / 60 min) x 0.5 min = 22.5 km = 22500 m
So, the length of the bridge is 22500 m - 150 m = 22350 m
#### 22350",Miscalculates distance after converting time units,45 km/hr x (1 hr / 60 min) x 0.5 min = 22.5 km,ok,ok,,6,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""First convert the speed from km/hr to m/s: 45 km/hr x (1000 m / 1 km) x (1 hr / 3600 s) = 12.5 m/s\nDistance = speed x time = 12.5 m/s x 30 s = 375 m\nSo, the length of the bridge is 375 m - 150 m = 225 m\n#### 225"", ""original_answer"": 225.0, ""transformed_answer"": 22350.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""This example removes the first step of the original solution. In the original_solution, step 1 involves converting the train’s speed from km/hr to m/s, and step 2 uses the converted speed to calculate the distance. Therefore, by deleting step 1, step 2 should change to calculate the distance using the original speed in km/hr. The calculation incorrectly assumes 0.5 min as time, which should still be 0.5 hr for consistency in units, leading to a distance calculation of 22.5 km (which is then incorrectly converted to 22500 m instead of 2250 m). Step 3 then mistakenly subtracts 150 m from this incorrect distance. It is the transformed step 1, which is also the original step 2, that serves as the first wrong step referencing an incorrectly converted number in the transformed_solution, so the wrong_step is 1."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:ced3f8f0ec04f3b7,full,missing_step,"Of 70 players on a football team, 31 are throwers. The rest of the team is divided so one third are left-handed and the rest are right-handed. Assuming that all throwers are right-handed, how many right-handed players are there total?","There are 70/3 = 23 left-handed players.
There are 70 - 23 = 47 right-handed players in total.
#### 47",Applies the fraction to the total instead of the remaining quantity,There are 70/3 = 23 left-handed players.,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""There are 70-31 = 39 non-throwers.\nThere are 39/3 = 13 left-handed players.\nThere are 39 - 13 = 26 right-handed non-throwers.\nThere are 26 + 31 = 57 right-handed players in total.\n#### 57"", ""original_answer"": 57.0, ""transformed_answer"": 47.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 of the original solution has been removed. Step 1 references the number 70 and 31 from the question, while step 2 references the result of step 1. Upon deletion of step 1, step 2 has been modified to directly reference the total number of players instead. However, the calculation in step 2 is incorrect because it should divide the number of non-throwers (70 - 31) rather than the total number of players. With step 1 missing, the calculation in the transformed solution incorrectly references the total number of players (70) when calculating left-handed players, which is incorrect because it omits subtracting the throwers (70 - 31) first. Following the instructions, I chose to use only the calculation involving the total number of players, but since we are removing the first step, this leads to an incorrect calculation for left-handed and consequently, right-handed players. The number of left-handed players is incorrectly calculated as 70/3 instead of (70 - 31)/3. The total right-handed players are calculated using this incorrect number, resulting in step 2 referencing an incorrect figure from the modified step 1. Therefore, the wrong_step is 1."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:cee9fd8df16aae04,full,referencing_context_value_error,"A train 280 m long, running with a speed of 72 km/hr will pass a tree in?","First convert the speed from km/hr to m/s. 75 km/hr * (1000 m / 1 km) * (1 hr / 3600 s) = 20.83 m/s
Time taken to pass the tree = Distance / Speed = 280 m / 20.83 m/s = 13.44 s
#### 13.44",Uses an incorrect given value when converting the speed,75 km/hr * (1000 m / 1 km) * (1 hr / 3600 s) = 20.83 m/s,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""First convert the speed from km/hr to m/s. 72 km/hr * (1000 m / 1 km) * (1 hr / 3600 s) = 20 m/s\nTime taken to pass the tree = Distance / Speed = 280 m / 20 m/s = 14 s\n#### 14"", ""original_answer"": 14.0, ""transformed_answer"": 13.44, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references 72 in the question as 75 wrongly, and calculates the speed 75 km/hr converted to m/s as 20.83 m/s instead of 72 km/hr to m/s being 20 m/s. This mistake also affects step 2 where the incorrect speed is used to calculate the time, resulting in 280 m / 20.83 m/s = 13.44 s instead of the correct 280 m / 20 m/s = 14 s. Even though step 2 used a wrong value, the division was done correctly. Since only step 1 has a referencing error, all other steps using the number 72 in the question, if any, are done correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:ceffccc6333efec8,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"Brian can only hold his breath underwater for 10 seconds. He wants to get better, so he starts practicing. After a week, he's doubled the amount of time he can do it. After another week, he's doubled it again from the previous week. The final week, he's increased it by 50% from the previous week. How long can Brian hold his breath for now?","At the end of his first week, Brian now has doubled his initial time of 10 seconds to 10*2=20 seconds
At the end of his second week, Brian doubled the previous week's time of 20 seconds to 20*2=40 seconds
At the end of his third week of practice, Brian adds another 50% of 40 seconds to his time so 40*.5= 20 seconds added
Having added 20 seconds to his time from his second week, Brian can now hold his breath for 40+25= 65 seconds
#### 65",Adds an amount different from the calculated percentage increase,"Having added 20 seconds to his time from his second week, Brian can now hold his breath for 40+25= 65 seconds",ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,from,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""At the end of his first week, Brian now has doubled his initial time of 10 seconds to 10*2=20 seconds\nAt the end of his second week, Brian doubled the previous week's time of 20 seconds to 20*2=40 seconds\nAt the end of his third week of practice, Brian adds another 50% of 40 seconds to his time so 40*.5= 20 seconds added\nHaving added 20 seconds to his time from his second week, Brian can now hold his breath for 40+20= 60 seconds\n#### 60"", ""original_answer"": 60, ""transformed_answer"": 65, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""Step 4 mistakenly references the added time of 20 seconds as 25 from step 3. Despite this inconsistency, step 3 in the transformed_solution <At the end of his third week of practice, Brian adds another 50% of 40 seconds to his time so 40*.5= 20 seconds added> remains exactly the same as step 3 in the original_solution, which correctly calculates the result as 20 seconds. The incorrect reference changes the calculation in step 4 from 40+20= 60 seconds to 40+25= 65 seconds. We only consider this single error, and all calculations are properly carried out based on the error made in that particular step."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:cf18c0256da0b6fd,full,counting_error,"Sheila works 8 hours per day on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, and 6 hours per day on Tuesday and Thursday. She does not work on Saturday and Sunday. She earns $288 per week. How much does she earn in dollars per hour?","On Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, Sheila works 8 hours per day, so the total hours worked on those days is 8 * 2 = 16 hours
On Tuesday and Thursday, Sheila works 6 hours per day, so the total hours worked on those days is 6 * 2 = 12 hours
The total hours worked in a week is 16 + 12 = 28 hours
If she earns $288 per week, then she earns 288 / 28 = 10.29 dollars per hour
#### 10.29",Miscounts the number of days when calculating total hours,8 * 2 = 16 hours,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""On Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, Sheila works 8 hours per day, so the total hours worked on those days is 8 * 3 = 24 hours\nOn Tuesday and Thursday, Sheila works 6 hours per day, so the total hours worked on those days is 6 * 2 = 12 hours\nThe total hours worked in a week is 24 + 12 = 36 hours\nIf she earns $288 per week, then she earns 288 / 36 = 8 dollars per hour\n#### 8"", ""original_answer"": 8.0, ""transformed_answer"": 10.29, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 incorrectly counts the three workdays of Monday, Wednesday, and Friday as only 2 days, changing the total hours worked from 8 * 3 = 24 hours to 8 * 2 = 16 hours. This error then affects the subsequent step 3, which calculates the total weekly working hours, and ultimately step 4, where the wage per hour is derived. The calculation process in step 4 is correct, but the altered number of hours leads to an incorrect wage per hour figure."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:cf5cd2a754a3d3b4,full,missing_step,"210 college students were asked in a survey if they preferred windows or mac brand computers. 60 students claimed that they preferred mac to windows brand computers. one third as many of the students who preferred mac to windows, equally preferred both brands. 90 of the students had no preference. how many of the students in the survey preferred windows to mac brand computers?","60/3 = 20 students preferred both brands
90 students had no preference
Total students who preferred mac = 60 + 20 = 80
Total students who preferred windows = 210 - 90 - 60 = 60
#### 60",Omits a category when subtracting known groups from the total,Total students who preferred windows = 210 - 90 - 60 = 60,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""60 students preferred mac to windows\n60/3 = 20 students preferred both brands\n90 students had no preference\nTotal students who preferred mac = 60 + 20 = 80\nTotal students who preferred windows = 210 - 90 - 80 = 40\n#### 40"", ""original_answer"": 40.0, ""transformed_answer"": 60.0, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 has been removed. In the original_solution, step 1 references the number 60 from the question, and step 4 references the result 80 of step 3, which includes the result of (60+20). So after deleting step 1, the step 4 should not include the count from deleted step 1. Therefore, the formula changes from 210 - 90 - 80 = 40 to 210 - 90 - 60 = 60. Due to this deletion and change, the answer to how many students preferred Windows brand computers is now incorrect, resulting in 60 instead of 40. It is step 4 in the transformed_solution, which is also step 5 in the original_solution, that serves as the first step calculating the number of students preferring Windows incorrectly due to the missing initial step, so the wrong_step is 4."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:cf5f49fe886de798,full,referencing_context_value_error,"David obtained 70, 60, 78, 60 and 65 marks (out of 100) in English, Mathematics, Physics, Chemistry and Biology. What are his average marks?","The total marks obtained = 70 + 60 + 78 + 65 + 65 = 338
Average marks = Total marks obtained / Total subjects = 338 / 5 = 67.6
#### 67.6",Uses an incorrect value for one item when calculating the total.,70 + 60 + 78 + 65 + 65 = 338,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The total marks obtained = 70 + 60 + 78 + 60 + 65 = 333\nAverage marks = Total marks obtained / Total subjects = 333 / 5 = 66.6\n#### 66.6"", ""original_answer"": 66.6, ""transformed_answer"": 67.6, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references 60 in the question as 65 wrongly for Chemistry, and calculates 70 + 60 + 78 + 65 + 65 = 338 instead of 70 + 60 + 78 + 60 + 65 = 333. The error affects the total calculation which is then used in step 2 to calculate the average, resulting in 338 / 5 = 67.6 instead of 333 / 5 = 66.6. Only this error is considered, all other steps that reference values from the question still reference them correctly except for the step referenced incorrectly. The transformed_answer is different from the original_answer due to this change."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:cfa1897d08467901,full,counting_error,"A cobbler can mend 3 pairs of shoes in an hour. From Monday to Thursday, the cobbler works for 8 hours each day, and on Friday, he only works from 8am to 11am. How many pairs of shoes can the cobbler mend in a week?","From Monday to Thursday, the cobbler can mend 3 x 8 = 24 pairs of shoes a day.
From Monday to Thursday, he can mend 24 x 3 = 72 pairs of shoes.
On Friday, he works for 11 - 8 = 3 hours.
On Friday, he can mend 3 x 3 = 9 pairs of shoes.
In a week, he can mend 72 + 9 = 81 pairs of shoes.
#### 81",Uses the wrong number of days when calculating a multi-day total,"From Monday to Thursday, he can mend 24 x 3 = 72 pairs of shoes.",ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""From Monday to Thursday, the cobbler can mend 3 x 8 = 24 pairs of shoes a day.\nFrom Monday to Thursday, he can mend 24 x 4 = 96 pairs of shoes.\nOn Friday, he works for 11 - 8 = 3 hours.\nOn Friday, he can mend 3 x 3 = 9 pairs of shoes.\nIn a week, he can mend 96 + 9 = 105 pairs of shoes.\n#### 105"", ""original_answer"": 105, ""transformed_answer"": 81, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 erroneously counts the working days from Monday to Thursday as 3 days instead of 4 days, resulting in 24 x 4 = 96 becoming 24 x 3 = 72. This affects the subsequent steps, specifically step 5 changes the total sum from 96 + 9 = 105 to 72 + 9 = 81 for the final transformed_answer. The mistake is isolated to the counting of the number of working days and does not affect the calculation of the number of pairs mended per hour or the number of hours worked on Friday."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:cfa7c37d814ca78f,full,unit_conversion_error,"If an object travels 90 feet in 3 seconds, what is the object’s approximate speed in miles per hour? (Note: 1 mile = 5280 feet)","First, calculate the speed in feet per second: 90 feet / 3 seconds = 30 feet per second.
Next, convert this speed to miles per hour: 30 feet per second * 3500 seconds per hour / 5280 feet per mile = 19.89 miles per hour.
#### 19.89",Uses an incorrect seconds-per-hour conversion factor,30 feet per second * 3500 seconds per hour / 5280 feet per mile = 19.89 miles per hour.,ok,ok,,6,False,False,False,,True,2,"{""original_solution"": ""First, calculate the speed in feet per second: 90 feet / 3 seconds = 30 feet per second.\nNext, convert this speed to miles per hour: 30 feet per second * 3600 seconds per hour / 5280 feet per mile = 20.45 miles per hour.\n#### 20.45"", ""original_answer"": 20.45, ""transformed_answer"": 19.89, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 makes an incorrect unit conversion by using 3500 seconds per hour instead of the correct value of 3600 seconds per hour, resulting in the original calculation rate of 30 feet per second * 3600 seconds per hour / 5280 feet per mile = 20.45 miles per hour becoming 30 feet per second * 3500 seconds per hour / 5280 feet per mile = 19.89 miles per hour in the transformed_solution. This is a single error because it is the first and only modification made to the original solution, and it has a direct impact on the calculation of the transformed answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:cfbbf9b38e7ca12b,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,Meryll wants to write 35 multiple-choice questions and 15 problem-solving questions for her Chemistry class. She already has written 2/5 of the multiple-choice and 1/3 of the problem-solving questions. How many more questions does she need to write for both multiple-choice and problem-solving?,"Meryll has written 35 questions x 2/5 = 14 multiple-choice questions.
While she has written 15 questions x 1/3 = 5 problem-solving questions.
The total number of questions that Meryll has to write is 35 questions + 15 questions = 50 questions.
Meryll has written a total of 14 questions + 5 questions = 19 questions.
Hence, she needs to write 50 questions - 20 questions = 30 questions more.
#### 30",Uses a different value than previously calculated in the final subtraction,"Hence, she needs to write 50 questions - 20 questions = 30 questions more.",ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Meryll has written 35 questions x 2/5 = 14 multiple-choice questions.\nWhile she has written 15 questions x 1/3 = 5 problem-solving questions.\nThe total number of questions that Meryll has to write is 35 questions + 15 questions = 50 questions.\nMeryll has written a total of 14 questions + 5 questions = 19 questions.\nHence, she needs to write 50 questions - 19 questions = 31 questions more.\n#### 31"", ""original_answer"": 31.0, ""transformed_answer"": 30.0, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""Step 4 mistakenly references 19 as 20 from step 3 and changes 50 questions - 19 questions = 31 questions more to 50 questions - 20 questions = 30 questions more. It should be noted that step 3 in the transformed_solution <The total number of questions that Meryll has to write is 35 questions + 15 questions = 50 questions.> should be the same as the step 3 in the original_solution <The total number of questions that Meryll has to write is 35 questions + 15 questions = 50 questions.> and calculates its result 50 correctly. Step 4 affects the subsequent step 5 related to it, changing from 50 questions - 19 questions = 31 questions more to 50 questions - 20 questions = 30 questions more. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of all steps starting from the wrong step and the ones following it is still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:cfc6ce9e6d293426,full,calculation_error,"Walking at the rate of 8 kmph a man covers a certain distance in 3 hrs. Running at a speed of 16 kmph, the man will cover the same distance in how many hours?","The distance covered by walking is 8 x 3 = 24 km because distance = speed x time
