#### 101.22",Adds an unrelated quantity to the total before averaging,adding an extra 20 kg not accounted for,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The total weight of the original 7 players is 7 x 103 = 721 kg\nThe new total weight is 721 + 110 + 60 = 891 kg\nThe new average weight is 891 / 9 = 99 kg\n#### 99"", ""original_answer"": 99.0, ""transformed_answer"": 101.22, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <assuming one player on the original team brought their pet to the weigh-in, adding an extra 20 kg not accounted for>, resulting in step 2 changing from 721 + 110 + 60 = 891 kg in the original_solution to 721 + 110 + 60 + 20 = 911 kg in the transformed_solution. The final result of the average weight is affected and changes from 99 kg to 101.22 kg. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:d62ba5feda5a9d63,full,adding_irrelevant_information,a shopkeeper labeled the price of his articles so as to earn a profit of 30 % on the cost price. he then sold the articles by offering a discount of 10 % on the labeled price. what is the actual percent profit earned in the deal?,"Let the cost price be $100.
He labeled the price at a 30% markup, but added an extra $5 for packaging costs, so the labeled price is 100 + 30% of 100 + 5 = 100 + 30 + 5 = $135.
He offered a 10% discount on the labeled price, so the selling price is 135 - 10% of 135 = 135 - 13.5 = $121.5.
His actual profit is 121.5 - 100 = $21.5.
His actual percent profit is (21.5/100) x 100% = 21.5%.
#### 21.5%",Adds an unsupported extra cost to the calculation,but added an extra $5 for packaging costs,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let the cost price be $100.\nHe labeled the price at a 30% markup, so the labeled price is 100 + 30% of 100 = 100 + 30 = $130.\nHe offered a 10% discount on the labeled price, so the selling price is 130 - 10% of 130 = 130 - 13 = $117.\nHis actual profit is 117 - 100 = $17.\nHis actual percent profit is (17/100) x 100% = 17%.\n#### 17%"", ""original_answer"": 17.0, ""transformed_answer"": 21.5, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Step 2 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement, which is <but added an extra $5 for packaging costs>, resulting in step 2 changing from 100 + 30% of 100 = 130 in the original_solution to 100 + 30% of 100 + 5 = 135 in the transformed_solution. This affects the final selling price and thus the actual profit and actual percent profit figures."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:d63d49690e5a4bf2,full,adding_irrelevant_information,Two trains are moving in the same direction at 162 kmph and 18 kmph. The faster train crosses a man in the slower train in 33 seconds. Find the length of the faster train?,"The relative speed of the two trains is 162 - 18 = 144 kmph
