eic,eic:d93795b709d09171,full,confusing_formula_error,What is the area of square field whose side of length 13 m?,"The area of a square is side+side+side+side, so the area of the square field is 13+13+13+13=52 square meters.
#### 52",Uses the perimeter formula instead of the area formula,The area of a square is side+side+side+side,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,12,"{""original_solution"": ""The area of a square is side*side, so the area of the square field is 13*13=169 square meters.\n#### 169"", ""original_answer"": 169.0, ""transformed_answer"": 52.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 confuses the area and perimeter of a square. The question requires that the area of the square to be solved be equal to the side length multiplied by side length, rather than the side length plus side length plus side length plus side length, which is equivalent to the perimeter of the square, resulting in 13*13=169 in the original_solution becoming 13+13+13+13=52 in the transformed_solution. This alteration affects the transformed_answer, changing it from 169 to 52. Note that the language description of step 1 < The area of a square is > in the original_solution remains unchanged in the transformed_solution. It should be also noted that the intention here is to only consider one step of confusing formula error—the one where the formula of the square area is mistakenly replaced with the formula for the square perimeter—while the rest of the calculation process is still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:d9449580e36aedaa,full,referencing_context_value_error,"If a train, traveling at a speed of 360 kmph, crosses a pole in 5 sec, then the length of the train is?","First, convert the speed from km/h to m/s: 350 km/h * (1000 m/1 km) * (1 h/3600 s) = approximately 97.22 m/s
The distance traveled by the train is the length of the train: distance = speed * time = 97.22 m/s * 5 s = approximately 486.11 m
