#### 1092",Uses an incorrect value for the number of floors per building,The number of floors is 13 floors/building x 2 buildings = 26 floors,ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,"building, floors",True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The number of floors is 12 floors/building x 2 buildings = 24 floors\nThe number of apartments is 6 apartments/floor x 24 floors = 144 apartments\nThe company has to buy 7 doors/apartment x 144 apartments = 1008 doors\n#### 1008"", ""original_answer"": 1008.0, ""transformed_answer"": 1092.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 references 12 in the question as 13 wrongly, and calculates 6 apartments/floor x 26 floors=156 apartments instead of 6 apartments/floor x 24 floors=144 apartments. Its subsequent related step will be affected, calculating 7 doors/apartment x 156 apartments=1092 doors instead of 7 doors/apartment x 144 apartments=1008 doors in step 3. Please note that you should only consider one error, so step 2 still references 24 in the question correctly."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_context_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:f0d0a82b2b0bc36e,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"There were 36000 hardback copies of a certain novel sold before the paperback version was issued. From the time the first paperback copy was sold until the last copy of the novel was sold, 9 times as many paperback copies as hardback copies were sold. If a total of 440000 copies of the novel were sold in all, how many paperback copies were sold?","Let x be the number of hardback copies sold after the paperback version was issued.
9x is the number of paperback copies sold after the hardbacks and because of a special promotion, an additional 10000 paperback copies were sold.
36000 + x is the total number of hardback copies sold.
9x + 10000 + 36000 + x = 440000.
10x + 46000 = 440000.
10x = 394000.
x = 39400.
9x = 354600.
The total number of paperback copies sold is 354600 + 10000 = 364600.
#### 364600",Introduces an unsupported extra quantity into the equation,"because of a special promotion, an additional 10000 paperback copies were sold.",ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let x be the number of hardback copies sold after the paperback version was issued.\n9x is the number of paperback copies sold after the paperback version was issued.\n36000 + x is the total number of hardback copies sold.\n9x + 36000 + x = 440000.\n10x + 36000 = 440000.\n10x = 404000.\nx = 40400.\n9x = 363600.\nThe number of paperback copies sold is 363600.\n#### 363600"", ""original_answer"": 363600, ""transformed_answer"": 364600, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <because of a special promotion, an additional 10000 paperback copies were sold>, resulting in step 3 changing from 9x + 36000 + x = 440000 in the original_solution to 9x + 10000 + 36000 + x = 440000 in the transformed_solution. The additional 10000 paperback copies affect the subsequent calculations, changing from 9x = 363600 to the total number of paperback copies sold is 354600 + 10000 = 364600 in the transformed_solution. The incorrect added information results in a different transformed_answer from the original_answer. It should be noted that the error is integrated into the existing step 3 and there is only one error of adding additional information."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:f0f196114209b51e,full,counting_error,What is the probability of getting 53 Mondays in a leap year?,"There are a total of 366 days in a leap year
There are 52 weeks in a year, so there are 52 Mondays in a normal year
In a leap year, there are 52 weeks and 1 extra day. Therefore, the probability of getting 53 Mondays in a leap year is 1/7 or approximately 0.1429
#### 1/7 or approximately 0.1429",Miscounts the days remaining after forming complete weeks,"In a leap year, there are 52 weeks and 1 extra day.",ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""There are a total of 366 days in a leap year\nThere are 52 weeks in a year, so there are 52 Mondays in a normal year\nIn a leap year, there are 52 weeks and 2 extra days. Therefore, the probability of getting 53 Mondays in a leap year is 2/7 or approximately 0.2857\n#### 2/7 or approximately 0.2857"", ""original_answer"": ""2/7 or approximately 0.2857"", ""transformed_answer"": ""1/7 or approximately 0.1429"", ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""The transformation changes the number of extra days in a leap year from 2 to 1 in step 3, affecting the calculation of the probability for 53 Mondays from 2/7 to 1/7. The subsequent steps carry on the adjusted value, while the setup and calculation steps remain consistent with the accurate leap year duration and number of weeks. This modification does not affect the previous or unrelated steps and results in a different transformed_answer from the original_answer."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:f134bf4df87a43d8,full,missing_step,"Bill is ordering a new truck. He has decided to purchase a two-ton truck with several added features: a king cab upgrade, a towing package, leather seats, running boards, and the upgraded exterior light package. The base price of the truck is $30,000, and the other features are at extra cost. The king cab is an extra $7,500, leather seats are one-third the cost of the king cab upgrade, running boards are $500 less than the leather seats, and the upgraded exterior light package is $1500. What is the total cost of Bill's new truck, in dollars?","The running boards are $500 less than the leather seats which are one-third the cost of the king cab upgrade, at a cost of $7500/3-$500=$2000
The total cost of the truck is $30,000+$7500+($7500/3)+$2000+$1500=$42,000
#### 42,000",Omits a listed component from the total calculation,"The total cost of the truck is $30,000+$7500+($7500/3)+$2000+$1500=$42,000",ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The leather seats are one-third the cost of the king cab upgrade, at a cost of $7,500/3=$2500.\nThe running boards are $500 less than the leather seats at a cost of $2500-$500=$2000\nThe total cost of the truck is $30,000+$7500+$2500+$2000+$1500=$43,500\n#### 43,500"", ""original_answer"": 43500.0, ""transformed_answer"": 42000.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""This example removes intermediate step 1. In the original_solution, step 1 calculates the cost of the leather seats as one-third the cost of the king cab upgrade, and step 2 references the result $2500 from step 1. Therefore, after deleting step 1, step 2 should reference the calculation for the cost of the leather seats directly as one-third of the cost of the king cab upgrade and then subtracting $500. The language in the transformed_solution is updated to combine the deleted step's calculation with the next step into 'The running boards are $500 less than the leather seats which are one-third the cost of the king cab upgrade, at a cost of $7500/3-$500=$2000'. Step 3 is adjusted accordingly to reference the recalculated price for the running boards and provide a new total cost, changing from $30,000+$7500+$2500+$2000+$1500=$43,500 to $30,000+$7500+($7500/3)+$2000+$1500=$42,000. Since step 1 in the transformed_solution now combines the original steps 1 and 2, the first wrong reference after the missing step occurs in the new step 1."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:f192572bcf618ec5,full,operator_error,"A certain number when divided by 39 leaves a remainder 16, what is the remainder when the same number is divided by 13?","Let the number be x.
x = 39a + 16 for some integer a
x = 39a + 16 = 13a/3 + 13*1 + 3
