So he rows at a speed of 3*2=6 mph
That means it takes him 60/5=12 hours to row around the lake
#### 12",Uses an incorrect previous-step value when calculating the final time,That means it takes him 60/5=12 hours to row around the lake,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The perimeter of the lake is 4*15=60 miles\nHe swims at a speed of 60/20=3 mph\nSo he rows at a speed of 3*2=6 mph\nThat means it takes him 60/6=10 hours to row around the lake\n#### 10"", ""original_answer"": 10.0, ""transformed_answer"": 12.0, ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""Step 4 mistakenly references 6 as 5 from step 3 and changes 60/6=10 hours to 60/5=12 hours to calculate the time it takes to row around the lake. It should be noted that step 3 in the transformed_solution <So he rows at a speed of 3*2=6 mph> should be the same as the step 3 in the original_solution <So he rows at a speed of 3*2=6 mph> and calculates its result 6 correctly. The subsequent step 4, which should be affected by step 3, is mistakenly using 5 instead of 6 that leads to the wrong calculation of 60 divided by 5 instead of 6. You need to only consider one error of referencing, so the calculation of all steps is still correct except for the intentionally altered one."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""referencing_previous_step_value_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:f51f44a9f86dadf1,full,counting_error,"I spend 40 hours a week at work and like to organize my time so that I spend an equal number of hours on the two tasks I currently have. Currently, I am spending 5 hours a day on task 1 and 3 on task 2. How many hours a week less do I need to spend on task 1 in order to evenly distribute my time?","The total hours spent on task 1 in a week is 5 * 6 = 30 hours
