x = 45 / 1.25
x = 40
#### 40",Makes an arithmetic error when dividing by a decimal multiplier,"x = 45 / 1.25
x = 40",ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""Let x be the enrollment on January 1, 1991.\n1.25x = 45\nx = 45 / 1.25\nx = 36\n#### 36"", ""original_answer"": 36.0, ""transformed_answer"": 40.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""In the transformed solution, step 3 is the calculation error where 45 / 1.25 is incorrectly calculated as 40 instead of the correct 36. This is the only error introduced, and it changes the final answer without affecting the setup of the equation in the previous steps. The transformed answer of 40 is incorrect because it is based on the incorrect calculation in step 3."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:f74af932f863bb52,full,confusing_formula_error,You draw a rectangle that is 7 inches wide. It is 4 times as long as it is wide. What is the area of the rectangle?,"The length of the rectangle is 4 * 7 inches = 28 inches.
The area of the rectangle is 7 inches + 7 inches + 28 inches + 28 inches = 70 inches.
#### 70",Uses addition of side lengths instead of multiplying length and width for area,The area of the rectangle is 7 inches + 7 inches + 28 inches + 28 inches = 70 inches.,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,,True,2,"{""original_solution"": ""The length of the rectangle is 4 * 7 inches = 28 inches.\nThe area of the rectangle is 7 inches * 28 inches = 196 square inches.\n#### 196"", ""original_answer"": 196.0, ""transformed_answer"": 70.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 confuses the perimeter and area of a rectangle. The question requires calculating the area by multiplying the length by the width, not by adding the lengths and widths, which would give the perimeter of the rectangle. As a result, '7 inches * 28 inches = 196 square inches' in the original_solution is changed to '7 inches + 7 inches + 28 inches + 28 inches = 70 inches' in the transformed_solution. The subsequent step affected is the final answer, changing from 196 square inches in the original_solution to 70 inches in the transformed_solution. The language description 'The area of the rectangle is' in the original_solution remains the same in the transformed_solution. The confused formula appears in step 2, where the formula for calculating area is mistakenly replaced with the formula for calculating the perimeter."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:f77fb26c6f69ebbb,full,counting_error,"On the weekend, Tony will walk to the store. On weekdays, he runs to the store. When he walks, he goes 2 MPH. When he runs he goes 10 MPH. The store is 4 miles away. If he goes on Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday, what is the average time in minutes that he spends to get to the store?","On Sunday he takes 2 hours to get there because 4 / 2 = 2
This takes him 120 minutes because 2 x 60 = 120
On Tuesday and Thursday, it takes him .4 hours to get to the store because 4 / 10 = .4
On Tuesday and Thursday, it takes him 24 minutes to get to the store because .4 x 60 = 24
In total it takes him 144 minutes to get to the store because 120 + 12 + 12 = 144
On average it takes him 48 minutes because 144 / 3 = 48
#### 48",Uses incorrect addends when summing individual travel times,In total it takes him 144 minutes to get to the store because 120 + 12 + 12 = 144,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,when,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""On Sunday he takes 2 hours to get there because 4 / 2 = 2\nThis takes him 120 minutes because 2 x 60 = 120\nOn Tuesday and Thursday, it takes him .4 hours to get to the store because 4 / 10 = .4\nOn Tuesday and Thursday, it takes him 24 minutes to get to the store because .4 x 60 = 24\nIn total it takes him 168 minutes to get to the store because 120 + 24 + 24 = 168\nOn average it takes him 56 minutes because 168 / 3 = 56\n#### 56"", ""original_answer"": 56.0, ""transformed_answer"": 48.0, ""wrong_step"": 5, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 5 erroneously adds the times for Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday as if Tony only spent 12 minutes each on Tuesday and Thursday, instead of 24 minutes, resulting in an incorrect total time of 144 minutes. This affects the final average calculation in step 6, changing it from 168 / 3 = 56 minutes to 144 / 3 = 48 minutes. The rest of the calculations are still correct, as we consider only one step of counting error."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""counting_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:f7be848dea4b3376,full,unit_conversion_error,"If it takes a machine 2 ⁄ 3 minute to produce one item, how many items will it produce in 1 hour?","First, convert 1 hour to minutes: 1 hour * 50 minutes per hour = 50 minutes.
Then, divide 50 minutes by 2/3 minutes per item: 50 / (2/3) = 50 * (3/2) = 75.
#### 75",Uses an incorrect conversion factor between hours and minutes,1 hour * 50 minutes per hour = 50 minutes,ok,ok,,9,False,False,False,,True,6,"{""original_solution"": ""First, convert 1 hour to minutes: 1 hour * 60 minutes per hour = 60 minutes.\nThen, divide 60 minutes by 2/3 minutes per item: 60 / (2/3) = 60 * (3/2) = 90.\n#### 90"", ""original_answer"": 90.0, ""transformed_answer"": 75.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 1 makes an incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 hour is wrongly taken to have 50 minutes instead of 60 minutes, resulting in 1 hour * 60 minutes per hour = 60 minutes in the original_solution becoming 1 hour * 50 minutes per hour = 50 minutes in the transformed_solution. And it affects the subsequent related step 2, which changes from 60 / (2/3) = 90 to 50 / (2/3) = 75. It should be noted that only one step of unit conversion error is introduced, so other steps should remain unaffected."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:f8010c7fff84e435,full,unit_conversion_error,"sarah is driving to the airport. after driving at 50 miles per hour for one hour, she realizes that if she continues at that same average rate she will be an hour late for her flight. she then travels 100 miles per hour for the rest of the trip, and arrives 30 minutes before her flight departs. how many miles did she drive in all?","At 50 miles per hour, Sarah drove for 1 hour, so she traveled 50 miles.
At 100 miles per hour, she needed to make up for being 1 hour late and arrive 30 minutes before the flight, a total of 1 hour and 40 minutes = 1.67 hours. She traveled at 100 mph for 1.67 hours, so she traveled 100 * 1.67 = 167 miles.
In total, Sarah drove 50 + 167 = 217 miles in all.
#### 217",Computes the time difference between late and early arrival incorrectly,a total of 1 hour and 40 minutes = 1.67 hours.,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,late,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""At 50 miles per hour, Sarah drove for 1 hour, so she traveled 50 miles.\nAt 100 miles per hour, she needed to make up for being 1 hour late and arrive 30 minutes before the flight, a total of 1 hour and 30 minutes = 1.5 hours. She traveled at 100 mph for 1.5 hours, so she traveled 100 * 1.5 = 150 miles.\nIn total, Sarah drove 50 + 150 = 200 miles in all.\n#### 200"", ""original_answer"": 200.0, ""transformed_answer"": 217.0, ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""Here, step 2 makes incorrect unit conversion, which means 1 hour and 30 minutes is wrongly converted to 1 hour and 40 minutes instead of 1 hour and 30 minutes, resulting in 1 hour and 30 minutes = 1.5 hours in the original_solution becoming 1 hour and 40 minutes = 1.67 hours in the transformed_solution. And it affects the subsequent related step 3, which changes the travel distance calculation from 100 * 1.5 = 150 miles to 100 * 1.67 = 167 miles. The sum in step 4 changes from 50 + 150 = 200 miles to 50 + 167 = 217 miles. It should be noted that you only need to consider one step of unit conversion error, so the unit conversion of step 1 and the other calculation processes are still correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""unit_conversion_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:f867692126abe223,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"the average (arithmetic mean) of 20, 40, and 60 is 9 more than the average of 10, 70, and what number?","The average of 20, 40, and 60 is (20 + 40 + 60) / 3 = 120 / 3 = 40
The average of 10, 70, and x is (10 + 70 + x) / 3
Because there is an error in recording one of the numbers, it should be 10, 80, and x, so the equation is (10 + 80 + x) / 3 + 9 = 40
(90 + x) / 3 + 9 = 40
(90 + x) / 3 = 31
90 + x = 93
x = 3
#### 3",Changes a given value without justification,"Because there is an error in recording one of the numbers, it should be 10, 80, and x",ok,ok,,5,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The average of 20, 40, and 60 is (20 + 40 + 60) / 3 = 120 / 3 = 40\nThe average of 10, 70, and x is (10 + 70 + x) / 3\nThe equation is (10 + 70 + x) / 3 + 9 = 40\n(80 + x) / 3 + 9 = 40\n(80 + x) / 3 = 31\n80 + x = 93\nx = 13\n#### 13"", ""original_answer"": 13.0, ""transformed_answer"": 3.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""Step 3 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Because there is an error in recording one of the numbers, it should be 10, 80, and x>, resulting in step 3 changing from (10 + 70 + x) / 3 + 9 = 40 in the original_solution to (10 + 80 + x) / 3 + 9 = 40 in the transformed_solution. This alters the subsequent calculations and changes the final answer from x = 13 to x = 3. It should be noted that only one error of adding additional information in one step is considered, and the other calculation processes should remain correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:f86cf29b71d95459,full,adding_irrelevant_information,"A towel, when bleached, lost 20% of its length and 20% of its breadth. What is the percentage decrease in area?","Assuming the original area of the towel gets an additional print on it which increases the area by 10%, then when bleached, it lost 20% of its length and 20% of its breadth. The area of the towel is decreased by 44% because 20% + 20% + (20% x 20%) - 10% = 44%
#### 44%",Adds an irrelevant percentage adjustment to the calculation,Assuming the original area of the towel gets an additional print on it which increases the area by 10%,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The area of the towel is decreased by 36% because 20% + 20% + (20% x 20%) = 36%\n#### 36%"", ""original_answer"": 36.0, ""transformed_answer"": 44.0, ""wrong_step"": 1, ""explanation"": ""Step 1 adds information that is not mentioned in the question statement <Assuming the original area of the towel gets an additional print on it which increases the area by 10%>, resulting in step 1 changing the calculation to include an irrelevant 10% increase which adjusts the final percentage decrease to 44%. This additional print and the subsequent calculation are not relevant or required by the original question. It should be noted that you only need to consider one error of adding additional information in one step, and the other calculation processes should be correct."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""adding_irrelevant_information"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:f891d3ee7c116f1c,full,confusing_formula_error,"James is building a hall of mirrors. Three of the walls will be completed covered with glass. If two of those walls are 30 feet by 12 feet and the third is 20 feet by 12 feet, how many square feet of glass does he need?","First find the area of one of the long walls: 30 feet * 12 feet = 360 square feet
Then double that amount since there are two walls: 360 square feet * 2 = 720 square feet
Then find the perimeter of the short wall: 20 feet + 12 feet + 20 feet + 12 feet = 64 square feet
Then add that perimeter to the area of the two long walls to find the total area: 720 square feet + 64 square feet = 784 square feet
#### 784",Uses a perimeter formula instead of an area formula,Then find the perimeter of the short wall: 20 feet + 12 feet + 20 feet + 12 feet = 64 square feet,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,3,"{""original_solution"": ""First find the area of one of the long walls: 30 feet * 12 feet = 360 square feet\nThen double that amount since there are two walls: 360 square feet * 2 = 720 square feet\nThen find the area of the short wall: 20 feet * 12 feet = 240 square feet\nThen add that area to the area of the two long walls to find the total area: 720 square feet + 240 square feet = 960 square feet\n#### 960"", ""original_answer"": 960.0, ""transformed_answer"": 784.0, ""wrong_step"": 3, ""explanation"": ""In the transformed solution, step 3 incorrectly uses the perimeter formula instead of the area formula by summing the sides of the wall. The error results in calculating the perimeter of 20 feet + 12 feet + 20 feet + 12 feet = 64 square feet instead of calculating the area with 20 feet * 12 feet = 240 square feet as in the original. This affects the final step, changing the total area from 720 square feet + 240 square feet = 960 square feet to 720 square feet + 64 square feet = 784 square feet. The language in the explanation of step 3 < Then find the perimeter of the short wall: > matches the original explanation but presents an incorrect calculation due to the introduced error. The single error is introduced in step 3 while other steps use correct formulas."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""confusing_formula_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""GSM8K""}",,,,
eic,eic:f89b0978cf6f9efa,full,referencing_context_value_error,Mitchell has 30 pencils. He has 6 more pencils than Antonio. How many pencils does Mitchell and Antonio have together?,"Antonio has 32-6 = 26 pencils.
