#### 64π",Confuses the square’s side length with its diagonal when finding the circle’s diameter,The diameter of the circle y is equal to the side length of the square x,ok,ok,,13,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""original_solution"": ""If the perimeter of x is 64, then each side length is 64 / 4 = 16.\nThe diameter of the circle y is equal to the side length of the square x because the square is inscribed in the circle.\nSo the diameter of y is 16.\nThe circumference of y is π times the diameter, so the circumference of y is 16π.\n#### 16π"", ""original_answer"": ""16π"", ""transformed_answer"": ""64π"", ""wrong_step"": 2, ""explanation"": ""In this example, step 1 is removed. The original step 1 references the number 64 from the question to find one side length of the square. By removing this step, the subsequent step that originally referenced the result of step 1 now incorrectly references the original total perimeter. Step 2, which is incorrectly using the perimeter to represent the diameter of the circle, should result in an incorrect circumference because it directly uses the perimeter 64 in place of the diameter to calculate the circle’s circumference, as opposed to dividing 64 by 4 to get the correct side length (which is also the diameter). Thus, the wrong step is step 2, because it is the first step in the transformed solution which uses the incorrect number, leading to the transformed answer 64π instead of 16π."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""missing_step"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
