#### 3/7",Calculates the product of combination counts incorrectly,The number of favorable outcomes is 6 x 6 = 30,ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,,False,1,"{""original_solution"": ""The total number of ways to choose 4 students out of 8 is C(8,4) = 70\nThe number of ways to choose 2 men out of 4 is C(4,2) = 6\nThe number of ways to choose 2 women out of 4 is also C(4,2) = 6\nSo, the total number of favorable outcomes is 6 x 6 = 36\nThe probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes, which is 36/70\n#### 18/35"", ""original_answer"": ""18/35"", ""transformed_answer"": ""3/7"", ""wrong_step"": 4, ""explanation"": ""In the transformation, step 4 mistakenly calculates 6 x 6 = 30 instead of 6 x 6 = 36. This leads to a change in the final probability calculation in step 5, which becomes 30/70 instead of the correct 36/70. All other steps remain unchanged because they are not affected by the calculation error introduced in step 4. This altered step directly leads to a different transformed_answer, which is 3/7 instead of 18/35."", ""is_single_error"": true, ""type_dir"": ""calculation_error"", ""source_benchmark"": ""MathQA""}",,,,
eic,eic:ff0362b729cf4fd0,full,referencing_previous_step_value_error,"Rubert has 4 times the number of candies James has. James has 3 times the number of candies Adam has. If Adam has 6 candies, how many candies do the three of them have in total?","James has 3 * 6 candies = 18 candies.
