$\mathbf{B}^4 = \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbf{B}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 4 \\ 4 & 7 \end{pmatrix}$

$\mathbf{B}^6 = \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbf{B}^4 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 4 \\ 4 & 7 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 23 & 14 \\ 14 & 23 \end{pmatrix}$

$\mathbf{B}^8 = \mathbf{B}^2 \mathbf{B}^6 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 23 & 14 \\ 14 & 23 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 67 & 40 \\ 40 & 67 \end{pmatrix}$

Step 2: Write the equation with the given condition:

$(\mathbf{B}^8 + \mathbf{B}^6 + \mathbf{B}^4 + \mathbf{B}^2 + \mathbf{I}) \mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} 10 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}$

Substitute the calculated values:

$\begin{pmatrix} 67 & 40 \\ 40 & 67 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 23 & 14 \\ 14 & 23 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 4 \\ 4 & 7 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} w_1 \\ w_2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 10 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}$

Combine the matrix:

$\begin{pmatrix} 100 & 62 \\ 62 & 100 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} w_1 \\ w_2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 10 \\ 10 \end{pmatrix}$

Step 3: Solve the system of linear equations:

$\begin{cases} 100w_1 + 62w_2 = 10 \\ 62w_1 + 100w_2 = 10 \end{cases}$

To solve this system, we can multiply the second equation by -1 and add it to the first equation:

$(100w_1 + 62w_2 = 10) - (-62w_1 + 100w_2 = 10)$

$182w_1 = 20$

Divide by 182:

$w_1 = \frac{20}{182} = \frac{10}{91}$

Now, substitute $w_1$ into the second equation:

$62(\frac{10}{91}) + 100w_2 = 10$

Solve for $w_2$:

$62(\frac{10}{91}) + 100w_2 = 10$

$62(\frac{10}{91}) + 100w_2 = 10$

$100w_2 = 10 - \frac{620}{91}$

$w_2 = \frac{10 - \frac{620}{91}}{100} = \frac{10 \times 91 - 620}{9100} = \frac{910 - 620}{9100} = \frac{290}{9100}$

So, the vector $\mathbf{w}$ is:

$\mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{10}{91} \\ \frac{290}{9100} \end{pmatrix}$

The answer is:

$\mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{10}{91} \\ \frac{290}{9100} \end{pmatrix}$",Miscomputes matrix products when calculating powers,$\mathbf{B}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix}$,ok,ok,,6,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""extent"": ""obvious error"", ""difficulty_file"": ""challenging"", ""subset"": ""check_type_answer""}",,,,
mathclean,mathclean:9f6480dae69c039d,full,expression error,"Find the maximum value of the determinant
