Since Sandy loses weight at a rate of 8 pounds per 4 weeks, it will take her 4 weeks * 2 = 8 weeks to lose the same amount of weight as Joey.
 8",Multiplies the time interval by the rate instead of finding time for the target amount,it will take her 4 weeks * 2 = 8 weeks,ok,ok,,15,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 1, ""incorrect_step"": ""To lose the same amount of weight as Joey, Sandy needs 4 weeks * 2 pounds per week = 8 pounds."", ""error_description"": ""if sandy takes 4 weeks to lose what joey loses in 1 week, then sandy loses 2 pounds in 4 weeks and not 2x4=8 pounds"", ""reference_solution"": ""Joey loses 8 pounds / 4 weeks = 2 pounds per week.\nIn order to match Joey, Sandy should lose 2 pounds / 4 = 0.5 of a pound per week.\nTo find the number of weeks it will take, divide the number of pounds by the rate of weight loss: 8 pounds / 0.5 pounds/week = 16 weeks\n 16"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi , could you please walk me through your solution?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Sure. Joey loses 8 pounds in 4 weeks, so he loses 8/4 = 2 pounds per week. To lose the same amount of weight as Joey, Sandy needs 4 weeks * 2 pounds per week = 8 pounds. Since Sandy loses weight at a rate of 8 pounds per 4 weeks, it will take her 4 weeks * 2 = 8 weeks to lose the same amount of weight as Joey. 8"", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""steven lets consider this example. lets say that i read 24 pages in 4 weeks. you take 4 weeks to read what I read in one week. then how many pages do you read in 4 weeks?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""To find out how many weeks it will take Sandy to lose the same amount of weight as Joey, I first calculated Joey's rate of weight loss, which is 8 pounds in 4 weeks, so he loses 2 pounds per week. Since Sandy needs 4 weeks to lose the same amount of weight that Joey loses in a single week, she should aim to lose 0.5 pounds per week. To determine how many weeks it will take Sandy to lose 8 pounds at a rate of 0.5 pounds per week, I divided 8 pounds by 0.5 pounds/week, which equals 16 weeks. So, it will take Sandy 16 weeks to lose the same amount of weight as Joey."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""Joey loses 8 pounds in 4 weeks, so he loses 8/4 = 2 pounds per week."", ""To lose the same amount of weight as Joey, Sandy needs 4 weeks * 2 pounds per week = 8 pounds."", ""Since Sandy loses weight at a rate of 8 pounds per 4 weeks, it will take her 4 weeks * 2 = 8 weeks to lose the same amount of weight as Joey."", "" 8""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:66599dc16871752f,full,Missing / Wrong factual knowledge,"A baker sells pastries for $5 and works 7 days a week. On Monday he sold 2. Every day the number of sales increases by 1 compared to the previous day. On average, how many pastries does he sell on each day of the week?","From Tuesday to Sunday, the baker sells pastries for 6 days, and the total number of pastries sold is 3+4+5+6+7+8 = 33.
The average number of pastries sold each day is 33/6 = 5.5 (rounded to one decimal place).
Therefore, on average, the baker sells 5.5 pastries each day of the week.
 5.5",Omits one day when calculating the weekly average,"From Tuesday to Sunday, the baker sells pastries for 6 days",ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 0, ""incorrect_step"": ""From Tuesday to Sunday, the baker sells pastries for 6 days, and the total number of pastries sold is 3+4+5+6+7+8 = 33."", ""error_description"": ""Monday is included as a day of the week."", ""reference_solution"": ""You know from the statement that on Monday he sells 2 pastries, so on Tuesday he sells 3, Wednesday 4, Thursday 5, Friday 6, Saturday 7 and Sunday 8 pastries.\nSo the average value is (2+3+4+5+6+7+8) pastries/7 days\nThe baker sells, on average, 35/7= 5 pastries/day for 1 week\n 5"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi Winnie! Tell me about how you solved this problem."", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""I started by counting the total number of pastries sold from Tuesday to Sunday, which was 3+4+5+6+7+8 = 33. Then I divided the total number of pastries sold by 6, since there were 6 days in the week, to get the average number of pastries sold each day, which was 33/6 = 5.5."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""The problem asks you to determine how many pastries are sold on average each day of the week, but you're missing one day--what's missing?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""The baker sells 2 pastries on Monday, 3 on Tuesday, 4 on Wednesday, 5 on Thursday, 6 on Friday, 7 on Saturday, and 8 on Sunday. To find the average number of pastries sold each day of the week, I added up the total number of pastries sold (2+3+4+5+6+7+8) and divided by the number of days in a week, which is 7. So, the average number of pastries sold per day is 35/7 = 5 pastries."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""From Tuesday to Sunday, the baker sells pastries for 6 days, and the total number of pastries sold is 3+4+5+6+7+8 = 33."", ""The average number of pastries sold each day is 33/6 = 5.5 (rounded to one decimal place)."", ""Therefore, on average, the baker sells 5.5 pastries each day of the week."", "" 5.5""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:666fdc872b84ec23,full,Extra quantity or Missing quantity,"Brian's friend Bobby has 5 fewer than 3 times as many video games as Brian does.  If Brian has 20 video games but lost 5 right before the comparison was made, how many does Bobby have?","Before losing 5 video games, Brian had 20, so after losing 5 he has 20-5 = 15 video games.
Three times as many video games as Brian had before losing 5 is 3*20 = 60 video games.
Bobby has 5 fewer than 3 times as many video games as Brian does, which means Bobby has 60-5 = 55 video games.
 55",Uses the original quantity instead of the reduced quantity in the comparison.,Three times as many video games as Brian had before losing 5 is 3*20 = 60 video games.,ok,ok,,12,False,False,False,comparison,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 1, ""incorrect_step"": ""Three times as many video games as Brian had before losing 5 is 3*20 = 60 video games."", ""error_description"": ""student calculated the number of video games that brian had after losing 5. then calculated the next step with the 20 video games."", ""reference_solution"": ""If Brian previously had 20 video games but lost 5, that means he now has 20-5=15 video games\nBrian has 15 video games, so if Bobby has 5 fewer than 3 times as many as Brian does we must first perform 15*3=45\nWe then subtract 5 from the previous total for 45-5=40 games in total.\n 40"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi , could you please walk me through your solution?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Sure. Before losing 5 video games, Brian had 20, so after losing 5 he has 20-5 = 15 video games. Then, three times as many video games as Brian had before losing 5 is 3*20 = 60 video games. Bobby has 5 fewer than 3 times as many video games as Brian does, which means Bobby has 60-5 = 55 video games. So the answer is 55."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""jia can you explain why you multiplied 3x20 in step 2?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""If Brian originally had 20 video games but lost 5, he now has 20-5=15 video games. Bobby has 5 fewer than 3 times as many video games as Brian, so we first calculate 15*3=45. Subtracting 5 from this total gives us 45-5=40 games in total for Bobby."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""Before losing 5 video games, Brian had 20, so after losing 5 he has 20-5 = 15 video games."", ""Three times as many video games as Brian had before losing 5 is 3*20 = 60 video games."", ""Bobby has 5 fewer than 3 times as many video games as Brian does, which means Bobby has 60-5 = 55 video games."", "" 55""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:669887e86383fe2b,full,Reached correct solution but proceeded further,"It will take Richard and Sarah 3 years to save enough for a down payment on a house at their current rates. If the house's downpayment is $108000, calculate the amount each person saves per month if they share the monthly savings.","Let x be the amount each person saves per month.
In one year, each person saves 12x.
In three years, each person saves 3(12x) = 36x.
Together, they save 2(36x) = 72x in three years.
Since the house's down payment is $108000, we have 72x = 108000.
Therefore, x = 108000/72 = $1500 per person per month.
Since they share the monthly savings, each person saves $1500/2 = $750 per month. 
 750",Divides an already per-person amount by two again,"Therefore, x = 108000/72 = $1500 per person per month. Since they share the monthly savings, each person saves $1500/2 = $750 per month.",ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 6, ""incorrect_step"": ""Since they share the monthly savings, each person saves $1500/2 = $750 per month. "", ""error_description"": null, ""reference_solution"": ""If they are to save every month for three years to reach the target amount, and each year has 12 months, they'll save for 12*3 = 36 months.\nIf the house's downpayment is $108000, the total amount they both save per month is $108000/36 = $3000 every month.\nSince they share the monthly savings, each person will pay $3000/2= =$1500\n 1500"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi Cody, talk me through your solution?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Sure. I started by letting x be the amount each person saves per month. Then I calculated that in one year, each person saves 12x, and in three years, each person saves 36x. So together, they save 72x in three years. Since the house's downpayment is $108000, I set up the equation 72x = 108000. Solving this equation, I got x = 108000/72 = $1500 per person per month. Since they share the monthly savings, each person saves $1500/2 = $750 per month."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""Ok, you've gone too far. You've correctly identified the 36 months of savings."", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""To find out how much Richard and Sarah save per month, I first calculated the total number of months they will save, which is 3 years * 12 months = 36 months. Then, I divided the total down payment amount by the total number of months to find out how much they need to save per month, which is $108,000 / 36 = $3,000. Since they share the monthly savings equally, I divided the total monthly savings by 2 to find out how much each person saves per month, which is $3,000 / 2 = $1,500."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""Let x be the amount each person saves per month."", ""In one year, each person saves 12x."", ""In three years, each person saves 3(12x) = 36x."", ""Together, they save 2(36x) = 72x in three years."", ""Since the house's down payment is $108000, we have 72x = 108000."", ""Therefore, x = 108000/72 = $1500 per person per month."", ""Since they share the monthly savings, each person saves $1500/2 = $750 per month. "", "" 750""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:66b0b47ab0ff780d,full,Extra quantity or Missing quantity,"Omar buys a 12-ounce cup of coffee every morning on the way to work. On the way to work, he drinks one-quarter of the cup. When he gets to his office, he drinks another half of the cup. He forgets to drink any more of his coffee once he starts working, and when he remembers his coffee, he only drinks 1 ounce of the remaining amount because it is cold. How many ounces will be left in the cup afterward?","Omar drinks 12 x 1/4 = 3 ounces of coffee on the way to work.
He drinks another 12 x 1/2 = 6 ounces of coffee at his office.
So, he drinks a total of 3 + 6 = 9 ounces of coffee.
Thus, there will be 12 - 9 = 3 ounces of coffee left in the cup afterward when he drinks 1 ounce of coffee because it is cold.
 3",Fails to subtract the final amount consumed from the remaining amount,"Thus, there will be 12 - 9 = 3 ounces of coffee left in the cup afterward when he drinks 1 ounce",ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,remaining,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 4, ""incorrect_step"": "" 3"", ""error_description"": ""Forgets to subtract 1 ounce when cold."", ""reference_solution"": ""Omar drinks 1/4 * 12 = 12/4 = 3 ounces on the way to work.\nOmar drinks 1/2 * 12 = 12/2 = 6 ounces when he gets to his office.\nWhen he drinks 1 ounce more, he will have drunk 3 + 6 + 1 = 10 ounces.\nThus, there will be 12 - 10 = 2 ounces left in the cup afterward.\n 2"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi Cody, You said Omar starts with 12 ounces of coffee and drinks 9 ounces before it turns cold.  that leaves 3 ounces correct?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Yes, that is correct."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""Once it is cold and there are 3 ounces left, Omar drinks 1 more ounce of coffee.  How much does coffee is left?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""First, Omar drinks one-quarter of the 12-ounce cup, which is 12/4 = 3 ounces. Then, when he gets to his office, he drinks another half of the cup, which is 12/2 = 6 ounces. After that, he drinks 1 more ounce, making it a total of 3 + 6 + 1 = 10 ounces he has drunk. Subtracting this from the total amount in the cup gives us 12 - 10 = 2 ounces left in the cup afterward."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""Omar drinks 12 x 1/4 = 3 ounces of coffee on the way to work."", ""He drinks another 12 x 1/2 = 6 ounces of coffee at his office."", ""So, he drinks a total of 3 + 6 = 9 ounces of coffee."", ""Thus, there will be 12 - 9 = 3 ounces of coffee left in the cup afterward when he drinks 1 ounce of coffee because it is cold."", "" 3""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:673d59949bd7d40b,full,Extra quantity or Missing quantity,"Five adults and two children go to see a movie and buy $12 worth of concessions. The total cost of their trip is $76. If each child's ticket is $7, how much, in dollars, are the adult tickets?","Let's call the cost of each adult ticket ""a"". We know that there are 5 adults and 2 children, so the total cost of the tickets is:
5a + 2(7) = 76
Simplifying, we get:
5a + 14 = 76
Subtracting 14 from both sides, we get:
5a = 62
Dividing both sides by 5, we get:
a = 12.4
So each adult ticket costs $12.40.
 12.4",Omits an additional cost from the total-cost equation,5a + 2(7) = 76,ok,ok,,8,False,False,False,,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 0, ""incorrect_step"": ""Let's call the cost of each adult ticket \""a\"". We know that there are 5 adults and 2 children, so the total cost of the tickets is:"", ""error_description"": ""They didn't deduct the concession at the start"", ""reference_solution"": ""First take out the cost of the concessions. That leaves $76 - $12 = $64 for the movie tickets\nEach child ticket costs $7, so two children's tickets cost $7 per child ticket * 2 child tickets = $14\nTo find the amount spent on adult tickets, subtract the cost of the children's tickets to get $64 - $14 = $50 for adult tickets.\nDivide $50 for adult tickets / 5 adults = $10 per adult ticket.\n 10"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hello Jordy, can you tell me about your maths today"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Jordy: Today I was working on a problem where five adults and two children went to see a movie and buy $12 worth of concessions. The total cost of their trip was $76. I figured out that each adult ticket cost $12.40, but I wasn't sure if that was correct."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""You did really well but I can see at the start of the problem it mentions that they buy $12 of concessions. Where have you dealt with this in your solution?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""First, I subtracted the cost of concessions ($12) from the total cost of the trip ($76), which left $76 - $12 = $64 for the movie tickets. Then, I calculated the cost of the children's tickets by multiplying the price of each child's ticket ($7) by the number of children (2), which equals $7 * 2 = $14. Next, I subtracted the cost of the children's tickets ($14) from the total movie ticket cost ($64) to find the cost of the adult tickets, which is $64 - $14 = $50. Finally, I divided the cost of the adult tickets ($50) by the number of adults (5) to find that each adult ticket costs $50 / 5 = $10."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""Let's call the cost of each adult ticket \""a\"". We know that there are 5 adults and 2 children, so the total cost of the tickets is:"", ""5a + 2(7) = 76"", ""Simplifying, we get:"", ""5a + 14 = 76"", ""Subtracting 14 from both sides, we get:"", ""5a = 62"", ""Dividing both sides by 5, we get:"", ""a = 12.4"", ""So each adult ticket costs $12.40."", "" 12.4""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:676f5d3726f8aeda,full,Misunderstanding of a question,"William washes cars as a side job. He typically spends 4 minutes washing a car’s windows, 7 minutes washing the car body, 4 minutes cleaning the tires, and 9 minutes waxing the car. This morning he washed 2 normal cars and one big SUV, which took twice as long as a normal car. How many minutes did William spend washing all the vehicles?","For one normal car, William spends 4+7+4+9 = 24 minutes.
So, for two normal cars, he will spend 2 x 24 = 48 minutes.
The big SUV takes twice as long as a normal car, so he will spend 2 x 24 x 2 = 96 minutes washing it.
Therefore, William spends a total of 48+96 = 144 minutes washing all the vehicles.
 144",Double-counts the number of normal cars when calculating the doubled amount.,"The big SUV takes twice as long as a normal car, so he will spend 2 x 24 x 2 = 96 minutes washing it.",ok,ok,,11,False,False,False,"cars, normal",True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 2, ""incorrect_step"": ""The big SUV takes twice as long as a normal car, so he will spend 2 x 24 x 2 = 96 minutes washing it."", ""error_description"": null, ""reference_solution"": ""William spends 4 + 7 + 4 + 9 = 24 minutes washing a normal car.\nHe washed 2 normal cars, so he spent 24 * 2 = 48 minutes on the normal cars.\nHe took twice as long as one car for the SUV, so he took 24 * 2 = 48 minutes on the SUV.\nThus, William spent 48 + 48 = 96 minutes washing all the vehicles.\n 96"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Please explain your answer to me Riya"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""I multiplied the time it takes to wash one normal car (24 minutes) by two, since there were two normal cars. Then, I multiplied the time it takes to wash one normal car (24 minutes) by two again, since the SUV took twice as long as a normal car. Adding the two together gave me my final answer of 144 minutes."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""so you have 2 x 24 for the time it takes to do two cars. hoow long does it take to do the SUV/"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""William spends a total of 24 minutes washing a normal car. Since he washed 2 normal cars, he spent 24 * 2 = 48 minutes on them. For the SUV, which took twice as long as a normal car, he spent 24 * 2 = 48 minutes. Therefore, William spent a total of 48 + 48 = 96 minutes washing all the vehicles."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""For one normal car, William spends 4+7+4+9 = 24 minutes."", ""So, for two normal cars, he will spend 2 x 24 = 48 minutes."", ""The big SUV takes twice as long as a normal car, so he will spend 2 x 24 x 2 = 96 minutes washing it."", ""Therefore, William spends a total of 48+96 = 144 minutes washing all the vehicles."", "" 144""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:686924eb642b8c9a,full,Extra quantity or Missing quantity,"Pirate Rick sailed his ship to a tropical island in search of a site to bury his treasure.  After finding the perfect site, it took him 4 hours to dig up 8 feet of sand under which to bury the treasure.  Once the treasure was buried, he left the island.  Then, a tropical storm came and washed away half of the sand from on top of the treasure.  Next, a giant tsunami wave poured over the island, adding 2 feet of new sand back onto the site of his treasure. When Pirate Rick returned, how long did it take for him to dig up his treasure?","After the storm, there were 8/2 = 4 feet of sand on top of the treasure.
After the tsunami, there were 4+2 = 6 feet of sand on top of the treasure.
To reach the treasure, Pirate Rick had to dig through a total of 6+8 = 14 feet of sand.
Since it took him 4 hours to dig up 8 feet of sand, it will take him (14/8) x 4 = 7 hours to dig up his treasure.
 7",Double-counts the original quantity after updating it,"To reach the treasure, Pirate Rick had to dig through a total of 6+8 = 14 feet of sand.",ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,after,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 2, ""incorrect_step"": ""To reach the treasure, Pirate Rick had to dig through a total of 6+8 = 14 feet of sand."", ""error_description"": ""Added an extra amount on"", ""reference_solution"": ""Pirate Rick digs at a rate of 8 feet of sand per 4 hours, or 8/4=2 feet per hour.\nOf the 8 feet of sand that were initially on top of the treasure, the storm washed 8/2=4 feet of sand away.\nThus, after the storm and the tsunami, the treasure was buried beneath 8-4+2=6 feet of sand.\nDigging at a rate of 2 feet per hour, Pirate Rick could uncover his treasure in 6/2=3 hours.\n 3"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi Claire, can you explain your answer?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Sure. I calculated that it would take Pirate Rick 7 hours to dig up his treasure because he had to dig through a total of 14 feet of sand. I figured this by taking the original 8 feet of sand he had to dig through and adding the 6 feet of sand from the storm and tsunami, which gave me a total of 14 feet. Since it took him 4 hours to dig up 8 feet of sand, I multiplied that by 14/8 to get 7 hours."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""OK, look at the question again and rethink how much sand was on top of the treasure after the storm and the tsunami."", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""Pirate Rick digs at a rate of 8 feet of sand per 4 hours, which means he digs at a rate of 8/4=2 feet per hour. After the storm washed away half of the initial 8 feet of sand, there were 8/2=4 feet of sand left on top of the treasure. Then, the tsunami added 2 feet of new sand, making the total sand covering the treasure 8-4+2=6 feet. Since Pirate Rick digs at a rate of 2 feet per hour, he would uncover his treasure in 6/2=3 hours."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""After the storm, there were 8/2 = 4 feet of sand on top of the treasure."", ""After the tsunami, there were 4+2 = 6 feet of sand on top of the treasure."", ""To reach the treasure, Pirate Rick had to dig through a total of 6+8 = 14 feet of sand."", ""Since it took him 4 hours to dig up 8 feet of sand, it will take him (14/8) x 4 = 7 hours to dig up his treasure."", "" 7""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:68c06edbd379ab6c,full,Extra quantity or Missing quantity,"Mandy’s phone data plan charges $30 per month for data. Her first month, she got a promotional rate of one-third the normal price. However, in the fourth month she went over her data limit and was charged an extra fee of $15. How much did Mandy pay in the first 6 months for data?","Mandy paid 30/3 = 10 dollars for her first month.
For months 2-3, she paid the normal price of $30 per month.
For month 4, she paid $30 + $15 = $45.
For months 5-6, she paid the normal price of $30 per month.
In total, Mandy paid 10 + (3 x 30) + 45 + (2 x 30) = $205 for data in the first 6 months.
 205",Double-counts the normal charge for one month,"In total, Mandy paid 10 + (3 x 30) + 45 + (2 x 30) = $205",ok,ok,,7,False,False,False,normal,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 4, ""incorrect_step"": ""In total, Mandy paid 10 + (3 x 30) + 45 + (2 x 30) = $205 for data in the first 6 months."", ""error_description"": ""The student accidentally added an extra month's price of data."", ""reference_solution"": ""Mandy paid 1/3 * 30 = 30 / 3 = $10 for her first month of data.\nShe paid 30 + 15 = $45 for her fourth month of data.\nMinus those two months, she had 6 - 2 = 4 months of regular charges.\nThus, Mandy paid 4 * 30 + 10 + 45 = 120 + 55 = $175 for the first 6 months for data.\n 175"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi Jordy, can you tell me how you got to your answer?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Sure. I calculated that Mandy paid $10 for her first month because it was a promotional rate of one-third the normal price. Then for months 2-3, she paid the normal price of $30 per month. For month 4, she paid $30 + $15 = $45. For months 5-6, she paid the normal price of $30 per month. So in total, Mandy paid 10 + (3 x 30) + 45 + (2 x 30) = $205 for data in the first 6 months."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""Okay, so Mandy paid $10 for the first month, 30 for the second month, 30 for the third month, 45 for the fourth month, 30 for the 5th month, and 30 for the 6th month. How much is that in total?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""Mandy's normal monthly data plan cost is $30. In her first month, she got a promotional rate of one-third the normal price, which is 30 / 3 = $10. In the fourth month, she went over her data limit and was charged an extra $15. So, for the first month, she paid $10 and for the fourth month, she paid $30 + $15 = $45. Subtracting those two months, she had 6 - 2 = 4 months of regular charges. Therefore, Mandy paid 4 * $30 + $10 + $45 = $120 + $10 + $45 = $175 for the first 6 months for data."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""Mandy paid 30/3 = 10 dollars for her first month."", ""For months 2-3, she paid the normal price of $30 per month."", ""For month 4, she paid $30 + $15 = $45."", ""For months 5-6, she paid the normal price of $30 per month."", ""In total, Mandy paid 10 + (3 x 30) + 45 + (2 x 30) = $205 for data in the first 6 months."", "" 205""]}",,,,
stepwise,stepwise:68f7b8d95db1fc4a,full,Missing / Wrong factual knowledge,"This weekend's football game matched the Seattle Seahawks with the Denver Broncos. The final score was 37-23, with the Seattle Seahawks taking the win. If a touchdown is worth 7 points and a field goal is worth 3 points, how many touchdowns did the Seattle Seahawks score if they scored 3 field goals during the game?","The Seattle Seahawks scored 23 points in total, and they scored 3 field goals, which is 3 x 3 = 9 points from field goals.
To find out how many points they scored from touchdowns, we can subtract the points from field goals from the total points: 23 - 9 = 14.
Since a touchdown is worth 7 points, the Seattle Seahawks scored 14/7 = 2 touchdowns.
 2",Uses the opposing team's score instead of the relevant total,The Seattle Seahawks scored 23 points in total,ok,ok,,10,False,False,False,score,True,1,"{""incorrect_index"": 1, ""incorrect_step"": ""To find out how many points they scored from touchdowns, we can subtract the points from field goals from the total points: 23 - 9 = 14."", ""error_description"": ""The student needed to subtract the points from field goals from the TOTAL Seahawk points, which was 37, not 23"", ""reference_solution"": ""The Seattle Seahawks scored 3 field goals during the length of the game and each field goal is worth 3 points, so 3 * 3=9 points were made by the Seattle Seahawks from field goals.\nThe Seattle Seahawks won so their total points would be 37 with 9 of those points accrued through the scoring of field goals, which makes 37-9=28 points left to be scored by touchdowns.\nThere are 28 points scored by touchdowns and each touchdown is worth 7 points, so this means that 28/7=4 touchdowns were scored by the Seattle Seahawks throughout the game.\n 4"", ""dialog_history"": [{""text"": ""Hi Cody, can you tell me how you got your answer?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}, {""text"": ""Sure, I subtracted the points from the field goals from the total points to find out how many points they scored from touchdowns. Then, since a touchdown is worth 7 points, I divided the points from touchdowns by 7 to get the number of touchdowns they scored."", ""user"": ""Student""}, {""text"": ""What was the total number of points the Seahawks scored?"", ""user"": ""Teacher""}], ""student_correct_response"": ""The Seattle Seahawks scored 3 field goals during the game, and each field goal is worth 3 points. So, 3 field goals * 3 points = 9 points were scored from field goals. Since the Seattle Seahawks won with a total of 37 points, and 9 of those points were from field goals, this leaves 37 - 9 = 28 points to be scored by touchdowns. Since each touchdown is worth 7 points, the number of touchdowns scored by the Seattle Seahawks would be 28 points / 7 points per touchdown = 4 touchdowns."", ""topic"": ""Math Word Problem"", ""student_incorrect_solution_steps"": [""The Seattle Seahawks scored 23 points in total, and they scored 3 field goals, which is 3 x 3 = 9 points from field goals."", ""To find out how many points they scored from touchdowns, we can subtract the points from field goals from the total points: 23 - 9 = 14."", ""Since a touchdown is worth 7 points, the Seattle Seahawks scored 14/7 = 2 touchdowns."", "" 2""]}",,,,
